export default {"basecamp/basecamp-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"brief","kind":"brief","title":"Your first day, for real","md":"This is the boss challenge for Base Camp. There is no new theory here: the challenge pulls together everything from this realm into one realistic scenario.\n\n**The scenario.** You start as a junior developer at *Lumen Studio* on Monday. IT handed you a fresh laptop. Before your 10 am stand-up you need to:\n\n1. Set up a tidy workspace folder.\n2. Configure git with your identity and the team's defaults.\n3. Take a look at the team's starter repository on GitHub and copy it to your machine.\n4. Check its recent history so you can say something sensible about the project at stand-up.\n\nTake a moment to recall the tools before you start:\n\n| Need | Command |\n|---|---|\n| Where am I / what is here | `pwd`, `ls -a` |\n| Make and enter folders | `mkdir -p`, `cd` |\n| Configure git | `git config --global key \"value\"` |\n| Read a GitHub repo | `gh repo view owner/name` |\n| Copy a repo | `git clone <url>` |\n| Read history | `git log --oneline` |\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Boss labs give fewer hints than normal labs. Try each task on your own before you open a hint: retrieving an answer from memory is exactly what makes it stick."},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"What Base Camp gave you","md":"- **Version control** keeps a complete, trustworthy history: what changed, who, when and why. Teams depend on it to work in parallel, review changes, deploy and undo.\n- **The terminal** is where git lives. Ten commands (`pwd`, `ls`, `cd`, `mkdir`, `touch`, `echo >`/`>>`, `cat`, `mv`, `cp`, `rm`) carry you a long way.\n- **Git** is the program on your machine. **GitHub** is a service that hosts git repositories and adds collaboration.\n- **Configure git** once per machine: `user.name`, `user.email`, `init.defaultBranch`, `core.editor`. Global settings live in `~/.gitconfig`; local ones in `.git/config` override them.\n\nNext realm: you create your own repository and make your first commits."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Monday, 9:15 am","md":"Complete the whole setup. The team's repository is `lumen-studio/starter-kit` on GitHub; you are a member of the organisation, so you can read it. Your workspace should be `~/code`."}},"basecamp/configure-git":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The commits signed by \"root\"","md":"Kofi's team noticed something odd in their history: dozens of commits by an author called `root <root@ubuntu-s-1vcpu>`. Nobody knew who that was. It turned out to be Kofi himself: he had been committing from a server where git had never been told his name, so it guessed from the machine.\n\nOn GitHub those commits were not linked to his profile, did not count towards his contribution graph, and when a bug turned up the team had to ask around to find out who wrote the code.\n\nThe fix took fifteen seconds:\n\n```term\n$ git config --global user.name \"Kofi Mensah\"\n$ git config --global user.email \"kofi@example.com\"\n```\n\nEvery commit you make carries a name and an email. Setting them is the very first thing to do on any machine where you use git."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Install, then configure","md":"**Install git**\n\n- **Windows**: download *Git for Windows* from git-scm.com. Accept the defaults, and use *Git Bash* as your terminal.\n- **macOS**: run `git --version` in Terminal; macOS offers to install the developer tools. Or `brew install git`.\n- **Linux**: `sudo apt install git` (Debian/Ubuntu) or `sudo dnf install git` (Fedora).\n\nCheck it worked:\n\n```term\n$ git --version\ngit version 2.47.0\n```\n\n**Configure it once per machine**\n\n```term\n$ git config --global user.name \"Your Name\"\n$ git config --global user.email \"you@example.com\"\n$ git config --global init.defaultBranch main\n$ git config --global core.editor \"code --wait\"\n```\n\n| Setting | Why |\n|---|---|\n| `user.name` | Shown as the author of every commit |\n| `user.email` | Links commits to your GitHub account. Use an email that is on your GitHub profile (or GitHub's private `…@users.noreply.github.com` address). |\n| `init.defaultBranch` | New repositories start on `main` instead of git's old default `master` |\n| `core.editor` | The editor git opens when it needs you to write text, such as a commit message |\n\n**Read your configuration**\n\n```term\n$ git config user.name          # one value\n$ git config --list             # everything\n$ git config --list --show-origin   # everything, plus which file it came from\n```"},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Three levels of settings","md":"Git reads settings from three places, and the most specific one wins:\n\n| Level | Flag | Stored in | Applies to |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| System | `--system` | `/etc/gitconfig` | every user on the machine |\n| Global | `--global` | `~/.gitconfig` | every repository of *your* user |\n| Local | (no flag) | `.git/config` inside a repository | only that repository |\n\nSo you can set your personal email globally, and override it inside your work repositories:\n\n```term\n$ cd ~/work/payroll\n$ git config user.email \"kofi@company.com\"   # local: only this repo\n```\n\nThe settings are plain text files. `~/.gitconfig` looks like this:\n\n```text\n[user]\n\tname = Kofi Mensah\n\temail = kofi@example.com\n[init]\n\tdefaultBranch = main\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Your name badge","md":"Configuring git is like filling in the name badge you wear at a conference. You only do it once at the door, but it is on display in every conversation you have afterwards.\n\nThe global/local levels are like wearing your personal badge everywhere, but swapping to a company badge while you are at the company's stand."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"What teams usually standardise","md":"Teams often add a few more global settings in their onboarding guide:\n\n```term\n$ git config --global pull.rebase true          # keep history linear when pulling\n$ git config --global push.autoSetupRemote true # first push of a branch sets its upstream\n$ git config --global fetch.prune true          # forget branches deleted on the server\n$ git config --global alias.st status           # shortcuts\n```\n\nYou will learn what each of these means later in the path. The point now: **git is configurable, and teams share good defaults.** When you join a team, ask for their recommended git settings."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Configuration gotchas","md":"**Typos in the key.** `git config --global user.mail` (missing `e`) silently creates a useless setting. Check with `git config --list`.\n\n**Forgetting `--global`.** Without it, the setting applies only to the repository you are standing in, and only if you are inside one.\n\n**Using the wrong email.** If the email does not match one on your GitHub account, your commits show up as an unknown grey avatar.\n\n**Quotes.** Names with spaces need quotes: `git config --global user.name \"Ada Lovelace\"`. Without them, git keeps only \"Ada\"."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Introduce yourself to git","md":"You are on a brand-new laptop. Git is installed but knows nothing about you: try `git config --list` and you will see it is almost empty.\n\nSet your identity and sensible defaults, then confirm everything is in place. Use your own name or a made-up one, but make the email look like an email."}},"basecamp/git-vs-github":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"Just put it on Git\"","md":"On her first day at a fintech startup, Ama was told: \"Clone the repo from GitHub, make a branch, and open a PR when you're done.\" She nodded and then searched for \"how to download Git from GitHub.\"\n\nThat search reveals a very common confusion. Git and GitHub are two different things that work together, like **email** and **Gmail**:\n\n- **Email** is a protocol: a way of sending messages that many programs understand.\n- **Gmail** is one company's service built around email, with a website, search, spam filters and more.\n\nLikewise:\n\n- **Git** is a program that runs on *your* computer and tracks the history of a project folder. It is free, open source and works completely offline.\n- **GitHub** is a website (owned by Microsoft) that stores git repositories online and adds collaboration features: pull requests, code review, issues, automation (Actions), and a social network for code.\n\nAma's instructions really meant: *use git on your laptop to copy the project from GitHub, make your changes with git, then use GitHub's website to propose them.*"},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Who does what","md":"```diagram\ngit-vs-github\n```\n\n| | Git | GitHub |\n|---|---|---|\n| What is it? | A command-line program | A website and cloud service |\n| Where does it run? | Your computer | Microsoft's servers |\n| Needs internet? | No | Yes |\n| Made by | Linus Torvalds (2005) and open-source contributors | GitHub Inc., founded 2008, Microsoft since 2018 |\n| Main jobs | Commits, branches, merges, history | Hosting repos, pull requests, reviews, issues, Actions, Pages |\n| Alternatives | Mercurial, SVN (older) | GitLab, Bitbucket, Azure Repos, Gitea |\n\nGit came first. Linus Torvalds wrote it in 2005 to manage the Linux kernel, a project with thousands of contributors. Its design goals were speed, integrity (no silent corruption), and letting everyone work independently with a full copy of the history."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Notebook and library","md":"Git is your **notebook**. You write in it at home, you can flip back through every page, and nobody else needs to be involved.\n\nGitHub is the **library** where you place a copy of your notebook so others can read it, make their own copies, suggest changes on sticky notes, and discuss them. The library also has noticeboards (issues), a review desk (pull requests) and a robot that checks every suggestion (Actions).\n\nYou can keep a notebook without ever going to a library. But to work with a team, you need a shared place, and GitHub is the most popular one."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Talking to each","md":"You will use two command-line tools:\n\n```term\n$ git --version\ngit version 2.47.0\n$ gh --version\ngh version 2.63.0\n```\n\n- `git` does everything local: history, commits, branches, merges. It also moves commits to and from servers (`git clone`, `git push`, `git pull`).\n- `gh` is GitHub's official command-line tool. It does GitHub things without opening the browser: create repositories, open pull requests, view issues.\n\n```term\n$ gh auth status                 # am I logged in to GitHub?\n$ gh repo view octo-org/handbook # read a repository's description and README\n$ git clone https://github.com/octo-org/handbook.git   # copy it to my machine\n```\n\nNotice that the URL in `git clone` is just an address. Git does not care that it is GitHub; the same command works with GitLab or your company's own server."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Where the line falls at work","md":"On a typical team day:\n\n| Task | Tool |\n|---|---|\n| Write code, commit, switch branches | git, on your laptop |\n| Get teammates' latest work | `git pull`, from GitHub |\n| Share your branch | `git push`, to GitHub |\n| Propose merging your branch | GitHub pull request (website or `gh pr create`) |\n| Review a colleague's change | GitHub's review screen |\n| Run tests on every change | GitHub Actions |\n| Track bugs and tasks | GitHub Issues (or Jira, Linear…) |\n\nSome companies use GitLab or Bitbucket instead. Everything you learn about git transfers 100%. The GitHub-specific parts (PRs, Actions) have close equivalents everywhere: GitLab calls a pull request a \"merge request.\""},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Mix-ups to avoid","md":"**\"I'll download git from GitHub.\"** Install git from git-scm.com (or your OS package manager). GitHub is not required to use git.\n\n**\"My code is on Git.\"** People say this, but strictly your code is in a git *repository*, which may *also* be on GitHub.\n\n**\"If GitHub is down, I can't work.\"** You can keep committing locally. You only need GitHub to share.\n\n**\"GitHub Desktop is git.\"** It is a graphical app that runs git commands for you. Helpful, but learn the commands first: every tool, tutorial and error message speaks git."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Meet both tools","md":"Your laptop has git installed and you are logged in to a (simulated) GitHub account named `you`. An organisation called `octo-org` has published its team handbook as a public repository.\n\nUse both tools: ask git and gh who they are, read the repository on GitHub, then make your own local copy with git. Open the **GitHub** panel to see the website side."}},"basecamp/terminal-survival":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Why developers live in a text window","md":"Nadia, a designer moving into front-end development, asked her mentor why everyone at the company typed commands into a black window when there were perfectly good apps with buttons.\n\nHer mentor opened a terminal and typed one line:\n\n```term\n$ git log --author=\"Nadia\" --since=\"1 month ago\" --oneline\n```\n\nIt listed every change Nadia had made in the last month, across a project with 40,000 files, in under a second. \"Find me the button for that,\" he said.\n\nButtons are great for things you do the same way every time. The terminal is better when you want to **say exactly what you mean**: combine options, repeat a command, paste it into a chat so a colleague can run the identical thing, or put it into an automated script. Git was born on the command line, and every git tool with buttons is running these same commands underneath.\n\nYou do not need to become a terminal expert. You need about ten commands. This lesson gives you all of them."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Giving directions in a building","md":"Using a terminal is like moving around a large building while giving instructions out loud.\n\n- You are always **standing in one room**: the *current directory*. `pwd` (\"print working directory\") answers \"where am I?\"\n- `ls` (\"list\") is looking around the room: what files and doors are here?\n- `cd hallway` (\"change directory\") walks through a door into the next room. `cd ..` steps back out to the room you came from. `cd ~` teleports you to your own office: your *home directory*.\n- `mkdir storage` builds a new room. `touch notes.txt` puts an empty sheet of paper on the desk. `rm notes.txt` shreds it, and there is no recycling bin, so be sure.\n\nA **path** is a set of directions. `projects/shop/README.md` means \"through the projects door, then the shop door, then the README.md sheet.\" Directions that start with `/` start from the building's front entrance (the *root*), no matter where you stand."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"The survival kit","md":"Every command follows the same pattern: **name**, then **options** (start with `-`), then **arguments** (what to act on).\n\n```term\n$ ls -a projects\n#  ^   ^  ^ argument: which folder\n#  |   option: -a shows hidden files too\n#  command name\n```\n\n| Command | What it does | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| `pwd` | Show the folder you are in | `pwd` |\n| `ls` | List files; `-a` includes hidden ones (names starting with `.`) | `ls -a` |\n| `cd <dir>` | Move into a folder; `cd ..` goes up, `cd ~` goes home | `cd projects` |\n| `mkdir <dir>` | Make a folder; `-p` makes parent folders too | `mkdir -p code/demo` |\n| `touch <file>` | Create an empty file | `touch notes.txt` |\n| `echo \"text\" > file` | Write text into a file, **replacing** what was there | `echo \"hi\" > a.txt` |\n| `echo \"text\" >> file` | **Append** a line to the end of a file | `echo \"more\" >> a.txt` |\n| `cat <file>` | Print a file | `cat a.txt` |\n| `mv <a> <b>` | Move or rename | `mv a.txt b.txt` |\n| `cp <a> <b>` | Copy | `cp b.txt c.txt` |\n| `rm <file>` | Delete a file; `-r` for folders | `rm c.txt` |\n| `clear` | Clear the screen | `clear` |\n\nTwo keyboard habits save hours:\n\n- Press {kbd:Tab} to auto-complete file and folder names. Type `cd pro` then {kbd:Tab}.\n- Press {kbd:↑} to bring back your previous command, then edit it.\n\nIn this simulator you also have `edit <file>` (aliases `nano`, `code`), which opens a file in an editor panel. In real life you would open the file in VS Code or another editor."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Files, folders and hidden things","md":"Your files form a tree. Here is the home folder you will work in throughout this path:\n\n```term\n/home/dev            ← your home, also written ~\n├── projects/\n│   └── shop/\n│       ├── .git/    ← hidden: git's database (you'll meet it soon)\n│       ├── index.html\n│       └── README.md\n└── notes.txt\n```\n\nA few rules worth burning in:\n\n- Names starting with a dot (`.git`, `.gitignore`, `.env`) are **hidden**. `ls` skips them; `ls -a` shows them. Git uses dot-files a lot.\n- `.` means \"this folder\" and `..` means \"the parent folder\". `git add .` means \"add everything in this folder\".\n- Spaces in names cause pain: `my file.txt` needs quotes (`cat \"my file.txt\"`). Developers prefer `my-file.txt`.\n- **`>` replaces, `>>` appends.** Mixing them up is the most common way beginners wipe out a file."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"The terminal at work","md":"On a real team, the terminal is how you:\n\n- run the project (`npm run dev`, `python manage.py runserver`),\n- run tests before you push (`npm test`),\n- use git and GitHub's `gh` tool,\n- connect to servers (`ssh deploy@server`),\n- follow setup instructions in a README, which are almost always written as commands to paste.\n\nBeing able to read a command and predict what it will do is a core professional skill. When a teammate pastes a command in chat, read it before you run it, especially anything with `rm`, `--force` or `sudo`.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> On Windows, install **Git for Windows**, which includes \"Git Bash\", a terminal where every command in this path works. On macOS use the Terminal app; on Linux, any terminal."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Terminal traps","md":"**Running a command in the wrong folder.** Many surprises come from being one folder off. When in doubt, `pwd` and `ls` first.\n\n**`>` when you meant `>>`.** `echo \"new line\" > notes.txt` deletes everything else in notes.txt.\n\n**`rm` is forever.** There is no undo in the terminal. (Git can save you, but only for files it was tracking. You will see how.)\n\n**Fear of the error message.** Errors are information. Read the first line and the last line; they usually say exactly what went wrong.\n\n**Typing commands from images.** Copying a command with a \"smart quote\" (`“` instead of `\"`) breaks it. Type quotes yourself."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Find your way around","md":"Your home folder already has a little content. Work through the tasks to practise every survival command. The **Files** panel shows what is on disk as you go.\n\nIf you get lost, `pwd` tells you where you are and `cd ~` takes you home."}},"basecamp/why-version-control":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The Friday the website broke","md":"Farhan had been at his first developer job for three weeks. On Friday afternoon he made a \"small change\" to the checkout page of a busy online shop, uploaded it to the server, and went home.\n\nBy 8 pm the shop had taken zero orders. The checkout button did nothing.\n\nHis manager called. \"What did you change?\" Farhan was not sure. He had edited four files that week. He had a folder on his desktop called `shop_backup_tuesday`, but he had kept working after Tuesday, and he could not remember which of his edits came before or after it. Undoing everything would also throw away three days of good work.\n\nThey fixed it at 1 am by comparing files line by line.\n\nOn Monday the team lead sat with him for ten minutes:\n\n```term\n$ git log --oneline -3\n9fba052 Change checkout button handler\n98969a4 Add discount code field\n085ad89 Fix typo on product page\n$ git revert 9fba052\n[main 6546696] Revert \"Change checkout button handler\"\n```\n\nTwo commands. The history showed exactly which change happened when, and the broken change was undone without touching the good ones. That is what version control gives you: **a precise, trustworthy memory of every change**, and the power to move through it.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> You will learn every command above in this path. For now, notice the shape of the fix: find the change, undo only that change."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Save points, but smarter","md":"Think of a long video game. Before the boss fight you **save**. If the fight goes badly, you **load** that save and try again. Without save points, one mistake costs you hours.\n\nVersion control is a save system for your project, with a few superpowers no game has:\n\n- **Every save has a note.** \"Add discount code field\" tells you *why* this save exists.\n- **You can see the difference between any two saves**, line by line.\n- **You can undo one old change** while keeping everything after it, like removing one bad move from the middle of a game.\n- **Several people can play at once** on their own copies and later combine their progress.\n- **Nothing is overwritten.** Saving again never destroys an earlier save.\n\nA second picture: a scientist's lab notebook. Every experiment gets a dated entry: what was done, by whom, and why. Nobody tears pages out. When something surprising happens months later, the notebook explains it. Git is that notebook for files, and it fills itself in with exact detail."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What a version control system records","md":"Without version control, people invent their own system, and it always decays into this:\n\n```diagram\nvcs-chaos\n```\n\nA version control system (VCS) replaces the copies with **one folder and a history**. Each entry in the history (git calls it a **commit**) records:\n\n| Question | What git stores |\n|---|---|\n| What changed? | A full snapshot of every tracked file at that moment |\n| Who changed it? | The author's name and email |\n| When? | A timestamp |\n| Why? | A message written by the author |\n| What came before? | A link to the previous commit |\n\nThat last row matters most. Because each commit points at the one before it, the commits form a **chain**: the history of the project. Everything else you will learn (branches, merges, GitHub, pull requests) is a way of creating, moving along or combining these chains.\n\nGit is a **distributed** VCS: every person's copy of a project holds the *complete* history, not just the latest files. You can browse history and commit on a plane with no internet. Servers like GitHub are just one more copy that everyone agrees to share."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"How real teams depend on it","md":"Version control is not optional in professional software. Nearly every company, from a two-person agency to Google, keeps its code in a VCS, and most use git. Here is what that looks like day to day:\n\n- **Parallel work.** Five developers change the same app at once, each on their own branch, and git combines their work.\n- **Code review.** Before a change goes live, a teammate reads exactly what changed (a \"diff\") and approves it. On GitHub this is a pull request.\n- **Deployments.** The live website is built from a specific commit. If a release breaks, the team redeploys the previous commit in minutes.\n- **Archaeology.** Six months later someone asks \"why does the timeout say 47 seconds?\" `git blame` shows the commit, the author and the message that explains it.\n- **Accountability without blame.** Every change has a name on it, which makes it easy to ask the right person a question.\n\nIt is not only for code. Writers keep books in git, scientists track analysis scripts and data notebooks, designers version design tokens, governments publish laws on GitHub, and system administrators version their server configuration."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Myths that trip up beginners","md":"**\"Git is a backup.\"** Not by itself. A repository on your laptop dies with your laptop. Git becomes a backup when you **push** to another machine, such as GitHub.\n\n**\"Git and GitHub are the same thing.\"** Git is a program on your computer. GitHub is a website that hosts git repositories and adds collaboration features. You will untangle these properly two lessons from now.\n\n**\"Version control is for big teams.\"** A solo developer benefits just as much: undo, history and safe experiments are useful even if nobody else ever sees the project.\n\n**\"I'll commit when it's finished.\"** The value comes from *small, frequent* commits. A history with one giant commit called \"stuff\" is almost as useless as no history.\n\n**\"Git stores only the differences.\"** Conceptually git stores full **snapshots**; it is just very clever about not storing the same content twice. Thinking in snapshots will make later topics much easier.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Version control will feel like extra steps for about a week. After that, working without it feels like writing without an undo key."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Tour a project's memory","md":"You have just joined a small bakery's website team. The project in `~/bakery` already uses git. Before learning any mechanics, see what a history can tell you.\n\nYou will use three commands: `git log` (list the history), `git show <id>` (look at one commit in detail) and `cat` (print a file). Every commit has an id like `a1b2c3d`; you only need the first 7 characters.\n\nThe panel on the right shows the same history as a graph. Watch it as you go."}},"branching/branching-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"brief","kind":"brief","title":"Context-switch chaos","md":"**The scenario.** You work on *Wayfarer*, a travel-booking site. It is Wednesday and three things land on you at once:\n\n1. You are mid-way through `feature/price-alerts`, with uncommitted edits and one brand-new file.\n2. Support reports that the booking confirmation page shows the wrong year. It needs a fix on a branch made from `main`, right now.\n3. Your lead asks you to tidy the repository: delete branches that are already merged, and rename the badly named branch `newstuff` to something meaningful.\n\nWhen you are done, your price-alerts work must be exactly as you left it, on its branch, and nothing anywhere may be lost.\n\n| Need | Tools |\n|---|---|\n| Pause uncommitted work, including new files | `git stash -u`, `git stash pop` |\n| Branch from main | `git switch main`, `git switch -c fix/…` |\n| Inspect branches | `git branch -v`, `--merged`, `--no-merged` |\n| Tidy | `git branch -d`, `git branch -m` |"},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"What Branching gave you","md":"- A branch is a 41-byte pointer to a commit. HEAD points at the current branch. Committing moves only the current branch.\n- `git switch -c <name>` creates and switches; `git switch <name>` moves; switching rewrites your working directory, and git refuses if uncommitted work would be overwritten.\n- `git branch -v`, `--merged`, `--no-merged`, `-m`, `-d`, `-D` manage branches. `-d` protects unmerged work.\n- `git stash` shelves uncommitted work (`-u` for untracked files); `pop` restores it.\n\nNext realm: Merging. Separate lines of work have to come back together."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Wednesday, three things at once","md":""}},"branching/create-switch":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Two tasks at once","md":"Zoe was halfway through building a \"dark mode\" feature when her product manager asked for a quick change: the \"Contact us\" email address on the homepage was wrong.\n\nWithout branches she would have to finish dark mode first, or ship a half-built dark mode with the fix. With branches:\n\n```term\n$ git switch -c dark-mode          # her feature work lives here\n...commits...\n$ git switch main                  # back to the clean, deployable line\n$ git switch -c fix-contact-email\n$ git commit -am \"Fix the contact email address\"\n$ git switch dark-mode             # and back to the feature, untouched\n```\n\nEach piece of work has its own branch. Switching takes a second and her files change to match the branch she is on."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Create and switch","md":"```term\n$ git branch                   # list local branches (* marks the current one)\n$ git branch search            # create a branch here, stay where you are\n$ git switch search            # move to an existing branch\n$ git switch -c search         # create AND switch (most common)\n$ git switch -c hotfix main    # create from a specific starting point\n$ git switch -                 # toggle back to the previous branch\n```\n\nThe classic equivalents still work everywhere:\n\n| Modern | Classic |\n|---|---|\n| `git switch <branch>` | `git checkout <branch>` |\n| `git switch -c <new>` | `git checkout -b <new>` |\n| `git switch --detach <commit>` | `git checkout <commit>` |\n\nBranch names usually describe the work and often use a prefix: `feature/search`, `fix/login-timeout`, `docs/install-guide`, sometimes with a ticket number: `fix/PAY-142-refund-rounding`. Names cannot contain spaces."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What switching does to your files","md":"Switching branches changes three things:\n\n1. **HEAD** now points at the other branch.\n2. The **staging area** is replaced with that branch's snapshot.\n3. The **working directory** is updated: files are added, changed or removed to match.\n\n```replay\ntitle: Files change when you switch\nshow: graph,areas\nsetup: init; commit \"Add homepage\" index.html; git switch -c dark-mode; commit \"Add dark theme\" dark.css\nstep: git switch main | On main, dark.css does not exist, so git removes it from the working directory.\nstep: git switch dark-mode | Back on dark-mode, dark.css reappears. Nothing was lost: it lives in the dark-mode commit.\n```\n\n**Uncommitted changes** are handled carefully:\n\n- If a modified file is the **same** on both branches, your change simply comes along.\n- If switching would **overwrite** your uncommitted change, git refuses:\n\n```term\nerror: Your local changes to the following files would be overwritten by checkout:\n\tindex.html\nPlease commit your changes or stash them before you switch branches.\nAborting\n```\n\nCommit, stash (lesson after next), or discard the change first."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Workbenches in a workshop","md":"A carpenter keeps several workbenches: one for the table she is building, one for a chair repair. Switching branches is walking to the other bench: the tools and half-finished pieces on each bench stay exactly where she left them. The one rule: do not carry a half-glued piece to the other bench and drop it on top of the chair. Git enforces that rule for you."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Branching habits that work","md":"- **Start every task from an up-to-date `main`**: `git switch main`, update it (Remotes realm), then `git switch -c <task>`.\n- **One branch, one purpose.** Mixing two features in one branch makes review and rollback painful.\n- **Short-lived branches** (days, not months) merge easily. Long-lived ones drift and conflict.\n- **Do not work directly on main** if your team uses pull requests. Many teams make it impossible with branch protection."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Switching traps","md":"**Creating a branch from the wrong place.** `git switch -c fix` starts from wherever HEAD is now, even if that is another feature branch. Switch to main first, or pass the start point: `git switch -c fix main`.\n\n**Forgetting which branch you are on** and committing to the wrong one. Check your prompt or `git status`. (The next lesson and Rewriting History show how to move commits.)\n\n**Untracked files follow you.** Untracked files are not on any branch, so they stay in the working directory when you switch, which can be confusing."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Two tasks, two branches","md":"You are building a feature for a recipe website when an urgent fix comes in. Keep both pieces of work on their own branches and switch between them cleanly."}},"branching/manage-branches":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Forty-three branches","md":"When Ines took over a project, `git branch` printed 43 branches. Some were merged months ago, some were abandoned experiments, one was called `test2-final`, and nobody knew which ones still mattered.\n\nShe cleaned it up in ten minutes with three questions git can answer:\n\n```term\n$ git branch --merged main      # which branches are already fully in main? (safe to delete)\n$ git branch --no-merged main   # which still hold unmerged work? (ask the owner)\n$ git branch -v                 # what is the last commit on each?\n```\n\nThirty-one branches were already merged and got deleted with `git branch -d`. Git refused to delete the unmerged ones with `-d`, which protected her from losing work by accident."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"List, rename, delete","md":"```term\n$ git branch                 # list local branches\n$ git branch -v              # with each branch's last commit\n$ git branch -vv             # also show upstream and ahead/behind (Remotes realm)\n$ git branch -a              # include remote-tracking branches\n$ git branch --merged        # branches already merged into the current one\n$ git branch --no-merged     # branches with work not merged into the current one\n$ git branch -m new-name     # rename the current branch\n$ git branch -m old new      # rename another branch\n$ git branch -d feature      # delete a branch that is fully merged\n$ git branch -D feature      # force-delete, merged or not\n$ git branch --show-current  # print just the current branch name\n```\n\n`-d` is the safe delete. If the branch has commits that are not merged anywhere, git refuses:\n\n```term\n$ git branch -d experiment\nerror: the branch 'experiment' is not fully merged.\nhint: If you are sure you want to delete it, run 'git branch -D experiment'\n```\n\nYou cannot delete the branch you are currently on: switch away first."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What \"merged\" means","md":"A branch is **merged into main** when every commit it points to is also reachable from main, in other words, when main's history contains the branch's tip.\n\n```replay\ntitle: Merged and unmerged branches\nshow: graph\nsetup: init; commit \"Base\"; git branch old-fix; git switch -c spike; commit \"Try a new layout\"; git switch main; commit \"Add footer\"\nstep: git branch --merged | old-fix points at \"Base\", which is in main's history, so it counts as merged. spike has a commit main does not have.\nstep: git branch -d old-fix | Safe delete succeeds: no work is lost.\nstep: git branch -d spike | Refused: spike's commit would become unreachable. Use -D only if you really mean it.\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Labels on a whiteboard","md":"Branches are sticky notes on a whiteboard of commits. Renaming rewrites the note. Deleting removes the note. `-d` checks that every commit the note marks is also covered by another note (usually main) before removing it; `-D` peels it off regardless."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Branch hygiene","md":"- **Delete branches after merging.** GitHub can do it automatically when a pull request merges, and offers a \"Delete branch\" button.\n- **Rename `master` to `main`**: `git branch -m master main` (plus a few steps on GitHub, covered later).\n- **Naming conventions** make `git branch` readable: `feature/…`, `fix/…`, `chore/…`, `release/…`.\n- Remote branches are cleaned up separately (`git push origin --delete`, `git fetch --prune`), in the Remotes realm."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Management traps","md":"**`-D` on the wrong branch.** Force-delete skips the safety check. The reflog can recover it, but it is easier not to need that.\n\n**Deleting the current branch.** Git refuses. Switch first.\n\n**Renaming a branch others use.** Renaming is local. Teammates and the remote still have the old name until you push the new name and delete the old one."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Spring-clean the branches","md":"A project has accumulated branches. Find out which ones are merged, delete those safely, keep the unmerged work, and fix a badly named branch."}},"branching/stash":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"Can you look at this right now?\"","md":"Omar was in the middle of a messy change, with half-edited files everywhere, when his lead pinged him: production checkout was broken, could he check `main` immediately?\n\nHis changes were not ready to commit. Switching branches would either carry the mess along or be refused. He typed:\n\n```term\n$ git stash\nSaved working directory and index state WIP on feature/reviews: 3c1d0a2 Add review form\n$ git switch main\n...investigates, fixes, commits...\n$ git switch feature/reviews\n$ git stash pop\n```\n\n`git stash` put his unfinished work on a shelf, gave him a clean working directory, and `git stash pop` put it all back afterwards."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"A stack of shelved changes","md":"```diagram\nstash\n```\n\n`git stash` takes your **uncommitted changes to tracked files** (staged and unstaged), saves them in a special commit, and resets your working directory to match HEAD.\n\n- Stashes form a **stack**: `stash@{0}` is the newest.\n- `pop` applies the newest stash **and removes it** from the stack. `apply` applies but keeps it.\n- Untracked files are **not** stashed unless you add `-u`.\n- A stash can be applied on **any** branch, not just the one it came from.\n\n```replay\ntitle: Stash, switch, come back\nshow: graph,areas\nsetup: init; commit \"Add review form\" reviews.js; echo \"stars: 5\" >> reviews.js\nstep: git stash | The edit is shelved. The working directory is clean, so switching is safe.\nstep: git switch -c hotfix | Do the urgent work on another branch.\nstep: commit \"Fix checkout total\" checkout.js | The hotfix is committed.\nstep: git switch main | Back on the original branch (in a real project, your feature branch).\nstep: git stash pop | The shelved edit returns and the stash is removed.\n```"},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Stash recipes","md":"```term\n$ git stash                       # shelve tracked changes\n$ git stash -u                    # also shelve untracked files\n$ git stash push -m \"half-done reviews\"   # with a description\n$ git stash list                  # see the stack\n$ git stash show -p stash@{1}     # look inside one stash\n$ git stash pop                   # apply the newest and remove it\n$ git stash apply stash@{2}       # apply a specific one, keep it\n$ git stash drop stash@{0}        # delete one\n$ git stash clear                 # delete all (careful)\n$ git stash branch new-branch     # create a branch from where the stash was made and apply it there\n```\n\nIf applying a stash conflicts with changes made since, you resolve the conflict like a merge conflict; the stash is kept so nothing is lost."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The shelf next to the desk","md":"Your desk is the working directory. A visitor needs the desk urgently. You sweep your half-finished papers into a labelled folder and put it on the shelf (`stash`). The desk is clear. When the visitor leaves, you take the top folder off the shelf and spread it out again (`pop`). The shelf can hold several folders, newest on top."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Stash or commit?","md":"Stash is perfect for **minutes or hours**. For anything longer, a commit is safer and easier to find:\n\n```term\n$ git commit -am \"WIP: reviews form (not ready)\"   # on your own branch\n...later...\n$ git reset --soft HEAD~1                           # turn it back into uncommitted changes\n```\n\nStashes are local, easy to forget, and do not show up in `git log` or on GitHub. Many developers prefer \"WIP commits on a feature branch\" for anything that might outlive the day."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Stash traps","md":"**Forgetting untracked files.** A brand-new file stays behind after `git stash`. Use `git stash -u`.\n\n**Stash amnesia.** Weeks later, `git stash list` shows ten mysterious entries. Use `-m` with a description, and pop or drop promptly.\n\n**`git stash clear` by accident.** The stashes become unreachable commits. Recovery is possible but painful.\n\n**Popping on the wrong branch.** It works, which is exactly the problem. Check `git branch` first."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"The urgent interruption","md":"You are halfway through a feature when an urgent fix is needed on main. Shelve your work, fix, and come back without losing anything, including a brand-new file you have not committed yet."}},"branching/what-is-a-branch":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"Isn't branching expensive?\"","md":"Kwame had used an older version control system at a previous job where creating a branch copied the entire project on the server. It took minutes, so people avoided it and everyone worked on one shared line, constantly breaking each other's work.\n\nOn his first day with git, a colleague created three branches in a few seconds while explaining something else. Kwame was suspicious. \"Where did all the copies go?\"\n\n```term\n$ cat .git/refs/heads/feature-login\ne9f1c2a7b0d34f6e8a1c5b2d9f0e7a6c3b1d4e5f\n```\n\nThere are no copies. A git branch is a **tiny file containing one commit id**: 41 bytes. Creating a branch writes that file. That is why git users branch for everything, even five-minute experiments."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"A branch is a movable label","md":"```diagram\nbranch-pointer\n```\n\nThree facts explain almost everything about branches:\n\n1. **A branch points at one commit**: the newest commit on that line of work (its *tip*).\n2. **HEAD points at the current branch.** HEAD answers \"where am I?\"\n3. **When you commit, the current branch moves forward** to the new commit. Other branches stay where they are.\n\n```replay\ntitle: Commit moves only the current branch\nshow: graph\nsetup: init; commit \"Create menu\"; commit \"Add soups\"\nstep: git branch desserts | A new branch is just a second label on the same commit. Nothing else changes; HEAD still points at main.\nstep: commit \"Add salads\" | Committing on main moves main forward. desserts stays behind.\nstep: git switch desserts | Switching moves HEAD to desserts and rewrites your files to match its commit.\nstep: commit \"Add cakes\" | Now desserts moves forward. The history has split into two lines.\n```\n\nThe history is a graph of commits. Branches are just names that make parts of the graph easy to find and extend. \"The main branch\" is not special to git: `main` is simply a branch everyone agreed to treat as the official line."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Bookmarks in a choose-your-own-adventure book","md":"Picture a choose-your-own-adventure book where you write new pages as you read. A branch is a **bookmark**. You can have many bookmarks, each in a different storyline. \"Switching branches\" means opening the book at another bookmark. Writing a new page (committing) moves the bookmark you are holding forward, while the others stay put.\n\nBookmarks are cheap. Losing a bookmark does not destroy the pages, though finding them again without it is harder (that is what the reflog is for)."},{"id":"inside","kind":"inside","title":"Look at the files","md":"In any repository:\n\n```term\n$ cat .git/HEAD\nref: refs/heads/main\n$ ls .git/refs/heads\ndesserts  main\n$ cat .git/refs/heads/desserts\n4f2a91c0d1...\n```\n\n- `.git/HEAD` names the current branch.\n- `.git/refs/heads/<name>` holds each branch's tip.\n- Committing writes a new commit object and then updates the current branch's file.\n\nThat is the entire mechanism. (Big repositories pack refs into one file, `packed-refs`, but the idea is the same.)"},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Why branches matter at work","md":"Every professional team uses branches to keep work separate until it is ready:\n\n- **One branch per feature or fix** (`feature/search`, `fix/login-timeout`): unfinished work never blocks or breaks anyone else.\n- **`main` stays deployable**: only reviewed, tested work is merged in.\n- **Experiments are free**: try an idea on a branch, delete it if it fails.\n- **Reviews are per branch**: a pull request is a request to merge one branch into another."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Branch misconceptions","md":"**\"A branch contains files.\"** A branch points at a commit; the commit contains the snapshot.\n\n**\"Switching branches loses my work.\"** Committed work stays on its branch. Uncommitted changes either move with you or git refuses to switch (more in the next lesson).\n\n**\"Deleting a branch deletes its commits.\"** It deletes the label. Commits that only that branch pointed to become unreachable and are cleaned up much later.\n\n**\"main is the root of everything.\"** It is only a convention. Some projects call it `master`, `trunk` or `develop`."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Pull back the curtain","md":"Explore a small repository's branches at the file level, then create a branch and watch exactly which label moves when you commit."}},"first-commits/commits":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"fixed stuff\"","md":"When Priya joined a payments team, she was asked to find out why refunds sometimes took two days. She opened the history of the refunds module:\n\n```term\n$ git log --oneline -- refunds/\nc41e9a2 fixed stuff\n88b0d1e more changes\n5aa2f03 wip\n1d9e774 update\n```\n\nFour commits, four useless messages. She had to open each one and read every line to understand what happened.\n\nIn the payments service next door, the history read like a story:\n\n```term\n$ git log --oneline -- payouts/\ne8f0c11 Retry failed payouts after 15 minutes instead of 24 hours\n7b2d5a9 Log the bank's error code when a payout is rejected\n40c8e7e Add payout status to the merchant dashboard\n```\n\nShe found the answer there in under a minute. A commit message is a note to your future self and your teammates. It costs ten seconds to write well and saves hours later."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"git commit","md":"```term\n$ git commit -m \"Add packing list for Japan\"     # commit what is staged, with a message\n[main 3f2a9c1] Add packing list for Japan\n 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)\n create mode 100644 japan/packing.md\n```\n\nThe output tells you the branch (`main`), the new commit's short id (`3f2a9c1`), its message, and a summary of what changed.\n\n| Command | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| `git commit -m \"msg\"` | Commit the staging area with a one-line message |\n| `git commit -m \"Title\" -m \"Body\"` | Two `-m` options: a title line, then a longer explanation |\n| `git commit` | Opens your editor to write the message (save and close to commit) |\n| `git commit -am \"msg\"` | Stage every **tracked** modified file, then commit. New files are not included. |\n| `git commit --amend` | Replace the last commit. You will master this in the Time Travel realm. |\n\nIf nothing is staged, git refuses:\n\n```term\n$ git commit -m \"Update\"\nOn branch main\nChanges not staged for commit:\n\tmodified:   index.html\n\nno changes added to commit (use \"git add\" and/or \"git commit -a\")\n```"},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What a commit is","md":"```diagram\ncommit-anatomy\n```\n\nA commit is a small record that points at a full snapshot of the staging area, plus metadata: author, committer, timestamps, the message, and the id of its **parent** commit. Its id is the SHA-1 hash of all of that.\n\nBecause each commit names its parent, commits form a chain going back to the very first commit (the *root commit*, which has no parent):\n\n```replay\ntitle: Commits link to their parents\nshow: graph\nsetup: init\nstep: commit \"Create blog skeleton\" | The root commit has no parent.\nstep: commit \"Add Lisbon post\" | The second commit records the first as its parent.\nstep: commit \"Add Porto post\" | And so on. The label main always points at the newest commit on the branch.\n```\n\nCommits are **immutable**. You can never change a commit; \"editing\" one (amend, rebase) really creates a new commit with a new id and moves the branch label to it."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Entries in a ship's log","md":"A captain's log has one entry per watch: the time, who wrote it, what happened, and it follows the previous entry. Nobody rips pages out. When something goes wrong, the investigators read the log in order.\n\nGood log entries say what happened and why: *\"Changed course to 270° to avoid a storm cell.\"* Bad entries say *\"stuff\"*. A commit message is a log entry for your project."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Writing messages your team will thank you for","md":"The widely used convention:\n\n```text\nShort summary in the imperative, under ~50 characters\n\nOptional body after a blank line, wrapped at ~72 characters.\nExplain WHY the change was needed and anything surprising about\nthe approach. The diff already shows WHAT changed.\n\nFixes #142\n```\n\n- **Imperative mood**: \"Add search box\", not \"Added search box\" or \"Adds search box\". Read it as \"If applied, this commit will *add search box*.\"\n- **One logical change per commit.** If your message needs the word \"and\", consider two commits.\n- **Reference issues** (\"Fixes #142\") so GitHub links them.\n- Many teams also use **Conventional Commits**: `feat: add search box`, `fix(auth): handle expired tokens`, `docs: explain setup`. You will meet them in the Team Workflows realm.\n\n| Weak message | Strong message |\n|---|---|\n| fixed bug | Fix crash when a cart is empty at checkout |\n| changes | Rename \"Buy\" button to \"Add to cart\" |\n| wip | Add draft of the pricing page (not linked yet) |\n| update README | Document how to run the tests locally |"},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Commit pitfalls","md":"**Stuck in the editor.** `git commit` without `-m` opens an editor. If that editor is Vim, you may feel trapped: press {kbd:Esc}, type `:wq` and press {kbd:Enter} to save and quit. Or configure a friendlier editor (`core.editor`). In this simulator the editor is a panel with Save and Cancel buttons.\n\n**`-am` does not add new files.** `git commit -am` only stages files git already tracks.\n\n**Committing secrets.** Passwords and API keys in a commit stay in history forever, even if a later commit deletes them. Check `git status` and `git diff --staged` before committing.\n\n**Giant commits.** A commit that touches 40 files for 5 different reasons is almost impossible to review or revert. Commit small and often."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Commit like a professional","md":"You will make several commits for the travel blog, including one with a title and a body, and one written in the editor. Aim for clear, imperative messages: the lab checks the shape of your messages."}},"first-commits/diff-log":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"What did I just change?\"","md":"Tomás has been editing a configuration file for an hour, trying to make the app connect to a new database. It finally works, but he changed a dozen things along the way and is no longer sure which of them mattered.\n\n```term\n$ git diff config.yml\n-  timeout: 5\n+  timeout: 30\n-  host: db-old.internal\n+  host: db-new.internal\n+  ssl: true\n```\n\nThree changes. He now knows exactly what to commit, and he spotted that the timeout change was only an experiment he should undo.\n\nBeing able to answer \"what changed?\" at any moment, between any two points in history, is one of git's superpowers. This lesson covers the three commands that answer it: `git diff`, `git log` and `git show`."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Which diff compares what","md":"Each `git diff` form compares two of the three areas:\n\n| Command | Compares | Answers |\n|---|---|---|\n| `git diff` | staging area → working directory | What have I changed but not staged? |\n| `git diff --staged` | last commit → staging area | What will my next commit contain? |\n| `git diff HEAD` | last commit → working directory | Everything I changed since the last commit |\n| `git diff A B` | commit A → commit B | What changed between two points in history |\n\nReading a diff:\n\n```term\ndiff --git a/menu.txt b/menu.txt      ← which file\nindex 3b18e51..a5c1f2d 100644          ← blob ids before..after\n--- a/menu.txt                         ← \"before\" side\n+++ b/menu.txt                         ← \"after\" side\n@@ -1,3 +1,3 @@                         ← hunk: lines 1-3 before, lines 1-3 after\n Soup: tomato                           ← unchanged (context)\n-Main: pasta                            ← removed\n+Main: risotto                          ← added\n Dessert: cake\n```\n\nGit shows a modified line as one removal and one addition. Three lines of unchanged context surround each change so you can see where it is."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"log and show","md":"```term\n$ git log                         # full history, newest first\n$ git log --oneline               # one line per commit\n$ git log --oneline --graph --all # history of every branch as a graph\n$ git log -n 3                    # only the last three commits\n$ git log --stat                  # which files each commit touched\n$ git log -p README.md            # every change ever made to one file, with diffs\n$ git log --author=\"Tomás\"        # filter by author\n$ git log --grep=\"timeout\"        # filter by message text\n$ git show                        # the latest commit: metadata + diff\n$ git show 3f2a9c1                # a specific commit\n$ git show HEAD~1:config.yml      # a file exactly as it was one commit ago\n```\n\n`HEAD` means \"the commit you are on now\". `HEAD~1` is its parent, `HEAD~2` the grandparent, and so on. You can use these anywhere git expects a commit."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Track changes, but better","md":"If you have used \"Track changes\" in a word processor, a diff will feel familiar: deletions marked in red, additions in green. The difference is that git can show you the tracked changes between **any two moments** in the project's life, for every file at once, and nobody has to remember to switch tracking on.\n\n`git log` is the table of contents of that history; `git show` opens one chapter."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Diffs at work","md":"- **Before committing**: `git diff --staged` is your last check. Professionals read their own diff before anyone else does.\n- **Code review**: a pull request on GitHub is essentially a diff with comments. Learning to read diffs fluently makes you a better reviewer.\n- **Debugging**: \"it worked yesterday\" becomes `git log --since=yesterday` followed by `git show` on each suspect.\n- **Stand-ups**: `git log --author=me --since=\"1 day ago\" --oneline` reminds you what you did."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Diff confusion","md":"**\"`git diff` shows nothing, but I changed files!\"** Your changes are probably staged. Plain `git diff` only shows unstaged changes; use `git diff --staged`.\n\n**New files do not appear in `git diff`.** Untracked files are not compared at all until you `git add` them; then they appear in `git diff --staged`.\n\n**The pager.** In a real terminal, long output opens in a pager (`less`). Press {kbd:q} to quit, {kbd:Space} for the next page, {kbd:/} to search.\n\n**Reading the wrong direction.** `git diff A B` shows how to get from A to B. Swap them and every `+` becomes `-`."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Investigate the menu","md":"A restaurant's menu project has some history and some unfinished edits. Use `diff`, `log` and `show` to investigate, then commit only after you have checked exactly what you are committing."}},"first-commits/first-commits-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"brief","kind":"brief","title":"Ship version one","md":"**The scenario.** A local football club asked you for a simple website. You have been working in a plain folder, `~/club-site`, without version control (oops). Today you put it under git properly and ship version one.\n\nThe folder contains real source files, but also a downloaded library folder, a log file, a secrets file and some editor junk. The club's coach also asked for one last-minute change.\n\nYou will need everything from this realm:\n\n| Skill | Commands |\n|---|---|\n| Start tracking | `git init` |\n| Keep junk out | `.gitignore`, `git status --ignored` |\n| Build focused commits | `git add <file>`, `git status`, `git diff --staged` |\n| Write good messages | `git commit -m \"Imperative summary\"` |\n| Inspect | `git log --oneline`, `git show` |\n| Fix file mistakes | `git restore`, `git rm --cached`, `git mv` |\n\n> [!TIP]\n> The boss checks the final state of the repository, not the exact commands you type. Plan the commits before you make them."},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"What First Commits gave you","md":"- `git init` turns a folder into a repository by creating `.git`, git's database.\n- Every file is **untracked**, **modified**, **staged** or **unmodified**. `git add` stages; `git commit` records the staging area as a snapshot.\n- A commit stores a full snapshot plus author, time, message and parent. Ids are SHA-1 hashes; commits are immutable.\n- `git diff` (unstaged), `git diff --staged` (staged), `git log`, `git show` let you inspect any change.\n- `.gitignore` keeps generated, personal and secret files out. It only affects untracked files.\n- `git rm`, `git mv`, `git restore` handle deletions, renames and undoing edits.\n\nNext realm: Time Travel. You will move through history and undo almost anything."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"The club website goes live","md":""}},"first-commits/gitignore":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The 400 MB commit","md":"On his first pull request, Diego's diff showed 18,437 changed files. Reviewers' browsers froze. He had committed the `node_modules/` folder (all the downloaded libraries, which anyone can recreate with `npm install`), a `.DS_Store` file from his Mac, the `dist/` build output, and, worst of all, a `.env` file containing the production database password.\n\nThe libraries and build output were just noise. The password was a security incident: it had to be changed immediately, because once it is pushed to GitHub you must assume someone has seen it.\n\nAll of this is prevented by one small text file named `.gitignore`."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What belongs in a repository","md":"**Commit** what people need to rebuild and understand the project:\n\n- source code, tests, documentation,\n- configuration that is the same for everyone (with **no secrets**),\n- the dependency *list* (`package.json`, `requirements.txt`), not the dependencies themselves.\n\n**Ignore** anything that is generated, personal, huge or secret:\n\n| Kind | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| Dependencies | `node_modules/`, `vendor/`, `.venv/` |\n| Build output | `dist/`, `build/`, `*.class`, `*.o` |\n| Logs and temp files | `*.log`, `tmp/`, `*.swp` |\n| OS and editor junk | `.DS_Store`, `Thumbs.db`, `.idea/`, `.vscode/` (sometimes) |\n| Secrets | `.env`, `*.pem`, `credentials.json` |\n\n`.gitignore` only affects **untracked** files. If a file is already committed, adding it to `.gitignore` does nothing until you stop tracking it with `git rm --cached`."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Pattern syntax","md":"`.gitignore` lists one pattern per line:\n\n```text\n# Comments start with a hash\nnode_modules/      # a trailing slash matches folders only\n*.log              # any file ending in .log, in any folder\n/build             # a leading slash anchors to the repository root only\n.env               # an exact name, anywhere\n!keep.log          # \"!\" re-includes something an earlier pattern excluded\ndocs/**/*.pdf      # ** matches any number of folders\n```\n\nThen commit the `.gitignore` itself, so the whole team shares the rules:\n\n```term\n$ echo \"node_modules/\" >> .gitignore\n$ git add .gitignore\n$ git commit -m \"Ignore installed dependencies\"\n```\n\nUseful checks:\n\n```term\n$ git status --ignored          # also list what is being ignored\n$ git rm --cached .env          # stop tracking a file that was committed by mistake (keeps it on disk)\n$ git add -f special.log        # force-add one ignored file (rarely needed)\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> GitHub keeps ready-made templates at github.com/github/gitignore (Node, Python, Java…), and offers to add one when you create a repository."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The bouncer's list","md":"`.gitignore` is the list held by the bouncer at the door of your repository. Anyone on the list is turned away from `git add`, quietly, without an argument. But the list only applies at the door: someone already inside the club (a file already tracked) is not thrown out just because their name is added to the list later. You have to escort them out yourself with `git rm --cached`."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Ignore rules on real projects","md":"- Add `.gitignore` in the **very first commit**, before anyone runs `git add .`.\n- Teams commit an **example** config with fake values (`.env.example`) and ignore the real one (`.env`).\n- Personal junk from your own tools (editor folders, OS files) can go in a **global** ignore file so you do not have to add it to every project: `git config --global core.excludesFile ~/.gitignore_global`.\n- When a secret is committed and pushed, removing it in a new commit is **not enough**. It remains in history. Rotate (change) the secret immediately, then consider rewriting history. The Hero Trials realm covers this."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Ignore gotchas","md":"**Adding a tracked file to `.gitignore` and expecting it to vanish.** It does not. Use `git rm --cached <file>` and commit.\n\n**Ignoring `.gitignore`.** Commit it. It is part of the project.\n\n**`*.log` vs `/logs`.** `*.log` matches files anywhere; `/logs` matches only a top-level `logs` entry; `logs/` matches any folder named logs.\n\n**Negation inside an ignored folder.** `node_modules/` followed by `!node_modules/keep.js` does not work: git does not even look inside an ignored folder. Ignore the folder's contents (`node_modules/*`) if you need exceptions."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Keep the junk out","md":"A web project has collected dependencies, logs, build output, an editor folder and a secrets file. Someone even committed a log file already. Write a `.gitignore` that keeps everything except the source out, fix the file that was tracked by mistake, and commit.\n\nUse `edit .gitignore` to write the file comfortably, or build it line by line with `echo \"pattern\" >> .gitignore`."}},"first-commits/init":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"A folder becomes a repository","md":"Lena runs a small cookbook blog and writes her recipes as text files in a folder called `recipes`. She keeps losing good versions of recipes when she \"improves\" them. A developer friend tells her: \"Run `git init` in that folder.\"\n\n```term\n$ cd recipes\n$ git init\nInitialized empty Git repository in /home/lena/recipes/.git/\n```\n\nNothing visible changed. Her recipe files are exactly where they were. But the folder now has a hidden subfolder, `.git`, and that changes everything: the folder is now a **repository**, and git can record its history.\n\nNothing is tracked yet, though. `git init` only builds the empty filing cabinet. Lena still has to decide which files go into it, and when. That is the next lesson."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What git init actually creates","md":"A repository is two things living in one folder:\n\n- **Your files** (the *working directory*): the recipes, code or documents you edit normally.\n- **The `.git` folder**: git's private database holding every commit, branch and setting.\n\n```term\nrecipes/\n├── .git/          ← the repository's database. Never edit by hand.\n│   ├── HEAD       ← which branch you are on: \"ref: refs/heads/main\"\n│   ├── config     ← settings for this repository only\n│   ├── objects/   ← every version of every file, compressed\n│   ├── refs/      ← branches and tags (tiny files holding commit ids)\n│   └── hooks/     ← optional scripts git can run automatically\n├── pancakes.md\n└── soup.md\n```\n\nDelete `.git` and the folder is an ordinary folder again, with **all history gone**. Copy the whole folder, `.git` included, and you have copied the repository with its full history.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> One project, one repository. Run `git init` in the project's top folder, not in your home folder. A repository in `~` would try to track every file you own."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Installing a flight recorder","md":"Think of `git init` as installing a flight recorder (a \"black box\") in an aircraft. Installing it does not record anything yet and does not change how the plane flies. But from now on, the recorder is there, ready to capture exactly what happens.\n\nThe `.git` folder is the black box. You do not open it during the flight; you use the instruments (git commands) to read and write it."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Creating repositories","md":"```term\n$ mkdir travel-blog && cd travel-blog   # make a project folder and enter it\n$ git init                              # turn it into a repository\nInitialized empty Git repository in /home/dev/travel-blog/.git/\n$ ls -a                                 # .git is hidden; -a shows it\n.  ..  .git\n$ git status                            # ask git what it sees\nOn branch main\n\nNo commits yet\n\nnothing to commit (create/copy files and use \"git add\" to track)\n```\n\nUseful variations:\n\n| Command | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| `git init` | Repository in the current folder |\n| `git init my-app` | Creates `my-app/` and a repository inside it |\n| `git init -b main` | Choose the first branch name for this repository |\n| `git status` | Inside a repo: what changed. Outside one: `fatal: not a git repository` |\n\nRunning `git init` twice is harmless: git replies `Reinitialized existing Git repository` and touches nothing."},{"id":"inside","kind":"inside","title":"Peeking at .git","md":"You never need to edit `.git` by hand, but looking inside once removes the mystery:\n\n```term\n$ cat .git/HEAD\nref: refs/heads/main\n$ ls .git\nconfig  description  HEAD  hooks  info  objects  refs\n```\n\n`HEAD` contains a single line saying \"the current branch is main.\" The branch `main` does not have a file in `refs/heads/` yet, because a branch is just a pointer to a commit and there are no commits. That is also why `git status` says \"No commits yet.\"\n\nIn this simulator you can `ls .git` and `cat .git/HEAD` too. Later, in the Power Tools realm, you will open the objects themselves."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Init mistakes","md":"**Initialising in the wrong folder.** If `git status` lists hundreds of files you never meant to track, you probably ran `git init` in your home folder. Fix: `rm -rf .git` in *that* folder (carefully: check `pwd` first).\n\n**Nesting repositories.** Running `git init` inside a folder that is already part of a repository creates a repository inside a repository. The outer one then sees the inner folder as a strange, untrackable thing. Check with `git status` before running `git init`.\n\n**Expecting init to save files.** After `git init`, nothing is tracked. Your files are not \"in git\" until you add and commit them.\n\n**Deleting `.git` to \"start fresh\".** That deletes the entire history. It is occasionally right, but never do it casually."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Start the travel blog","md":"You are starting a travel blog. Create the project folder, turn it into a repository, and look at what git created. Watch the **Files** and **Three areas** panels: the folder is a repository now, but the repository is still empty."}},"first-commits/rm-mv-restore":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The deleted stylesheet","md":"Ravi was tidying up a project and deleted `old-styles.css`. Ten minutes later the site looked broken: one page still used it. Before git, this meant digging through backups. Ravi typed one command:\n\n```term\n$ git restore old-styles.css\n```\n\nThe file was back, exactly as it was in the last commit.\n\nRemoving, renaming and restoring files are everyday tasks. Git can do all of them, and it helps to know how each one looks from git's point of view."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Deletes and renames are changes too","md":"Git compares the working directory with the staging area and the last commit. So:\n\n- **Deleting** a tracked file is a change (\"deleted: old-styles.css\") that you stage and commit like any other.\n- **Renaming** a file looks like a deletion of the old name plus a new untracked file, until both halves are staged; then git recognises it as a **rename** because the content is the same.\n- **Restoring** copies a version from the staging area or a commit back over your working file.\n\n```replay\ntitle: Renaming a file, two ways\nshow: graph,areas\nsetup: init; commit \"Add notes\" notes.txt\nstep: mv notes.txt journal.txt | A plain mv: git sees notes.txt deleted and journal.txt untracked.\nstep: git add -A | After staging both sides, git recognises a rename (same content, new name).\nstep: git commit -m \"Rename notes to journal\" | Committed. git mv would have done the mv and the staging in one step.\n```"},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"rm, mv, restore","md":"```term\n$ git rm old.css                 # delete the file AND stage the deletion\n$ git rm --cached secrets.txt    # stop tracking, keep the file on disk\n$ git rm -r old-folder/          # remove a whole folder\n$ git mv draft.md post.md        # rename and stage in one step\n$ git restore app.js             # discard unstaged edits: back to the staged version\n$ git restore --staged app.js    # unstage (keep the edit in the working directory)\n$ git restore --source=HEAD~2 app.js   # bring back the version from two commits ago\n$ git restore .                  # discard every unstaged change (careful!)\n```\n\n| You want to… | Command |\n|---|---|\n| Throw away edits you have not staged | `git restore <file>` |\n| Take a file out of the staging area | `git restore --staged <file>` |\n| Undo a `git rm` that is not committed yet | `git restore --staged <file>` then `git restore <file>` |\n| Recover a file deleted in an earlier commit | `git restore --source=<commit-before-deletion> <file>` |\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Older tutorials use `git checkout -- <file>` and `git reset HEAD <file>` for the same jobs. They still work. `git restore` (added in Git 2.23) does one thing and is clearer."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The archive and the draft","md":"Think of the last commit as a signed, archived copy of every document, and your working directory as drafts on your desk. `git restore file` says \"throw away my draft of this document and give me a fresh copy from the archive.\" It cannot fail to find the archived copy, but it **does** destroy your draft, so be sure the draft is worthless."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Restore with care","md":"**`git restore` destroys uncommitted edits.** There is no undo for discarding changes that were never committed or staged. If unsure, `git stash` first (you will meet it in the Branching realm) or copy the file.\n\n**`git rm` vs `rm`.** Plain `rm` deletes the file; you still need to stage the deletion (`git add -A` or `git rm`). `git rm` does both.\n\n**`git rm --cached` is what you want for secrets and junk**, not `git rm`, which would also delete your local copy.\n\n**Renaming case only** (`readme.md` to `README.md`) can confuse case-insensitive file systems (Windows, macOS). Use `git mv` for that."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Spring cleaning","md":"The blog has an outdated file, a badly named file, and a file you are about to break by accident. Use git's file commands, then commit the clean-up."}},"first-commits/three-areas":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Two changes, one commit","md":"Sam is fixing a bug in an app's login page. While in there, he also starts redesigning the footer, but the footer is only half done when the bug fix is ready.\n\nSam wants to save the bug fix **now**, as one clean change the team can review and ship, and keep working on the footer without including it.\n\nMany version control systems would force Sam to save everything at once. Git gives him a middle step: he can **pick** which changes go into the next commit.\n\n```term\n$ git status\nChanges not staged for commit:\n\tmodified:   login.js\n\tmodified:   footer.css\n$ git add login.js\n$ git commit -m \"Fix login when the password contains a space\"\n```\n\nOnly `login.js` went into the commit. `footer.css` is still modified, waiting. This middle step is called the **staging area**, and it is the most important idea in this realm."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Working directory, staging area, repository","md":"```diagram\nthree-areas\n```\n\nGit keeps your project in three places at once:\n\n1. **Working directory**: the actual files on disk. You edit these.\n2. **Staging area** (also called **the index**): a draft of the next commit. `git add` copies a file's current content here.\n3. **Repository**: the permanent history inside `.git`. `git commit` turns the staging area into a new commit.\n\nEvery file you see is in one of these states:\n\n| State | Meaning | Shown by `git status` as |\n|---|---|---|\n| Untracked | Git has never been told about this file | `Untracked files:` (red) |\n| Modified | Tracked, changed since the last commit, not staged | `Changes not staged for commit:` (red) |\n| Staged | The change is in the staging area, ready to commit | `Changes to be committed:` (green) |\n| Unmodified | Same as the last commit | not listed at all |\n\nWatch a file travel through the areas:\n\n```replay\ntitle: One file through the three areas\nshow: graph,areas\nsetup: init\nstep: echo \"# Travel log\" > README.md | A new file appears in the working directory. Git sees it but does not track it: it is untracked.\nstep: git add README.md | git add copies the file's current content into the staging area. It is now staged as a new file.\nstep: git commit -m \"Start the travel log\" | git commit turns the staging area into a permanent snapshot. The repository now has its first commit.\nstep: echo \"Day 1: Lisbon\" >> README.md | Editing the file changes only the working directory. The staging area and repository still hold the old version, so the file is modified.\nstep: git add README.md | Staging again copies the new content into the staging area.\nstep: git commit -m \"Add the first day\" | A second commit. Each commit links to the one before it.\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Packing a parcel","md":"Think of shipping a parcel:\n\n- Your **room** is the working directory: things are scattered everywhere, some finished, some not.\n- The open **box** on the table is the staging area. You choose what to put in it with `git add`.\n- **Sealing and posting** the box is `git commit`. Once posted, the parcel and its label (the commit message) are permanent.\n\nYou can put an item in the box and take it out again before sealing (`git restore --staged`). You can keep adding items over an afternoon. Nothing is shipped until you seal it. And if you change an item after putting it in the box, the box still holds the old version until you put the new one in."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"status and add","md":"```term\n$ git status            # the command you will run more than any other\n$ git status -s         # short form: two columns, staged | unstaged\n$ git add file.txt      # stage one file\n$ git add src/          # stage everything inside a folder\n$ git add .             # stage every change in this folder and below\n$ git add -A            # stage every change in the whole repository, including deletions\n```\n\nThe short format packs a lot into two letters:\n\n```term\n$ git status -s\nA  README.md      ← A in the left column: new file, staged\n M app.js         ← M in the right column: modified, not staged\nMM style.css      ← staged, then modified again\n?? notes.txt      ← untracked\n```\n\nLeft column = staging area compared to the last commit. Right column = working directory compared to the staging area.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Run `git status` before every `git add` and every `git commit`. It costs nothing and it is how professionals avoid committing the wrong thing."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Why teams love the staging area","md":"- **Focused commits.** A reviewer can understand \"Fix login when the password contains a space\" in thirty seconds. A commit called \"fix login + footer redesign + typo\" takes much longer and is harder to undo safely.\n- **Undo precision.** If the footer redesign causes a bug next month, the team can revert that commit without touching the login fix.\n- **Reviewing yourself.** Staging is a natural checkpoint: `git status` and `git diff --staged` show exactly what you are about to share. Many embarrassing commits (passwords, debug prints, huge files) are caught right here."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Staging surprises","md":"**\"I edited the file after `git add`, so the edit is staged.\"** No. `git add` copies the content *at that moment*. Edit again and you must add again. `git status` will show the file in both lists (`MM`).\n\n**`git add .` adds more than you think.** It stages everything under the current folder, including new files you forgot about. Check `git status` first. (A `.gitignore` file, coming soon, keeps junk out.)\n\n**Untracked files are invisible to commits.** A brand-new file is not included in any commit until you `git add` it, even with `git commit -a`.\n\n**Empty folders.** Git tracks files, not folders. An empty folder never shows up. People put a placeholder file like `.gitkeep` in folders they need to exist."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Pack the first parcel","md":"The travel blog has a few files already, but nothing has been committed. Practise moving files between the areas, and watch the **Three areas** panel after every command. You only need `git status`, `git add` and `git commit` for this lab."}},"github/authentication":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"Password authentication was removed\"","md":"Emeka tried to push for the first time from a new computer. Git asked for his GitHub username and password, he typed them, and got:\n\n```term\nremote: Support for password authentication was removed on August 13, 2021.\nfatal: Authentication failed for 'https://github.com/emeka/api/'\n```\n\nGitHub no longer accepts account passwords for git operations. You prove who you are with either a **token** over HTTPS (usually handled for you by `gh auth login` or a credential manager) or an **SSH key**. He set up an SSH key once, and never typed a password for git again."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Two ways to prove who you are","md":"| | HTTPS | SSH |\n|---|---|---|\n| URL | `https://github.com/owner/repo.git` | `git@github.com:owner/repo.git` |\n| Proof | a personal access token (PAT) or OAuth token, stored by a credential helper | a key pair: private key on your machine, public key on GitHub |\n| Setup | `gh auth login` (easiest) or create a PAT and let the credential manager remember it | `ssh-keygen`, then add the `.pub` key to GitHub |\n| Works through strict firewalls | usually yes | sometimes blocked (port 22) |\n\n```diagram\nssh-keys\n```\n\nAn SSH key pair is two files. The **private** key (`id_ed25519`) never leaves your machine. The **public** key (`id_ed25519.pub`) can be shared freely: GitHub uses it to check that whoever connects holds the matching private key."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Setting up both","md":"**HTTPS with the GitHub CLI (easiest)**\n\n```term\n$ gh auth login            # follow the prompts; choose HTTPS; git is configured to use the token\n$ gh auth status\n```\n\n**SSH**\n\n```term\n$ ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C \"you@example.com\"   # create the key pair (press Enter for defaults)\n$ cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub                     # the PUBLIC key: safe to share\n$ gh ssh-key add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub --title \"work laptop\"   # or paste it in GitHub → Settings → SSH keys\n$ ssh -T git@github.com                         # test: \"Hi you! You've successfully authenticated…\"\n$ git remote set-url origin git@github.com:you/api.git   # switch an existing repo to SSH\n```\n\nA passphrase on your key adds protection if the laptop is stolen; an SSH agent remembers it so you type it rarely."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Padlock and key","md":"Handing GitHub your public key is like giving the building manager a padlock that only your key opens. They fit your padlock to your door. Anyone can see the padlock; only you have the key. If your laptop is stolen, you ask the manager to remove that padlock (delete the key on GitHub) and fit a new one."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Security habits","md":"- **Never share or commit private keys or tokens.** A token in a repository is the same as a password in a repository.\n- **One key per machine**, named clearly (\"work laptop\", \"home desktop\"), so you can revoke one without the others.\n- **Fine-grained tokens with expiry dates** for scripts and CI, limited to the repositories they need.\n- **Two-factor authentication** on your GitHub account. Many organisations require it."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Authentication traps","md":"**Using your GitHub password for git over HTTPS.** It will not work. Use `gh auth login` or a token.\n\n**Pasting the private key into GitHub.** Always the `.pub` file.\n\n**\"Permission denied (publickey)\"** means GitHub did not accept any of your SSH keys: the key is not added, the agent is not running, or you are using a different key than you think. `ssh -T git@github.com` is the quickest test.\n\n**403 on push with valid credentials** means you are authenticated but not allowed to write to that repository: ask for access or fork it (Open Source realm)."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Switch to SSH","md":"Your repository uses HTTPS. Set up an SSH key, register it with GitHub, test the connection, and switch the repository to SSH. Try a push before you add the key to see the error first."}},"github/code-review":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The review that saved a launch","md":"Two days before launch, Ji-woo reviewed her colleague Tom's pull request \"Add discount codes\". The code looked clean. She checked it out locally and tried a code twice: the discount applied twice. One review comment, one fix, one re-review, and the bug never reached a customer.\n\nCode review is not about catching people out. It is how teams share knowledge, keep quality consistent, and catch the mistakes everyone makes."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"The review loop","md":"1. The author opens a PR and requests reviewers.\n2. Reviewers read the diff (and often run it), then submit a review:\n   - **Comment**: questions or remarks, no verdict.\n   - **Request changes**: must be addressed before merging.\n   - **Approve**: good to go.\n3. The author pushes fixes to the same branch; the PR updates; reviewers look again.\n4. Once the required approvals and checks are in place, someone merges.\n\nGitHub does not let you approve your own pull request: review is, by definition, a second person."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Reviewing from the terminal","md":"```term\n$ gh pr list                               # PRs waiting\n$ gh pr view 21 --comments                 # description, reviews and comments\n$ gh pr diff 21                            # read the changes\n$ gh pr checkout 21                        # get the branch locally to run it\n$ npm test                                 # actually try it\n$ gh pr review 21 --request-changes --body \"Codes can be applied twice: see cart.js line 14.\"\n$ gh pr review 21 --approve\n$ gh pr review 21 --comment --body \"Nice cleanup!\"\n$ git switch main                          # back to your own work\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A second pilot","md":"Commercial aircraft have two pilots and checklists not because pilots are careless, but because everyone misses things sometimes, and the cost of a miss is high. The co-pilot reads back each step. A code reviewer is the co-pilot for a change."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Review etiquette","md":"**As a reviewer**\n\n- Review the **code, not the person**: \"This loop runs twice\" beats \"You wrote this wrong\".\n- Explain **why**, and suggest an alternative.\n- Separate must-fix from nice-to-have: prefix optional remarks with \"nit:\".\n- Respond within a working day; waiting blocks your teammate.\n- Approve when the change makes the code **better than before**, not perfect.\n\n**As an author**\n\n- Keep PRs small and well described.\n- Reply to every comment (a fix, or a reason).\n- Do not take it personally: the goal is shared."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Review traps","md":"**Rubber-stamping.** \"LGTM\" (looks good to me) without reading the diff gives a false sense of safety.\n\n**Reviewing only the diff.** Some bugs only appear when you run the code or look at what *was not* changed.\n\n**Huge PRs.** Ask the author to split them.\n\n**Endless nitpicks.** Agree on a formatter and linter so reviews focus on behaviour."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Review and be reviewed","md":"A teammate has opened a pull request with a bug. Review it properly: read it, run it, and request changes. Then open your own PR and respond to the review you receive."}},"github/github-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"brief","kind":"brief","title":"One feature, end to end","md":"**The scenario.** You are a developer at *Fieldnotes*, a note-taking app. The repository `fieldnotes/web` is on GitHub, `main` is protected (direct pushes are blocked, merges need one approval), and your teammate Priya reviews quickly.\n\nA user reported that exported notes lose their titles. Take it all the way:\n\n1. File an issue describing the bug.\n2. Clone the repository and fix the bug on a properly named branch.\n3. Publish the branch and open a pull request that will close the issue automatically.\n4. Respond to Priya's review by pushing a follow-up commit.\n5. When the PR is approved, merge it (squash) and delete the branch.\n6. Bring your local `main` up to date and confirm the issue closed.\n\n| Need | Tools |\n|---|---|\n| Issues | `gh issue create`, `gh issue view` |\n| Branch & publish | `git switch -c`, `git push -u` |\n| PR | `gh pr create --title … --body \"Fixes #N\"`, `gh pr view` |\n| Merge | `gh pr merge --squash --delete-branch` |\n| Sync | `git switch main`, `git pull` |"},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"What GitHub gave you","md":"- A GitHub repository adds code browsing, issues, pull requests, Actions and settings around a bare git repository. `gh repo create --source=. --push` publishes a local project.\n- Authenticate with **HTTPS tokens** (`gh auth login`) or **SSH keys** (`ssh-keygen`, `gh ssh-key add`, `ssh -T git@github.com`). Passwords no longer work for git.\n- **Issues** track work; `Fixes #N` closes them when a PR merges.\n- **Pull requests** propose merging a branch; every push updates them; merge with a merge commit, squash, or rebase.\n- **Code review**: comment, request changes, approve. Review the code, not the person."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Fix the export bug through the full GitHub flow","md":""}},"github/github-repos":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"From laptop to portfolio","md":"Rania built a weather dashboard for a course project. It lived only on her laptop, in a git repository with forty commits. A recruiter asked for a link to her code.\n\n```term\n$ cd weather-dashboard\n$ gh repo create weather-dashboard --public --source=. --push\n✓ Created repository rania/weather-dashboard on GitHub\n✓ Added remote https://github.com/rania/weather-dashboard.git\n✓ Pushed commits to https://github.com/rania/weather-dashboard.git\n```\n\nOne command created an empty repository on GitHub, added it as `origin`, and pushed her whole history. The recruiter could now read her code, her README, and every commit message (which is one more reason to write good ones)."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What a GitHub repository is","md":"A GitHub repository is a **bare git repository** on GitHub's servers (history and branches, no working directory) plus everything GitHub builds around it:\n\n| Part | What it gives you |\n|---|---|\n| Code view | Browse files and history at any commit, in the browser |\n| README.md | Rendered on the front page: the project's introduction |\n| Branches & tags | The same refs you push from git |\n| Issues | A task and bug tracker |\n| Pull requests | Proposals to merge one branch into another, with review |\n| Actions | Automation (tests, deploys) on every push |\n| Settings | Visibility, collaborators, branch protection |\n\n**Visibility**: *public* repositories can be read by anyone; *private* ones only by people you invite (collaborators) or your organisation's members. Writing (pushing) always requires permission."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Creating and connecting repositories","md":"There are two directions, and both end with a local repository whose `origin` points at GitHub:\n\n**Start on GitHub, then clone**\n\n```term\n$ gh repo create travel-blog --public --clone   # create it and clone it\n$ cd travel-blog\n```\n\n**Start locally, then publish** (the common case)\n\n```term\n$ cd travel-blog\n$ gh repo create travel-blog --public --source=. --push\n```\n\nOr by hand, using the website's \"New repository\" button and then:\n\n```term\n$ git remote add origin https://github.com/you/travel-blog.git\n$ git push -u origin main\n```\n\nOther everyday commands:\n\n```term\n$ gh repo view                 # description and README of the current repo\n$ gh repo view octo-org/handbook\n$ gh repo list                 # your repositories\n$ gh browse                    # open the current repo in the browser\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Renting a shop front","md":"Your local repository is your workshop. Creating a GitHub repository is renting a shop front on a busy street: you put your work in the window (the README and code view), customers can walk in and leave notes (issues), other craftspeople can propose improvements (pull requests), and you can hire a robot assistant that checks every delivery (Actions). The workshop stays yours; the shop is where collaboration happens."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Good repository hygiene","md":"- **A README** that answers: what is this, how do I run it, how do I contribute.\n- **A LICENSE** for public code (otherwise, legally, nobody may reuse it).\n- **A `.gitignore`** from the start (GitHub offers templates when you create a repo).\n- **A description and topics** so people can find it.\n- **The default branch** is `main`; branch protection rules keep it safe (Team Workflows realm)."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Repository traps","md":"**Creating the GitHub repo with a README, then pushing a local repo.** Both histories have a first commit, so they are unrelated and the push is rejected. Either create the GitHub repo empty, or clone it first.\n\n**Publishing secrets.** Check `git log -p` for passwords before making a repository public. Once public, assume anything in history has been copied.\n\n**Private does not mean secure storage for secrets.** Still never commit them."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Publish your project","md":"You have a local project with a few commits and no remote. Publish it to GitHub with the CLI, check the remote, and look at it the way visitors will."}},"github/issues":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The bug report in a chat thread","md":"At a small agency, bugs were reported in a group chat. \"The contact form is broken on mobile\" scrolled away under lunch plans within an hour. Two weeks later a client asked why it was still broken. Nobody remembered who had looked at it, or whether anyone had.\n\nThey moved to GitHub Issues. Every bug and task got a number, a description, labels, an assignee, and a status. When a pull request said `Fixes #42`, the issue closed itself the moment the fix merged, with a link to the exact change."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What an issue is","md":"An **issue** is a numbered discussion thread attached to a repository, used for bugs, feature requests, tasks and questions.\n\n| Field | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| Title | One-line summary, e.g. \"Contact form does not submit on iOS Safari\" |\n| Body | Details: steps to reproduce, expected vs actual behaviour, screenshots |\n| Labels | Categories: `bug`, `enhancement`, `good first issue`, `priority: high` |\n| Assignees | Who is working on it |\n| Milestone / Project | Which release or board it belongs to |\n| State | Open or closed |\n\nIssues and pull requests **share one numbering sequence** per repository: if issue #41 exists, the next PR is #42.\n\n**Closing keywords** link work to issues. In a PR description (or a commit message on the default branch), `Fixes #42`, `Closes #42` or `Resolves #42` closes issue 42 automatically when the PR merges."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Issues from the terminal","md":"```term\n$ gh issue create --title \"Contact form fails on iOS\" --body \"Steps: …\" --label bug\n$ gh issue list                     # open issues\n$ gh issue list --label bug         # filtered\n$ gh issue view 42                  # read one\n$ gh issue comment 42 --body \"I can reproduce this on iOS 18.\"\n$ gh issue close 42                 # close manually (e.g. \"won't fix\")\n$ gh issue reopen 42\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The ticket rail in a restaurant kitchen","md":"In a busy kitchen, every order is a ticket on the rail: numbered, visible to everyone, with clear details. Cooks take a ticket, work on it, and spike it when the dish goes out. Nobody shouts orders across the room and hopes someone remembers. Issues are the ticket rail for software."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Writing issues people can act on","md":"A good bug report contains:\n\n```text\n**What happened:** The contact form shows a spinner forever on iPhone.\n**Expected:** A \"Thanks, we'll be in touch\" message.\n**Steps to reproduce:**\n1. Open /contact on iOS Safari 18\n2. Fill in all fields, press Send\n**Notes:** Works on desktop Chrome. Console shows a CORS error.\n```\n\n- One problem per issue.\n- Search before filing: duplicates waste everyone's time.\n- Use **labels** consistently, and `good first issue` to welcome newcomers (Open Source realm).\n- Many teams use **issue templates** (`.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/`) so reports arrive in this shape."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Issue traps","md":"**Vague titles** like \"Bug\" or \"Doesn't work\".\n\n**Closing keywords in the wrong place.** They act when the PR merges into the **default branch**. \"Fixes #42\" in a PR into a release branch will not close it automatically.\n\n**Using issues as a chat.** Long debates belong in a discussion or a call; summarise the decision in the issue."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Track the work","md":"The team's website has a couple of open issues. Report a new bug properly, comment on an existing one, and close one that turned out not to be a bug."}},"github/pull-requests":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"Can you just merge it?\"","md":"When Hugo joined a team that used pull requests, he found it slow at first: why open a PR, wait for review and checks, then merge, when he could just push to `main`?\n\nThree months later he had changed his mind. PRs had caught two bugs he would have shipped, taught him half the codebase through review comments, and twice let the team revert a whole feature with one click. Most importantly, nobody ever broke `main` without noticing, because nothing reached `main` without a second pair of eyes and a green test run."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"A pull request is a proposal to merge a branch","md":"```diagram\npr-lifecycle\n```\n\nA **pull request (PR)** asks: *please merge branch `head` into branch `base`* (usually `feature/x` into `main`). It shows the combined diff, the commits, review comments, and automated check results. While it is open, **every push to the head branch updates the PR**.\n\nWhen it is approved and checks pass, it can be merged in one of three ways:\n\n| Button | What lands on main | History |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Create a merge commit** | all the branch's commits plus a merge commit | full detail, non-linear |\n| **Squash and merge** | one new commit containing the whole PR | linear, one commit per PR |\n| **Rebase and merge** | the branch's commits replayed onto main | linear, keeps each commit |\n\nAfter merging, the branch is usually deleted, and everyone **pulls main** to get the result."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"PRs with the GitHub CLI","md":"```term\n$ git switch -c feature/dark-mode\n$ git commit -am \"Add dark mode toggle\"\n$ git push -u origin feature/dark-mode\n$ gh pr create --title \"Add dark mode\" --body \"Adds a toggle in settings. Fixes #12\"\n$ gh pr list                     # open PRs\n$ gh pr view 15                  # details, reviews, checks\n$ gh pr diff 15                  # the combined diff\n$ gh pr checks 15                # CI status\n$ gh pr merge 15 --squash --delete-branch    # merge it (once allowed)\n$ git switch main && git pull    # get the merged result locally\n```\n\n`gh pr create --fill` uses your commit messages for the title and body."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Submitting an article to an editor","md":"You do not publish straight to the front page of a newspaper. You submit your article (open a PR), an editor reads it and leaves notes (review), you revise and resubmit (push more commits, the PR updates), the fact-checkers run their checks (CI), and the editor publishes it (merge). The newspaper (main) stays trustworthy."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"PRs that get merged quickly","md":"- **Small**: under ~400 changed lines when possible. Big PRs get skimmed, small ones get reviewed.\n- **One purpose**: a PR that fixes a bug and refactors a module is two PRs.\n- **A clear description**: what and why, how to test, screenshots for UI, and `Fixes #N`.\n- **Draft PRs** (`gh pr create --draft`) to share early work for feedback.\n- **Keep it current**: if main moves, rebase your branch (`git rebase origin/main` + `--force-with-lease`) or merge main into it, depending on team policy."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"PR traps","md":"**Opening a PR from main.** Always work on a branch; a PR from main to main has nothing to compare.\n\n**Forgetting to push new commits.** Commits that are only local do not appear in the PR.\n\n**Not pulling after merge.** Your local main is stale until you pull. Start the next branch from an up-to-date main.\n\n**Squash-merge surprise.** After a squash merge, your feature branch's commits are not in main (main has one new commit instead), so `git branch -d` may refuse. That is expected: use `-D` once you confirmed the PR merged."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Your first pull request","md":"Fix an issue on a branch, open a pull request that closes it, merge it with a squash, and bring your local main up to date."}},"merging/conflicts":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Two people, one line","md":"At a small restaurant chain, Chen changed the lunch price on the menu page to $14 on a branch for a promotion. Meanwhile Amara changed the same line to $16 on `main` because ingredient costs had risen. When Chen merged:\n\n```term\n$ git merge promo-lunch\nAuto-merging menu.html\nCONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in menu.html\nAutomatic merge failed; fix conflicts and then commit the result.\n```\n\nGit did exactly the right thing. Both people changed the **same line** in **different** ways. Choosing automatically would mean silently throwing away one person's decision. So git stopped and asked a human.\n\nA conflict is not an error and nothing is broken. It is git saying: *\"I need you to decide.\"*"},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"When conflicts happen","md":"A conflict happens only when, compared with the common ancestor (the base), **both** sides changed the **same lines** (or adjacent lines) differently, or one side edited a file the other side deleted.\n\n| Ours | Theirs | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| changed line 5 | did not touch line 5 | ours wins automatically |\n| did not touch line 5 | changed line 5 | theirs wins automatically |\n| changed line 5 to X | changed line 5 to X | identical: no conflict |\n| changed line 5 to X | changed line 5 to Y | **conflict** |\n| edited file | deleted file | **conflict** (modify/delete) |\n\n```replay\ntitle: How a conflict arises\nshow: graph\nsetup: init; commit \"Add menu\" menu.txt; git switch -c promo-lunch; sed -i \"s/Add menu/Lunch: 14 dollars/\" menu.txt; git commit -qam \"Lunch promo price\"; git switch main; sed -i \"s/Add menu/Lunch: 16 dollars/\" menu.txt; git commit -qam \"Raise lunch price\"\nstep: git merge promo-lunch | Both branches rewrote the same line differently. Git stops mid-merge and marks the file as conflicted.\nstep: git status | status lists the file under \"Unmerged paths\" and explains how to finish or abort.\n```"},{"id":"inside","kind":"inside","title":"Anatomy of conflict markers","md":"Git writes both versions into the file, fenced by markers:\n\n```diagram\nconflict-anatomy\n```\n\n- Between `<<<<<<< HEAD` and `=======` is **your** side (the branch you are on).\n- Between `=======` and `>>>>>>> branch-name` is **their** side (the branch being merged).\n- Everything outside the markers merged cleanly.\n\nWhile the merge is unfinished, the repository is in a special state: `git status` says *You have unmerged paths*, the prompt shows `MERGING`, and `git commit` refuses until every conflict is resolved."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Inspecting a conflict","md":"```term\n$ git status                  # which files are conflicted (\"both modified\")\n$ git diff                    # the conflicting hunks\n$ git log --merge --oneline   # the commits on both sides that touched the conflicted files\n$ git merge --abort           # give up: back to exactly how things were before the merge\n```\n\n`git merge --abort` is always safe while a merge is in progress. Use it whenever you want to stop and think, talk to the other person, or start over."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Two editors, one sentence","md":"Two editors both rewrote the same sentence of an article differently. A good copy chief does not pick one at random: she puts both versions side by side and asks someone who understands the story to decide. Conflict markers are the side-by-side view."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Conflicts are a people problem","md":"- The best resolution often needs a **conversation**: \"You set $14 for the promo, I set $16 for costs. Which wins?\"\n- Conflicts get bigger the longer branches live apart. Merge (or rebase) `main` into long branches regularly, and keep branches short.\n- Formatting changes (re-indenting a whole file) cause huge conflicts. Teams use automatic formatters so everyone's code looks the same.\n- Lock files (`package-lock.json`) conflict often; teams usually regenerate them instead of hand-editing."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Conflict panics","md":"**\"I broke the repository.\"** You did not. `git merge --abort` returns you to before the merge.\n\n**Committing the markers.** Git will happily commit a file still containing `<<<<<<<`. Always search for markers before committing.\n\n**Assuming a conflict-free merge is a correct merge.** Git merges text, not meaning. If one branch renamed a function and the other added a call to the old name, the merge succeeds and the code breaks. Run the tests after merging."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Meet your first conflict","md":"Two branches changed the same line of a menu. Trigger the conflict, read the markers, inspect it, then abort cleanly. Resolving is the next lesson."}},"merging/fast-forward":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The merge that wasn't","md":"Felipe finished his `feature/newsletter` branch, switched to `main` and ran `git merge feature/newsletter`, bracing for something dramatic. Git printed:\n\n```term\nUpdating 3c1d0a2..9e8f7b6\nFast-forward\n newsletter.html | 12 ++++++++++++\n 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)\n```\n\nNo new commit, no questions. Nobody had touched `main` since he branched off, so there was nothing to *combine*: git simply moved the `main` label forward to his newest commit. That is a **fast-forward**."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"When main has not moved","md":"```replay\ntitle: A fast-forward merge\nshow: graph\nsetup: init; commit \"Create site\"; git switch -c feature/newsletter; commit \"Add signup form\"; commit \"Add thank-you page\"; git switch main\nstep: git merge feature/newsletter | main had no commits of its own since the branch started, so git just slides main forward. History stays a straight line.\nstep: git branch -d feature/newsletter | The feature branch is fully merged, so it can be deleted safely.\n```\n\nA fast-forward is possible when the branch you are merging **contains** the current branch's tip, i.e. the current branch is an ancestor of the other one. Git then has nothing to decide: every commit already exists and is already in the right order.\n\n```diagram\nmerge-types\n```"},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Merging","md":"The pattern is always: **switch to the branch that should receive the work, then merge the other branch into it.**\n\n```term\n$ git switch main\n$ git merge feature/newsletter      # bring feature's commits into main\n$ git merge --ff-only feature/x     # merge only if a fast-forward is possible, otherwise refuse\n$ git merge --no-ff feature/x       # always create a merge commit, even if ff is possible\n$ git branch -d feature/newsletter  # tidy up afterwards\n```\n\n`git merge` reads as \"merge *that* into *here*\". Merging the wrong way round (main into your feature branch) is also a legitimate operation, used to bring a feature branch up to date, but it is not the same thing."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Catching up in a relay","md":"In a relay race, if the second runner never started running (main had no new commits), \"merging\" the first runner's progress is trivial: the second runner simply teleports to where the first one stopped. No negotiation needed. That is a fast-forward."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Fast-forward on teams","md":"- `--ff-only` is a popular safety setting for pulling (`git config --global pull.ff only`): it refuses to create surprise merge commits.\n- Some teams prefer `--no-ff` for feature branches so that history shows a merge commit marking \"the newsletter feature landed here\", which is easy to revert as a unit.\n- GitHub's \"Rebase and merge\" and \"Squash and merge\" buttons also produce linear history (Collaboration realm)."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Fast-forward traps","md":"**Merging from the wrong branch.** `git merge main` while on `feature` brings main into feature. Check `git branch` before merging.\n\n**Expecting a merge commit.** With a fast-forward there is none; `git log` shows just the feature commits on main.\n\n**Deleting the branch before merging.** `-d` protects you; `-D` does not."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Land a finished feature","md":"A finished feature branch is waiting. Merge it into main as a fast-forward, confirm nothing new was created, and clean up."}},"merging/merging-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"brief","kind":"brief","title":"Release-day merge train","md":"**The scenario.** It is release day at *Brightside*, a small company building a fitness app. Three feature branches must land in `main` before the release:\n\n- `feature/streaks`: no conflicts expected.\n- `feature/dark-theme`: touches `settings.json`, which `main` also changed.\n- `feature/workout-timer`: changes the same line of `app.js` as `feature/dark-theme`.\n\nYour job: merge all three into `main`, resolve every conflict sensibly, keep the history honest (merge commits where branches diverged), and leave `main` clean, with every feature present and no conflict markers anywhere. Then delete the merged branches.\n\nThink about order: merging one branch changes what the next merge sees.\n\n| Need | Tools |\n|---|---|\n| Merge | `git merge <branch>`, `--no-edit` |\n| Inspect | `git status`, `git diff`, `git log --oneline --graph` |\n| Resolve | `edit`, `git checkout --ours/--theirs`, `git add`, `git commit` |\n| Escape hatch | `git merge --abort` |\n| Verify | `git grep -n \"<<<<<<<\"`, `git branch --merged` |"},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"What Merging gave you","md":"- **Fast-forward**: when the receiving branch has not moved, git just slides the label.\n- **Three-way merge**: base + ours + theirs, recorded in a merge commit with two parents.\n- **Conflicts** happen when both sides change the same lines differently (or edit vs delete). Git marks them with `<<<<<<<`, `=======`, `>>>>>>>`.\n- **Resolve** by editing (or `--ours`/`--theirs`), then `git add`, then `git commit`. `git merge --abort` is always available.\n- A clean text merge is not proof of correct code: test after merging."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Ship all three features","md":""}},"merging/resolving":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The conflict that needed both","md":"The conflict in Chen's menu was simple: one line, one decision. Two weeks later a harder one arrived: three files, one of them deleted on the other branch. Chen worked through it methodically:\n\n```term\n$ git status\nUnmerged paths:\n\tboth modified:   menu.html\n\tboth modified:   prices.json\n\tdeleted by them: specials.html\n```\n\nFor `menu.html` she combined both sides by hand. For `prices.json` she took the other branch's version entirely. For `specials.html` she agreed with the deletion. After each decision, `git add` (or `git rm`) marked the file resolved, and a final `git commit` concluded the merge."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"The resolution workflow","md":"```term\n$ git status                         # 1. list conflicted files\n$ edit menu.html                     # 2a. edit: keep what you want, delete ALL markers\n$ git checkout --theirs prices.json  # 2b. or take one side's whole file\n$ git checkout --ours config.yml     #     (--ours = the branch you are on)\n$ git rm specials.html               # 2c. or accept a deletion\n$ git add menu.html prices.json      # 3. mark resolved\n$ git status                         # 4. \"All conflicts fixed but you are still merging\"\n$ git commit --no-edit               # 5. conclude the merge (or: git merge --continue)\n```\n\nResolving means making the file contain **exactly what it should contain after the merge**. That might be your side, their side, both, or something new that combines the ideas."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What \"resolved\" means to git","md":"During a conflicted merge, git keeps up to three versions of each conflicted file in the staging area (base, ours, theirs). `git add <file>` replaces them with the single version from your working directory, which tells git *\"this is the answer.\"* Git does not check that you removed the markers; that part is your job.\n\n```replay\ntitle: Resolve, add, commit\nshow: graph,areas\nsetup: init; commit \"Add menu\" menu.txt; git switch -c promo-lunch; sed -i \"s/Add menu/Lunch: 14 dollars/\" menu.txt; git commit -qam \"Lunch promo price\"; git switch main; sed -i \"s/Add menu/Lunch: 16 dollars/\" menu.txt; git commit -qam \"Raise lunch price\"; git merge promo-lunch\nstep: echo \"Lunch: 16 dollars (14 on Mondays)\" > menu.txt | Write the version you actually want, with no markers.\nstep: git add menu.txt | Adding marks the conflict as resolved.\nstep: git commit --no-edit | The merge commit is recorded with both parents.\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The mediator's final draft","md":"Resolving a conflict is writing the final draft after a mediation. You are not choosing \"the winner\" by default; you are writing the version both parties would sign. Sometimes that is one side's wording, sometimes both, sometimes a new sentence. Once written, you hand it in (`git add`) and the agreement is signed (`git commit`)."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Resolving well","md":"- **Understand both changes before editing.** `git log --merge -p` shows the commits involved.\n- **Ask the other author** when intent is unclear. A two-minute chat beats a wrong guess.\n- **Build and test** after resolving: a clean text merge can still be broken code.\n- **Use a merge tool** if you like visual diffs: VS Code shows \"Accept Current / Incoming / Both\" buttons above each conflict; `git mergetool` launches configured tools.\n- **`--ours` and `--theirs`** are perfect for generated files, but dangerous for code you have not read."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Resolution traps","md":"**Leaving a marker behind.** Search before committing: `git grep -n \"<<<<<<<\"`.\n\n**`--ours`/`--theirs` swap meaning during rebase.** In a rebase, \"ours\" is the branch you are rebasing onto. You will meet this in the Rewriting History realm.\n\n**Resolving by deleting both sides.** It removes the conflict and the content. Make sure the result is what you meant.\n\n**Forgetting to commit.** Until you commit, the merge is unfinished; the prompt still says MERGING."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Resolve a three-file conflict","md":"A merge conflicts in three different ways. Resolve each one appropriately, finish the merge, and verify that no markers remain."}},"merging/three-way":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Both sides moved","md":"While Ana built `feature/reviews`, her teammates merged two other changes into `main`. When she merged, git could not just slide a label: both branches had new commits since they split.\n\n```term\n$ git switch main\n$ git merge feature/reviews\nMerge made by the 'ort' strategy.\n reviews.js | 20 ++++++++++++++++++++\n 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+)\n```\n\nGit found the **common ancestor** (the commit where the branches split), worked out what each side changed since then, combined both sets of changes, and recorded the result in a new **merge commit** with two parents."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Base, ours, theirs","md":"A three-way merge compares three snapshots:\n\n| Name | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| **base** | the common ancestor, where the branches split |\n| **ours** | the tip of the branch you are on (HEAD) |\n| **theirs** | the tip of the branch you are merging in |\n\nFor each file and each region of lines:\n\n- changed only in ours → keep ours,\n- changed only in theirs → take theirs,\n- changed in both, identically → keep it,\n- changed in both, differently → **conflict** (next lesson).\n\n```replay\ntitle: A three-way merge creates a merge commit\nshow: graph\nsetup: init; commit \"Create shop\"; git switch -c feature/reviews; commit \"Add review form\"; commit \"Add star ratings\"; git switch main; commit \"Add footer\"; commit \"Fix header link\"\nstep: git merge --no-edit feature/reviews | Both branches have new commits, so git combines them and records a merge commit with two parents.\nstep: git log --oneline --graph | The graph shows the two lines of work joining.\n```\n\nThe merge commit's first parent is the branch you were on (main); the second parent is the branch you merged. That ordering is what `HEAD^1` vs `HEAD^2` refer to."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Merge options","md":"```term\n$ git merge feature/reviews              # merge; opens the editor for the message if a merge commit is needed\n$ git merge --no-edit feature/reviews    # accept the default message (\"Merge branch 'feature/reviews'\")\n$ git merge --no-ff feature/reviews      # force a merge commit even when ff is possible\n$ git merge -m \"Merge reviews feature\" feature/reviews\n$ git log --oneline --graph              # see the shape of history\n$ git log --first-parent --oneline       # only main's own line (one entry per merged feature)\n$ git revert -m 1 <merge-commit>         # undo a whole merged feature (keep parent 1)\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Merging two edits of a document","md":"Two editors each received a copy of the same chapter (the base). One rewrote the introduction; the other fixed typos in the conclusion. A sensible publisher compares both copies with the original: different parts changed, so both edits can go into the final version. Only if both editors rewrote the **same** paragraph differently does the publisher need to ask someone to decide."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Merge commits in practice","md":"- On GitHub, the default \"Create a merge commit\" button produces exactly this kind of commit for a pull request.\n- `--no-ff` merges keep each feature grouped: `git log --first-parent` reads like a list of features.\n- A bad feature can be undone as one unit: `git revert -m 1 <merge>`.\n- Some teams dislike merge commits (\"railroad tracks\") and prefer rebasing or squashing; you will compare the approaches in the Rewriting History realm."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Three-way traps","md":"**Merging with uncommitted changes.** Git refuses if the merge would touch files you have modified. Commit or stash first.\n\n**Forgetting `--no-edit`** opens the editor. In a real terminal that may be Vim; save and quit to accept the default message.\n\n**Thinking a merge copies commits.** It does not. The feature's commits stay exactly as they were; the merge commit just joins the two histories."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Both sides moved","md":"Main and a feature branch have both moved on. Merge them, read the graph, and explore the two parents of the merge commit."}},"open-source/contributing":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"A first contribution, start to finish","md":"Amani wanted her first open-source contribution to be small. The project `bookworm/reader` had an issue labelled `good first issue`: \"README install command is outdated\". She read `CONTRIBUTING.md`, forked, fixed, and opened a pull request that said \"Fixes #12\". A maintainer replied within a day: \"Thanks! One tiny thing: please use `npm ci` instead of `npm install`.\" She pushed one more commit and the PR was merged. Her GitHub profile now listed her as a contributor."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"The contribution flow","md":"```term\nupstream  ──fork──▶  origin (your fork)  ──clone──▶  local\n                                           ◀──push── (branch)\nupstream  ◀──────── pull request from origin:branch ──\n```\n\n1. Find something to work on (`good first issue`, `help wanted`, or ask first in an issue).\n2. Read `CONTRIBUTING.md`: how to set up, test, name branches, write commits.\n3. Fork and clone; add `upstream`.\n4. Create a branch **from upstream's latest main**.\n5. Commit, push the branch to **origin** (your fork).\n6. Open a PR from `you:branch` to `upstream:main`, linking the issue.\n7. Respond to review by pushing to the same branch.\n8. After merge: sync your fork, delete the branch."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Contributing with gh","md":"```term\n$ gh repo fork bookworm/reader --clone\n$ cd reader\n$ git fetch upstream\n$ git switch -c docs/install-command upstream/main\n$ edit README.md\n$ git commit -am \"Update install command in README\"\n$ git push -u origin docs/install-command\n$ gh pr create --title \"Update install command in README\" --body \"Fixes #12\"\n```\n\n`gh pr create` run inside a fork opens the PR against the **upstream** repository by default; the head is `you:docs/install-command`."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Suggesting an edit to a published book","md":"You cannot edit a printed book, but you can send the publisher a marked-up page with a polite note explaining the correction, referencing the reader's complaint they already logged. If they agree, the next printing includes it, and your name goes in the acknowledgements."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"What maintainers wish contributors knew","md":"- **Start small.** Typos, docs, tests and small bugs are perfect first PRs.\n- **Ask before big changes.** Open an issue first: \"Would you accept a PR that adds X?\"\n- **Follow the project's style**, even where you prefer another.\n- **One topic per PR**, with a clear description and tests if relevant.\n- **Be patient and kind.** Many maintainers are unpaid volunteers.\n- **Do not take silence personally**; a polite ping after a week or two is fine."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Contribution traps","md":"**Branching from your fork's stale main** instead of upstream's latest main: your PR shows unrelated changes or conflicts.\n\n**Opening the PR against your own fork.** Check the PR's base repository: it should be the upstream project.\n\n**Committing generated files or formatting the whole project.** Keep the diff focused."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Make your first contribution","md":"The `bookworm/reader` project has a good first issue. Contribute a fix from your fork, following the full flow."}},"open-source/forks":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"Permission denied to you\"","md":"Carlos found a typo in the documentation of a popular open-source charting library. He cloned it, fixed the typo, committed and pushed:\n\n```term\n$ git push\nremote: Permission to chartkit/chartkit.git denied to carlos.\nfatal: unable to access 'https://github.com/chartkit/chartkit.git/': The requested URL returned error: 403\n```\n\nOf course: he is not a maintainer of chartkit, so he cannot write to its repository. Nobody can let thousands of strangers push to their project. Open source works differently: you make **your own copy on GitHub**, a **fork**, push there, and ask the maintainers to pull your change in."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Clone vs fork","md":"| | Clone | Fork |\n|---|---|---|\n| Where | your computer | GitHub (server side) |\n| Made with | `git clone` | the Fork button or `gh repo fork` |\n| You can push to it | only if you have write access to the original | yes, it is yours |\n| Purpose | work locally | contribute to projects you cannot write to |\n\n```diagram\nfork-triangle\n```\n\nA contributor's setup has three repositories:\n\n- **upstream**: the original project (`chartkit/chartkit`). You can read it; you cannot push.\n- **origin**: your fork (`carlos/chartkit`). You push here.\n- **local**: your clone of the fork, with both `origin` and `upstream` as remotes.\n\nThe pull request goes **from your fork's branch to upstream's main**."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Fork and wire up remotes","md":"```term\n$ gh repo fork chartkit/chartkit --clone   # fork on GitHub, clone your fork, add upstream\n$ cd chartkit\n$ git remote -v\norigin    https://github.com/carlos/chartkit.git (fetch)\norigin    https://github.com/carlos/chartkit.git (push)\nupstream  https://github.com/chartkit/chartkit.git (fetch)\nupstream  https://github.com/chartkit/chartkit.git (push)\n```\n\nIf you already cloned the original (like Carlos), convert in place:\n\n```term\n$ gh repo fork --remote          # creates the fork; renames origin → upstream; adds your fork as origin\n```\n\nOr by hand:\n\n```term\n$ git remote rename origin upstream\n$ git remote add origin https://github.com/carlos/chartkit.git\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A photocopy of the master plans","md":"An architecture firm keeps the master plans in its archive. Visitors cannot draw on them. But you may take a photocopy to your own desk (fork), sketch improvements on it, and then submit your marked-up copy to the firm's review board (pull request). If they like it, *they* update the master plans."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Forks beyond open source","md":"- **Open source**: forks are how almost every outside contribution happens.\n- **Companies** sometimes use forks for contractors or for very large teams, though most internal work uses branches in one shared repository.\n- Your fork is a real repository: it has its own branches, and you can even run its Actions. But issues and the official history live upstream."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Fork traps","md":"**Working on your fork's main.** Keep `main` clean and identical to upstream's main; do every contribution on a branch.\n\n**Mixing up origin and upstream.** Push to origin (your fork); fetch from upstream (the original).\n\n**Stale forks.** A fork does not update itself. The sync lesson covers keeping it current."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Fork a project you cannot push to","md":"You want to contribute to the `chartkit/chartkit` library. First experience the 403, then fork it properly and set up both remotes."}},"open-source/opensource-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"brief","kind":"brief","title":"Your first real contribution","md":"**The scenario.** The open-source project `mapkit-community/tiles` (a map tile server) has a `good first issue`: *\"The default zoom level in config.json should be 12, not 21 (typo).\"* You are not a maintainer.\n\nYour mission:\n\n1. Fork the project and set up `origin` (your fork) and `upstream` (the project).\n2. While you work, the maintainers merge other PRs. Make sure your branch is based on the **latest** upstream main when you open the PR.\n3. Fix the issue on a well-named branch, push it to your fork, and open a PR to upstream that closes the issue.\n4. A maintainer will ask for a small change. Push it.\n5. After the maintainer merges, sync your fork's main and your local main with upstream, and delete your branch.\n\n| Need | Tools |\n|---|---|\n| Fork | `gh repo fork --clone`, `git remote -v` |\n| Stay current | `git fetch upstream`, `git rebase upstream/main` |\n| Contribute | `git push -u origin <branch>`, `gh pr create --body \"Fixes #N\"` |\n| Sync | `git merge --ff-only upstream/main`, `git push origin main` or `gh repo sync` |"},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"What Open Source gave you","md":"- A **fork** is your server-side copy of a project you cannot push to; the PR goes from your fork's branch to **upstream**.\n- Remotes: **origin** = your fork, **upstream** = the original.\n- Branch from **upstream/main**; keep your fork's main as a clean mirror; sync with fetch + fast-forward + push (or `gh repo sync`).\n- Welcoming projects have README, LICENSE, CONTRIBUTING, a code of conduct and `good first issue` labels."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Fix the zoom default upstream","md":""}},"open-source/oss-etiquette":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Two projects, same idea","md":"Two similar Markdown parsers appeared on GitHub the same month. One had a two-line README and no license. The other had a README with a demo GIF, install and usage instructions, a `LICENSE` (MIT), a `CONTRIBUTING.md`, a code of conduct, and a dozen issues labelled `good first issue`.\n\nA year later the second one had 140 contributors. The first had one. The code was of similar quality. The difference was that one project was **welcoming and usable**, and the other was a mystery."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"The files that make a project open","md":"| File | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `README.md` | What it is, why, how to install and use it, where to get help |\n| `LICENSE` | The legal permission to use, change and share the code. **No license = all rights reserved.** |\n| `CONTRIBUTING.md` | How to set up, test, and submit changes; branch and commit conventions |\n| `CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md` | Expected behaviour, and how to report problems |\n| `SECURITY.md` | How to report vulnerabilities privately (not in a public issue) |\n| `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/`, `PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md` | Structure for reports and PRs |\n| `CHANGELOG.md` | What changed in each release |\n\nGitHub recognises these files and links them from the repository page (\"community standards\").\n\n**Common licenses**: MIT and Apache-2.0 (permissive: do almost anything, keep the notice), GPL-3.0 (copyleft: derivative works must stay open under GPL). choosealicense.com explains them in plain language."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Opening a community garden","md":"A garden with a locked gate and no sign gets no volunteers. Put up a sign explaining what grows here (README), the rules for picking (LICENSE), a rota and tool shed instructions (CONTRIBUTING), a note about being kind to each other (Code of Conduct), and a list of easy jobs for first-timers (good first issues). Suddenly neighbours join in."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Setting up a project's community files","md":"```term\n$ echo \"# Project\" > README.md\n$ curl -o LICENSE https://...        # or pick one when creating the repo on GitHub\n$ mkdir -p .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE\n$ edit CONTRIBUTING.md\n$ git add . && git commit -m \"Add community health files\"\n$ gh issue create --title \"Add a dark mode example\" --label \"good first issue\" --body \"…\"\n```"},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"The maintainer's side","md":"When you maintain a project (even a small one at work):\n\n- **Label** issues so contributors can find their way in.\n- **Respond** to first-time contributors quickly and kindly. The first experience decides whether they come back.\n- **Automate** the boring parts: CI checks, formatting, templates.\n- **Say no politely**, with reasons, when a PR does not fit the project.\n- **Credit** contributors in release notes."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Open-source traps","md":"**Publishing code without a license** and assuming people may use it. They legally may not.\n\n**Reporting a security hole in a public issue.** Follow `SECURITY.md` or contact the maintainers privately.\n\n**Demanding features** from volunteers. Offer to help instead.\n\n**Copying code from another project** without respecting its license."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Make a project welcoming","md":"You maintain a small open-source library that nobody contributes to. Add the community files that turn it into a welcoming project, and create a starter issue for newcomers."}},"open-source/sync-fork":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"312 commits behind","md":"Six months after her first contribution, Amani wanted to fix another bug in `bookworm/reader`. Her fork's page said: *This branch is 312 commits behind bookworm:main.* Her local clone was just as stale. A branch made from that old main would produce a PR full of conflicts.\n\nKeeping a fork in sync is a two-step habit: bring upstream's commits into your local main, then push them to your fork."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Three copies of main","md":"| Copy | Updated by |\n|---|---|\n| `upstream/main` (the project) | maintainers merging PRs |\n| your local `main` | you: `git pull upstream main` / `git merge upstream/main` |\n| `origin/main` (your fork on GitHub) | you: `git push origin main`, or `gh repo sync` |\n\nYour fork's main should never contain your own work: it is a mirror of upstream. That way syncing is always a fast-forward.\n\n```replay\ntitle: Syncing a fork\nshow: graph\nsetup: github bookworm/reader; git clone -q https://github.com/bookworm/reader.git; cd reader; git remote rename origin upstream; teammate bookworm/reader \"Add dark mode\"; teammate bookworm/reader \"Fix crash on empty library\"\nstep: git fetch upstream | Download the project's new commits; upstream/main moves.\nstep: git merge --ff-only upstream/main | Fast-forward your local main to the project's main.\n```"},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Sync recipes","md":"```term\n$ git switch main\n$ git fetch upstream\n$ git merge --ff-only upstream/main    # or: git pull upstream main\n$ git push origin main                 # update your fork on GitHub\n$ gh repo sync                         # GitHub-side alternative: sync your fork's main from upstream\n\n# bring an in-progress branch up to date\n$ git switch my-feature\n$ git rebase upstream/main             # then: git push --force-with-lease\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Updating your photocopy","md":"Your photocopy of the master plans goes stale as the firm revises the originals. Before sketching new improvements, you collect the latest pages from the archive (fetch upstream), replace your copy's pages (fast-forward main), and file the updated copy on your desk shelf (push to your fork)."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Sync habits","md":"- **Sync before starting each new contribution**, and branch from `upstream/main`.\n- **Rebase long-lived PR branches** onto `upstream/main` when maintainers ask, then `--force-with-lease` (your branch, your fork: safe).\n- **Never commit on your fork's main.** If you did, move the commits to a branch and reset main to `upstream/main`."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Sync traps","md":"**`--ff-only` refuses.** Your main has commits upstream does not have: you committed on main. Move them to a branch (`git branch rescue`), then `git reset --hard upstream/main`.\n\n**Syncing origin but not local** (or vice versa). After `gh repo sync`, run `git pull origin main` locally too.\n\n**Pushing a feature branch to upstream.** You cannot (no permission), and you should not try: push to origin."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Catch up with the project","md":"Your fork of `bookworm/reader` and your local clone are stale, and you have an open PR branch that needs updating too. Bring everything up to date."}},"power-tools/aliases-config":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Fifty keystrokes a minute","md":"Watching a senior engineer pair-program, Theo noticed she never typed `git status`. She typed `git st`. Instead of `git log --oneline --graph --decorate --all` she typed `git lg`. Undoing the last commit was `git undo`. Over a day those shortcuts saved hundreds of keystrokes, and more importantly, she never had to remember long option lists."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Aliases","md":"```term\n$ git config --global alias.st status\n$ git config --global alias.co checkout\n$ git config --global alias.sw switch\n$ git config --global alias.br \"branch -vv\"\n$ git config --global alias.lg \"log --oneline --graph --decorate --all\"\n$ git config --global alias.last \"log -1 HEAD --stat\"\n$ git config --global alias.undo \"reset --soft HEAD~1\"\n$ git config --global alias.unstage \"restore --staged\"\n$ git config --global alias.amend \"commit --amend --no-edit\"\n$ git lg                          # use them like built-in commands\n$ git config --global --list | grep alias     # see them all\n```\n\nExtra arguments are appended: `git unstage app.js` runs `git restore --staged app.js`.\n\nAliases starting with `!` run shell commands: `git config --global alias.cleanup \"!git branch --merged | grep -v main | xargs git branch -d\"`."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Power-up settings worth knowing","md":"| Setting | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| `pull.rebase true` | `git pull` rebases instead of merging |\n| `push.autoSetupRemote true` | the first push of a branch sets its upstream |\n| `fetch.prune true` | every fetch removes stale `origin/*` branches |\n| `rebase.autoSquash true` | `git rebase -i` applies fixup!/squash! commits automatically |\n| `rerere.enabled true` | git remembers how you resolved a conflict and reuses it |\n| `init.defaultBranch main` | new repos start on main |\n| `core.editor \"code --wait\"` | your editor for messages |\n| `diff.colorMoved zebra` | moved lines are highlighted differently from changed ones |\n| `merge.conflictStyle zdiff3` | conflict markers also show the common ancestor |\n\nAll of them live in `~/.gitconfig`, a plain text file you can back up and copy between machines."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Speed-dial","md":"Aliases are speed-dial for git. You still know the full number (the real command), but you do not have to dial it every time."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Sharing and restraint","md":"- Many teams share a recommended `.gitconfig` snippet in their onboarding docs.\n- Keep aliases memorable and few. A teammate reading over your shoulder should still understand.\n- Learn the full commands first: aliases are for speed, not for avoiding understanding. In interviews, docs and other people's machines, only the real commands exist."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Alias traps","md":"**Shadowing real commands.** An alias with the name of a built-in command is ignored by git.\n\n**Quoting.** Multi-word aliases need quotes: `alias.lg \"log --oneline --graph\"`.\n\n**Dangerous shortcuts.** An alias for `reset --hard` makes destruction too easy to type."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Build your toolkit","md":"Set up a handful of aliases and power-up settings, then use them in a small repository."}},"power-tools/bisect":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"400 commits, one bug","md":"The invoice totals in a billing system were off by a few cents, but only for some customers. It had worked three weeks earlier, at release v4.2. Since then, 400 commits had landed. Reading them all would take days.\n\nRosa used `git bisect`:\n\n```term\n$ git bisect start\n$ git bisect bad                # current version is broken\n$ git bisect good v4.2          # this release was fine\nBisecting: 199 revisions left to test after this (roughly 8 steps)\n```\n\nGit checked out a commit halfway between. She ran the totals test and told git `good` or `bad`. After **nine** tests, git printed the exact commit that introduced the rounding error. With `git bisect run npm test`, she could even have let git do the testing on its own."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Binary search through history","md":"```diagram\nbisect\n```\n\nYou give git one **bad** commit and one **good** commit. Git checks out the commit in the middle. You test it and answer `good` or `bad`, which discards half of the remaining suspects. Repeat until one commit is left: the **first bad commit**.\n\nThe number of steps grows very slowly: 16 commits need about 4 tests, 1,000 commits about 10, a million about 20.\n\nBisect requires a **reliable test** that tells good from bad. It works best when every commit builds (another reason for atomic commits)."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Driving a bisect","md":"```term\n$ git bisect start\n$ git bisect bad                    # HEAD is broken\n$ git bisect good v4.2              # a known good commit or tag\n#   ... git checks out a middle commit; test it ...\n$ git bisect good                   # or: git bisect bad\n#   ... repeat until \"<id> is the first bad commit\" ...\n$ git bisect reset                  # back to where you started\n\n$ git bisect start HEAD v4.2        # bad and good in one line\n$ git bisect run npm test           # automatic: exit code 0 = good, non-zero = bad\n$ git bisect skip                   # this commit cannot be tested (e.g. does not build)\n$ git bisect log                    # what you answered so far\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Finding a dead bulb in a string of lights","md":"A string of 400 fairy lights is dark because one bulb is dead. Testing bulbs one by one takes forever. Instead, test whether power reaches the middle. If it does, the dead bulb is in the second half; if not, the first half. Each test halves the search. Nine tests find the bulb among 400."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Bisect in real debugging","md":"- Turn \"it used to work\" into a **specific commit**, author and message, which usually explains the *why*.\n- Write the test first (a failing unit test, or a small script), then `git bisect run` it.\n- Combine with `git show <commit>` and `git blame` to understand the change.\n- Your teammates will be impressed. Mostly because it feels like magic."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Bisect traps","md":"**Forgetting `git bisect reset`.** You stay in detached HEAD on some old commit. The prompt shows BISECTING.\n\n**Mislabelling one commit.** One wrong `good`/`bad` sends the search into the wrong half. If results look odd, `git bisect log`, then restart.\n\n**Flaky tests.** A test that sometimes fails will mislead bisect."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Hunt the regression","md":"The calculator's tests pass at tag v1.0 but fail now, twelve commits later. Find the commit that broke it, first by hand, then automatically with `git bisect run`."}},"power-tools/blame-archaeology":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"Why is the timeout 47 seconds?\"","md":"Ben found a strange line in the API client: `timeout: 47`. Not 30, not 60: 47. Changing it felt risky without knowing why it existed.\n\n```term\n$ git blame -L 12,12 client.js\n8f2a1c9 (Hana Ito 2025-03-14 10:22:01 +0000 12)   timeout: 47,\n$ git show 8f2a1c9\n    Raise timeout to 47s: the bank's API times out at 45s and\n    retries need 2s of headroom. See incident #318.\n```\n\nTwenty seconds of archaeology answered the question and prevented an outage. **Code tells you how; history tells you why.**"},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Archaeology tools","md":"```term\n$ git blame file.js                    # who last changed each line, in which commit\n$ git blame -L 10,20 file.js           # only lines 10-20\n$ git blame -e file.js                 # show emails\n$ git log -S \"timeout: 47\" --oneline   # commits that added or removed this exact text\n$ git log -p -- file.js                # the full history of one file with diffs\n$ git log --follow -- new-name.js      # follow a file across renames (real git)\n$ git show <commit>                    # read the commit that explains it\n$ git log --grep=\"incident\"            # search messages\n```"},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What blame really tells you","md":"`git blame` shows, for each line, the **last commit that changed it**. That is not always who *wrote the idea*:\n\n- A formatting commit (\"reindent everything\") becomes the blame for every line it touched. Many teams list such commits in `.git-blame-ignore-revs` and run `git blame --ignore-revs-file .git-blame-ignore-revs`.\n- A moved block shows the move, not the original author (`git blame -C` can detect moves).\n\nSo blame is the **starting point**: take the commit id, then `git show` it, then search further back with `git log -S` if needed."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Layers in an archaeological dig","md":"Each commit is a layer of soil. `git blame` labels every artefact with the layer it was found in. Sometimes a later layer simply disturbed an older object (a reformat). An experienced archaeologist digs deeper (`git log -S`) before concluding who made the pot."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Blame without blaming","md":"Despite the name, `git blame` is not about fault. It finds **context** and **the right person to ask**. Healthy teams treat it as \"who can tell me more about this?\", never as ammunition. Good commit messages make every blame useful."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Archaeology traps","md":"**Stopping at the first blame result** when it is a formatting commit.\n\n**Ignoring the message.** The commit id is a means to an end: read the message, the linked issue and the PR discussion.\n\n**Concluding \"nobody knows why\" too early.** Search messages and code changes before deleting mysterious code."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Explain the mystery numbers","md":"A payments client contains two suspicious constants. Use blame, show and the pickaxe to find out who set them, when, and why."}},"power-tools/hooks":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The commit that would have leaked a key","md":"Kenji's team had leaked an API key twice in a year. Each time, someone committed a `.env`-style line by accident. They added a tiny **pre-commit hook**: a script git runs before every commit, which refuses the commit if the staged changes contain something that looks like a secret.\n\n```term\n$ git commit -m \"Add payment client\"\nBlocked: staged changes contain an API key.\n```\n\nThe third leak never happened."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Scripts at key moments","md":"**Hooks** are executable scripts in `.git/hooks/` that git runs at specific moments. If a \"pre-\" hook exits with a non-zero code, git aborts the operation.\n\n| Hook | Runs | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|\n| `pre-commit` | before a commit is created | lint, format check, secret scan, tests |\n| `prepare-commit-msg` | before the editor opens | prefill a template or ticket number |\n| `commit-msg` | after you write the message | enforce Conventional Commits |\n| `pre-push` | before a push | run the test suite |\n| `post-merge`, `post-checkout` | after those operations | reinstall dependencies |\n\nA hook is just a program: shell, Python, Node, anything executable. It must be **executable** (`chmod +x`), and it is **not** committed with the repository (`.git/` is never shared). Teams share hooks through tools like **husky**, **lefthook** or **pre-commit**, or with `git config core.hooksPath .githooks` pointing at a committed folder."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Writing a pre-commit hook","md":"Put this in `.git/hooks/pre-commit`:\n\n```text\n#!/bin/sh\nif git diff --cached | grep -q \"API_KEY\"; then\n  echo \"Blocked: staged changes contain an API key.\"\n  exit 1\nfi\n```\n\nThen make it executable and commit as usual:\n\n```term\n$ chmod +x .git/hooks/pre-commit\n$ git commit -m \"Add payment client\"      # the hook runs first\n$ git commit --no-verify -m \"…\"           # skip hooks (use rarely, and knowingly)\n```\n\nIn this simulator, write hooks with `edit .git/hooks/pre-commit`. The shell supports `if … then … fi`, `grep -q`, `echo`, pipes and `exit`.\n\nA **commit-msg** hook receives the path of the message file as `$1`:\n\n```text\n#!/bin/sh\nif ! grep -qE \"^(feat|fix|docs|chore|refactor|test)(\\(.+\\))?: \" \"$1\"; then\n  echo \"Use a Conventional Commit message, e.g. 'fix: handle empty carts'.\"\n  exit 1\nfi\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The bouncer checks bags","md":"`.gitignore` is the guest list. Hooks are the bouncer who also checks bags: before a commit is allowed in, the bouncer looks inside and turns away anything dangerous."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Hooks and CI together","md":"Local hooks give **fast feedback** but can be skipped (`--no-verify`) or simply not installed. CI checks on pull requests are the **enforcement**. Use both: hooks to catch problems in seconds, CI so nothing slips through."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Hook traps","md":"**Forgetting `chmod +x`.** Git ignores non-executable hooks (and real git prints a hint).\n\n**Slow hooks.** A three-minute pre-commit hook teaches people to use `--no-verify`. Keep pre-commit fast; run the full suite in pre-push or CI.\n\n**Assuming hooks are shared.** They are not, unless you use a tool or `core.hooksPath`."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Guard the repository","md":"Add a pre-commit hook that blocks leftover `console.log` debugging, and a commit-msg hook that enforces Conventional Commits. Watch them block a bad commit, then make a good one."}},"power-tools/internals":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"Git is just a content-addressable filesystem\"","md":"Halima kept memorising commands without feeling she understood git. A colleague said: \"Spend one hour inside `.git` and everything will click.\" They hashed a tiny piece of text:\n\n```term\n$ echo \"hello\" | git hash-object --stdin\nce013625030ba8dba906f756967f9e9ca394464a\n```\n\nEvery git installation in the world produces that same id for the content \"hello\" plus a newline, because the id **is** a hash of the content. Commits, folders and files are all stored that way. Branches are just files holding ids. After that hour, merge, rebase and reset stopped being magic."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Four kinds of objects","md":"```diagram\nobject-model\n```\n\n| Object | Stores | Points to |\n|---|---|---|\n| **blob** | the content of one file (no name!) | nothing |\n| **tree** | one directory: names, modes and ids of blobs and subtrees | blobs, trees |\n| **commit** | author, committer, message, one tree, zero or more parents | a tree, parent commits |\n| **tag** (annotated) | tagger, message, and an object id | usually a commit |\n\nEvery object's id is the **SHA-1 of its type, size and content**. So:\n\n- identical content is stored **once**, however many commits contain it;\n- changing anything (even one byte of a message) changes the id, and every id that depends on it;\n- history is **tamper-evident**: you cannot change an old commit without changing every later id.\n\nBranches and tags are **refs**: small files mapping names to ids. HEAD is a ref to a ref."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Exploring the object database","md":"```term\n$ git cat-file -t HEAD              # type: commit\n$ git cat-file -p HEAD              # the commit: tree, parent, author, message\n$ git cat-file -p HEAD^{tree}       # the root tree: one line per file or folder\n$ git cat-file -p <blob-id>         # a file's content\n$ git cat-file -s <id>              # size in bytes\n$ git hash-object file.txt          # the id this content would have\n$ git rev-parse HEAD                # resolve any name to a full id\n$ git ls-files --stage              # the index: mode, blob id, stage, path\n$ ls .git/objects                   # loose objects, in folders named by the first 2 hex digits\n$ cat .git/refs/heads/main          # a branch is just an id\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A library that files pages by fingerprint","md":"Imagine a library where every page is filed under a fingerprint computed from its exact text. Two identical pages share one slot. A chapter (tree) is a card listing page fingerprints in order. A book edition (commit) is a card pointing at its chapter list and at the previous edition. Change one comma and you get a new fingerprint, a new chapter card and a new edition card. Nothing old is ever overwritten."},{"id":"inside","kind":"inside","title":"How familiar commands map to objects","md":"- `git add` writes **blobs** for the staged content and records them in the index.\n- `git commit` writes **trees** from the index and a **commit** pointing at the root tree and the parent, then moves the branch ref.\n- `git branch x` writes one ref file.\n- `git merge` (non-fast-forward) writes a commit with **two parents**.\n- `git rebase` writes new commits with new parents (hence new ids); trees for unchanged files are reused.\n- `git gc` packs loose objects into compressed pack files and removes unreachable ones after their grace period."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Internals traps","md":"**Editing files in `.git` by hand.** Use commands; you can corrupt the repository.\n\n**\"SHA-1 is broken, so git is insecure.\"** Git uses hardened SHA-1 with collision detection and supports SHA-256 repositories. For everyday integrity, ids remain reliable.\n\n**Expecting blobs to know their filename.** Names live in trees, which is why renaming a file creates a new tree but reuses the same blob."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Open the hood","md":"Explore a small repository at the object level: hash content, read a commit, walk its tree down to a blob, and prove that identical content is stored only once."}},"power-tools/power-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"brief","kind":"brief","title":"The mystery regression","md":"**The scenario.** *Quill* is a small Markdown-to-HTML converter. Since last week, headings render without their text. The last known good release is tag `v2.0`. Since then there are about a dozen commits from four people. Your job:\n\n1. Find the exact commit that broke headings, ideally letting `git bisect run` do the work.\n2. Find out who wrote the faulty line and read why they changed it.\n3. Fix the bug on `main` with a revert, without rewriting history.\n4. Make sure it cannot quietly happen again: add a `pre-commit` hook that runs the tests and blocks the commit if they fail.\n5. Add a `git lg` alias for the graph view.\n\n| Need | Tools |\n|---|---|\n| Find the commit | `git bisect start HEAD v2.0`, `git bisect run npm test`, `git bisect reset` |\n| Understand it | `git show`, `git blame`, `git log -S` |\n| Fix | `git revert` |\n| Prevent | `.git/hooks/pre-commit`, `chmod +x` |\n| Speed | `git config --global alias.lg \"…\"` |\n\nIn this repository, `npm test` checks that a heading like `# Hello` renders as `<h1>Hello</h1>`."},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"What Power Tools gave you","md":"- `git bisect` finds the first bad commit by binary search; `git bisect run` automates it.\n- `git blame`, `git log -S` and `git show` explain why code is the way it is.\n- Aliases and settings (`pull.rebase`, `fetch.prune`, `rerere`, `autoSquash`) speed you up.\n- Hooks (`pre-commit`, `commit-msg`, `pre-push`) automate checks locally; CI enforces them.\n- Under the hood: blobs, trees, commits and tags, addressed by content hashes; refs are names for ids.\n- Worktrees give one repository several working folders; submodules pin other repositories; LFS keeps big files out of history."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Track down the heading bug","md":""}},"power-tools/worktree-submodules":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Two branches open at once","md":"Olu was halfway through a large refactor when a production bug came in. Stashing a refactor that touched 30 files felt risky, and switching branches meant rebuilding the project each time. Instead:\n\n```term\n$ git worktree add ../shop-hotfix -b hotfix/vat main\n$ cd ../shop-hotfix          # a second folder, on the hotfix branch\n```\n\nOne repository, two working folders, each on its own branch. He fixed the bug in the second folder, pushed, and went back to the refactor exactly as he had left it."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Worktrees","md":"A **worktree** is an extra working directory attached to the same repository. All worktrees share the object database, branches and configuration; each has its own HEAD, index and files.\n\n| | Clone again | Worktree |\n|---|---|---|\n| Disk space | a full second copy of history | only the working files |\n| Branches | separate; push and pull between clones | shared instantly |\n| Same branch in two places | possible (and confusing) | refused by git |\n\n```term\n$ git worktree add ../hotfix -b hotfix/vat main   # new folder + new branch from main\n$ git worktree add ../review feature/search       # an existing branch in a new folder\n$ git worktree list\n$ git worktree remove ../hotfix\n```"},{"id":"model-2","kind":"model","title":"Submodules","md":"A **submodule** embeds another repository inside yours, pinned to a **specific commit**. The parent repository stores only the submodule's URL (in `.gitmodules`) and the pinned commit id.\n\n```term\n$ git submodule add https://github.com/acme/design-tokens.git libs/tokens\n$ git commit -m \"Add design tokens as a submodule\"\n$ git clone --recurse-submodules <url>     # clone a project including its submodules\n$ git submodule update --init --recursive  # after a normal clone or pull\n$ cd libs/tokens && git pull && cd ../..   # move the pin to a newer commit…\n$ git commit -am \"Update design tokens\"    # …and record the new pin in the parent\n```\n\nSubmodules are powerful but easy to get wrong (forgetting `--recurse-submodules`, a detached HEAD inside the submodule). Many teams prefer a package manager or a monorepo instead."},{"id":"model-3","kind":"model","title":"Big files: Git LFS","md":"Git stores every version of every file forever, so large binaries (videos, datasets, design files) make repositories huge. **Git LFS** (Large File Storage) stores those files on a separate server and keeps only small pointer files in git:\n\n```term\n$ git lfs install\n$ git lfs track \"*.psd\"\n$ git add .gitattributes design.psd\n$ git commit -m \"Track Photoshop files with LFS\"\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Desks, shelves and warehouses","md":"- **Worktrees** are several desks in one office, all using the same shared filing cabinet.\n- A **submodule** is a book from another library on your shelf, with a sticky note saying exactly which edition you rely on.\n- **LFS** is a warehouse for bulky items; your shelf holds only the claim tickets."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Advanced-tool traps","md":"**Deleting a worktree folder by hand.** Use `git worktree remove` (or `git worktree prune` afterwards).\n\n**Committing inside a submodule and forgetting to push it.** The parent then points at a commit nobody else can fetch.\n\n**Adding LFS after big files are already in history.** They stay in old commits; migrating needs `git lfs migrate`."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Fix a bug without leaving your refactor","md":"You are mid-refactor with uncommitted changes when a hotfix is needed. Use a worktree to fix it in parallel, then clean up. Submodules and LFS appear in the quiz."}},"remotes/clone-origin":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"Where does origin come from?\"","md":"On her first day, Grace cloned the company's main repository:\n\n```term\n$ git clone https://github.com/acme/storefront.git\nCloning into 'storefront'...\n$ cd storefront\n$ git branch -a\n* main\n  remotes/origin/HEAD -> origin/main\n  remotes/origin/main\n  remotes/origin/checkout-v2\n```\n\nShe had never created anything called `origin`, and there were branches she had never made. Her mentor explained: cloning copies the whole repository **and** remembers where it came from. That remembered address has a nickname, `origin`, and git keeps a read-only bookmark of every branch it saw there: `origin/main`, `origin/checkout-v2`."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Remotes and remote-tracking branches","md":"```diagram\nremote-model\n```\n\n- A **remote** is a named URL of another copy of the repository. `origin` is just the default name given to the one you cloned from.\n- A **remote-tracking branch** like `origin/main` is your local, read-only record of where `main` was **on that remote the last time you talked to it**. It only moves when you `fetch`, `pull` or `push`.\n- Your **local branch** `main` is yours to commit on. After a clone, it is set to **track** `origin/main` (its *upstream*), so git can tell you if you are ahead or behind.\n\nThree different things all called \"main\": your `main`, your bookmark `origin/main`, and GitHub's actual `main`. Most remote confusion comes from mixing them up."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Clone and inspect remotes","md":"```term\n$ git clone <url>                     # copy a repository; creates origin and a local main\n$ git clone <url> my-folder           # choose the folder name\n$ git remote                          # list remote names\n$ git remote -v                       # with their URLs\n$ git remote show origin              # details: branches, tracking\n$ git branch -a                       # local and remote-tracking branches\n$ git branch -vv                      # local branches with upstream and ahead/behind\n$ git remote add upstream <url>       # add a second remote\n$ git remote rename origin old        # rename\n$ git remote set-url origin <new-url> # change the address (e.g. HTTPS → SSH)\n$ git remote remove old               # forget a remote\n```\n\nURLs come in two flavours you will meet constantly:\n\n| Kind | Example | Authentication |\n|---|---|---|\n| HTTPS | `https://github.com/acme/storefront.git` | token / credential manager |\n| SSH | `git@github.com:acme/storefront.git` | SSH key |\n\nBoth reach the same repository. The GitHub realm covers authentication."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A map with a \"last updated\" date","md":"`origin/main` is like a printed map of a city with \"last updated: Tuesday\" in the corner. It is accurate for Tuesday. If roads changed since, your map does not know until you buy a new edition (`git fetch`). Your own notes in the margin (local commits) are separate from the printed map."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Typical remote setups","md":"- **Your team's repository**: one remote, `origin`, which everyone pushes to.\n- **Open source with a fork**: `origin` is your fork, `upstream` is the original project (Open Source realm).\n- **Several servers**: e.g. `origin` on GitHub and `deploy` on a hosting provider; `git push deploy main` deploys.\n- `git clone` copies **all** history and branches. Only the default branch gets a local branch; others become local branches the moment you `git switch` to them."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Remote traps","md":"**Thinking `origin/main` is live.** It is a snapshot from your last fetch. Fetch before comparing.\n\n**Committing on `origin/main`.** You cannot: checking it out gives a detached HEAD. Work on local branches.\n\n**Wrong URL type.** An SSH URL without an SSH key fails with \"Permission denied (publickey)\". Switch to HTTPS or set up a key.\n\n**Cloning into an existing repository folder.** Clone creates a new folder. Run it from where you want the project folder to appear."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Clone the storefront","md":"Your team's storefront repository lives on GitHub. Clone it, inspect the remote, explore remote-tracking branches, and check out a branch that so far exists only on GitHub."}},"remotes/divergence":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The pull that asked a question","md":"After a git upgrade, Malik's usual `git pull` stopped and printed a wall of hints ending in:\n\n```term\nfatal: Need to specify how to reconcile divergent branches.\n```\n\nHis local `main` had two commits that GitHub did not have, and GitHub had three that he did not have. The branches had **diverged**. Older git versions silently created a merge commit in this situation; newer versions ask you to choose a policy once, so pulls stop surprising you."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Divergence and the three choices","md":"```replay\ntitle: Divergent branches: rebase vs merge\nshow: graph\nsetup: github acme/app; git clone -q https://github.com/acme/app.git; cd app; commit \"My change A\"; commit \"My change B\"; teammate acme/app \"Their change X\"; teammate acme/app \"Their change Y\"; git fetch -q\nstep: git status | Ahead by 2, behind by 2: the branches have diverged. A fast-forward is impossible in either direction.\nstep: git pull --rebase | Rebase policy: your two commits are replayed on top of theirs. Straight line, your commits get new ids.\nstep: git push | Now a normal push works.\n```\n\nWhen local and remote have both moved, a pull must combine them, and there are three policies:\n\n| Setting | Behaviour on divergence | History |\n|---|---|---|\n| `pull.rebase false` | merge (creates a merge commit) | true record, with \"Merge branch 'main' of …\" commits |\n| `pull.rebase true` | rebase your local commits on top | linear |\n| `pull.ff only` | refuse; you decide manually | never surprising |\n\n```term\n$ git config --global pull.rebase true    # a popular choice for everyday work\n$ git pull --rebase                       # or choose per pull\n$ git pull --no-rebase                    # merge this time\n```"},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Seeing and fixing divergence","md":"```term\n$ git fetch\n$ git status                          # \"have diverged, and have 2 and 3 different commits each\"\n$ git log --oneline --graph --all     # see both lines\n$ git log --oneline origin/main..main # mine, not on the server\n$ git log --oneline main..origin/main # theirs, not on my machine\n$ git pull --rebase                   # replay mine on top of theirs\n$ git rebase --continue               # if a conflict stopped it\n$ git push\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Two hikers on a forked trail","md":"You and a friend walked from the same signpost but took different paths for a while. To continue together you either **walk back and follow their path, then repeat your detour on top** (rebase), or **meet at a new junction that joins both paths** (merge). Either way, you cannot pretend the other person's path does not exist."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"What teams choose","md":"- Many teams set `pull.rebase true` for local branches to avoid noisy \"Merge branch 'main' of github.com/…\" commits.\n- Whatever you choose, **be consistent** across the team and document it.\n- If a rebase-pull conflicts and you get lost, `git rebase --abort` and try `git pull --no-rebase` instead."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Divergence traps","md":"**Answering the hint with `--force`.** Divergence is solved by integrating, not overwriting.\n\n**Rebasing commits you already pushed elsewhere.** `pull --rebase` only replays commits that are not on the upstream, so it is safe for normal use.\n\n**Ignoring \"ahead/behind\" in `git status`.** It tells you exactly what will happen on your next push or pull."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Reconcile divergent branches","md":"While you worked offline, teammates pushed to main. Experience the divergence error, choose a policy, reconcile with a rebase, and push."}},"remotes/fetch-pull":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"Pull broke my branch\"","md":"Every morning Dev typed `git pull` without thinking. One day it produced a merge commit he did not expect, on a branch where he had local commits, and his history became tangled.\n\nHis lead suggested a habit: **look before you leap**.\n\n```term\n$ git fetch\nFrom https://github.com/acme/storefront\n   3c1d0a2..9e8f7b6  main       -> origin/main\n$ git log --oneline main..origin/main\n9e8f7b6 Add gift cards\n4a2b1c0 Fix currency rounding\n$ git merge origin/main\n```\n\n`git fetch` downloaded the new commits and moved `origin/main`, but did not touch his work. He could inspect exactly what had arrived, then integrate it on his terms."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Fetch updates bookmarks; pull also integrates","md":"```replay\ntitle: fetch, then merge (what pull does)\nshow: graph\nsetup: github acme/storefront; git clone -q https://github.com/acme/storefront.git; cd storefront; teammate acme/storefront \"Add gift cards\"\nstep: git status | Your clone has no idea anything changed. origin/main is still where it was at clone time.\nstep: git fetch | fetch downloads new commits and moves origin/main. Your main has not moved.\nstep: git merge origin/main | Integrate: main fast-forwards to origin/main. fetch + merge is exactly what git pull does.\n```\n\n| Command | Downloads | Moves `origin/*` | Changes your branch & files |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `git fetch` | yes | yes | **no** |\n| `git pull` | yes | yes | yes: merges (or rebases) the upstream into the current branch |\n\n`git pull` = `git fetch` + `git merge @{upstream}` (or `git rebase` with `--rebase` / `pull.rebase=true`)."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Fetch and pull","md":"```term\n$ git fetch                     # fetch from the current branch's remote (usually origin)\n$ git fetch --all               # from every remote\n$ git fetch --prune             # also forget branches deleted on the remote\n$ git log --oneline main..origin/main   # what arrived that I don't have\n$ git diff main origin/main     # how the files differ\n$ git pull                      # fetch + merge into the current branch\n$ git pull --rebase             # fetch + rebase your local commits on top\n$ git pull --ff-only            # only if it's a fast-forward; otherwise refuse\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Checking the mailbox vs opening every letter","md":"`git fetch` is collecting the mail from the mailbox and putting it on the hall table. You can read the envelopes, decide what to open, and nothing in your house changes yet.\n\n`git pull` is collecting the mail and immediately opening every letter and filing its contents into your folders. Convenient, but you did not get to look first."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Pulling well","md":"- **Pull before you start work** each day, and before you push.\n- **`git fetch` + look** when you are unsure what will happen.\n- Teams often set `git config --global pull.rebase true` or `pull.ff only` so pulls never create surprise merge commits on local branches.\n- If a pull conflicts, it is just a merge (or rebase) conflict: resolve it the usual way."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Fetch/pull traps","md":"**Pulling with uncommitted changes.** If incoming changes touch the same files, git refuses. Commit or stash, or use `git pull --rebase --autostash`.\n\n**Expecting `fetch` to change your files.** It never does.\n\n**Pulling on the wrong branch.** `git pull` integrates into the **current** branch. Check `git branch` first."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Look before you leap","md":"Teammates have pushed while you were away. Fetch, inspect exactly what arrived, then integrate."}},"remotes/force-prune":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The force push that erased a day","md":"Late one evening Sven rebased his branch, pushed with `--force`, and logged off. He had forgotten that Nora had pushed two commits to the same branch an hour earlier. His force push replaced the remote branch with his version; her commits were simply gone from GitHub.\n\nThey recovered them from Nora's laptop the next morning, but the team adopted a rule that day: **never `--force`; use `--force-with-lease`**.\n\n```term\n$ git push --force-with-lease\n ! [rejected]        feature/search -> feature/search (stale info)\n```\n\n`--force-with-lease` would have refused, because the remote branch was no longer where Sven's `origin/feature/search` said it was. Someone had pushed since his last fetch."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Force with a safety check","md":"| Command | Overwrites remote branch… |\n|---|---|\n| `git push` | never (fast-forwards only) |\n| `git push --force` | always, even if others pushed since you last fetched |\n| `git push --force-with-lease` | only if the remote is still exactly where your `origin/<branch>` bookmark says it is |\n\nYou need a force push after **rewriting** a branch you had already pushed (rebase, amend, interactive rebase), typically your own feature branch during review. Never on shared branches like `main`."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Force, delete, prune","md":"```term\n$ git push --force-with-lease               # safe overwrite of your own rewritten branch\n$ git push origin --delete feature/old      # delete a branch on the remote\n$ git push origin :feature/old              # older syntax for the same thing\n$ git fetch --prune                         # remove origin/* bookmarks for branches deleted on the remote\n$ git config --global fetch.prune true      # prune automatically on every fetch\n$ git branch -vv                            # \": gone]\" marks local branches whose upstream was deleted\n$ git remote prune origin                   # prune without fetching\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Replacing a page, politely","md":"`--force` is ripping out the last pages of the shared logbook and gluing in your own, without looking. `--force-with-lease` is first checking that the last page is still the one you remember. If someone has added a page since, you stop and talk to them."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Cleaning up after merges","md":"After a pull request merges, its branch is usually deleted on GitHub (automatically or with a button). Your machine still has:\n\n1. the local branch `feature/x`, and\n2. the bookmark `origin/feature/x`.\n\n`git fetch --prune` removes the bookmark; `git branch -d feature/x` removes the local branch. `git branch -vv` shows which local branches have lost their upstream (`[origin/feature/x: gone]`)."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Force and prune traps","md":"**`--force` on main.** Rewrites history for the whole team. Protected branches on GitHub usually forbid it.\n\n**Fetching right before `--force-with-lease`.** A fresh fetch updates your bookmark to the remote's current state, so the lease check passes even if you have not looked at the new commits. Look at what you fetched before forcing.\n\n**Deleting the remote branch someone still uses.** Check open pull requests first."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Rewrite safely and clean up","md":"Your feature branch on GitHub needs a rebase-and-force-push, a teammate interferes, and afterwards you clean up merged branches locally and remotely."}},"remotes/push-upstream":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"Rejected?!\"","md":"Priya finished a fix and pushed:\n\n```term\n$ git push\nTo https://github.com/acme/storefront.git\n ! [rejected]        main -> main (fetch first)\nerror: failed to push some refs to 'https://github.com/acme/storefront.git'\nhint: Updates were rejected because the remote contains work that you do not\nhint: have locally. ...\n```\n\nHer first instinct was to search \"how to force push\". Her lead stopped her. The message meant exactly what it said: **someone else had pushed first**. Git was protecting their work. Pushing would only be allowed once her branch *contained* theirs.\n\n```term\n$ git pull --rebase\n$ git push\n   9e8f7b6..a1c2d3e  main -> main\n```"},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Push only moves forward","md":"`git push` asks the remote to move its branch to your commit. The remote agrees only if that is a **fast-forward**: the remote's current commit must already be in your history. Otherwise your push would throw away commits that exist only on the server.\n\n```replay\ntitle: A rejected push, and the fix\nshow: graph\nsetup: github acme/storefront; git clone -q https://github.com/acme/storefront.git; cd storefront; commit \"Fix price rounding\"; teammate acme/storefront \"Add gift cards\"\nstep: git push | Rejected: GitHub has a commit you do not have. Pushing would erase it.\nstep: git pull --rebase | Fetch the teammate's commit and replay yours on top.\nstep: git push | Now GitHub's main is in your history, so the push is a fast-forward.\n```"},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Pushing and upstreams","md":"```term\n$ git push                          # push the current branch to its upstream\n$ git push origin main              # push main to origin explicitly\n$ git push -u origin feature/x      # first push of a new branch: create it on origin and set upstream\n$ git push origin feature/x:review  # push local feature/x to a remote branch called review\n$ git push --tags                   # push tags\n$ git branch -vv                    # see each branch's upstream and ahead/behind\n$ git branch -u origin/feature/x    # set the upstream of the current branch afterwards\n```\n\nA brand-new branch has no upstream, so a plain `git push` explains what to do:\n\n```term\nfatal: The current branch feature/gift-cards has no upstream branch.\nTo push the current branch and set the remote as upstream, use\n\n    git push --set-upstream origin feature/gift-cards\n```\n\n`-u` is short for `--set-upstream`. After that one push, plain `git push` and `git pull` work on that branch. Setting `git config --global push.autoSetupRemote true` makes git do this automatically.\n\n`git status` uses the upstream to tell you where you stand:\n\n```term\nYour branch is ahead of 'origin/main' by 2 commits.\n  (use \"git push\" to publish your local commits)\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A shared logbook","md":"The remote branch is a shared logbook. You may only **append** pages after the last page in it. If someone appended pages since you last looked, you must first copy their pages into your own copy (pull), add yours after them, and then append to the shared logbook. Tearing out their pages so yours fit (force push) is not allowed without a very good reason."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Push habits","md":"- **Pull (rebase) before you push**, especially on shared branches.\n- **Push feature branches early** with `-u`. It backs up your work and lets others see it.\n- On GitHub, pushing a new branch prints a link to open a pull request for it.\n- Many teams block direct pushes to `main` entirely (protected branches): all changes go through pull requests."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Push traps","md":"**Reaching for `--force` on a rejection.** A rejection on a shared branch means \"integrate first\", not \"overwrite\".\n\n**Pushing the wrong branch.** `git push origin main` while on a feature branch pushes your local *main*, not the feature.\n\n**Expecting `git push` to push all branches.** By default it pushes only the current branch."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Your first pushes","md":"You cloned the team repository. Push a fix to main (a teammate will beat you to it), then publish a new feature branch with an upstream."}},"remotes/remotes-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"brief","kind":"brief","title":"The busy morning","md":"**The scenario.** You are on the payments team at *Kestrel*, a booking platform. The repository `kestrel/payments` is busy: four teammates push constantly. This morning:\n\n- You need the latest code: clone the repository into `~/work`.\n- Build a small fix on a new branch `fix/refund-rounding`, publish it with an upstream, then update it after a teammate's change lands on main.\n- While you work, teammates push to `main` and to your branch's base. Your pushes will be rejected at least once.\n- Your teammate Nora already merged and deleted `feature/old-checkout` on GitHub; clean up your bookmarks.\n- Finally, `main` on your machine must match GitHub, and your fix branch must be published, rebased on the latest main, and never force-pushed without a lease.\n\n| Need | Tools |\n|---|---|\n| Get the code | `git clone`, `git remote -v`, `git branch -a` |\n| Stay current | `git fetch`, `git log main..origin/main`, `git pull --rebase` |\n| Publish | `git push -u origin <branch>` |\n| Rewrite your own pushed branch | `git rebase origin/main`, `git push --force-with-lease` |\n| Clean up | `git fetch --prune`, `git branch -vv` |"},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"What Remotes gave you","md":"- A **remote** is a named URL (`origin` by default). **Remote-tracking branches** (`origin/main`) are read-only bookmarks updated by fetch, pull and push.\n- **fetch** downloads and moves bookmarks; **pull** = fetch + merge/rebase.\n- **push** only fast-forwards. Rejected? Integrate first. `-u` sets the upstream on a branch's first push.\n- **Divergence**: choose a pull policy (`pull.rebase true` is popular).\n- **Force** only your own rewritten branches, always `--force-with-lease`. **Prune** stale bookmarks."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Ship a fix on a busy repository","md":""}},"rewriting/cherry-pick":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"One fix, two releases","md":"The mobile team at a ride-sharing startup supports two versions: the current `main` and a `release/2.4` branch still used by many customers. Kenji fixed a crash in fare calculation on `main`. Customers on 2.4 needed the same fix, but nothing else from `main`, which contained half-finished features.\n\n```term\n$ git switch release/2.4\n$ git cherry-pick 7c3e9a1\n[release/2.4 b42f0d8] Fix crash when the fare is zero\n 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)\n```\n\n`git cherry-pick` copied exactly that one commit's change onto the release branch."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Copying one commit's change","md":"```diagram\ncherry-pick\n```\n\nCherry-pick takes the **change** a commit introduced (its diff against its parent) and applies it on top of the current branch as a **new commit**: same message and author, new id, new parent.\n\n```replay\ntitle: Cherry-picking a hotfix onto a release branch\nshow: graph\nsetup: init; commit \"Release 2.4\"; git branch release-2.4; commit \"Start new booking flow\"; commit \"Fix crash when fare is zero\"; commit \"Redesign the map\"; git switch release-2.4\nstep: git cherry-pick main~1 | Only the fix is copied to release-2.4, as a brand-new commit. The new booking flow and map redesign stay on main.\n```"},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Cherry-pick recipes","md":"```term\n$ git cherry-pick <commit>          # copy one commit here\n$ git cherry-pick A B C             # several commits, in that order\n$ git cherry-pick A..B              # a range (commits after A up to B)\n$ git cherry-pick -x <commit>       # add \"(cherry picked from commit …)\" to the message\n$ git cherry-pick --no-commit <c>   # apply the change but let me commit it\n$ git cherry-pick --continue | --abort | --skip   # after a conflict\n```\n\n`-x` is good practice on release branches: the note records where the fix came from."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Photocopying one recipe","md":"You have a big family cookbook (main) and a small pocket book (the release branch). You do not copy the whole cookbook into the pocket book; you photocopy the one recipe you need and paste it in. It is a copy, not the original page: if someone later edits the original, the copy does not change."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"When to cherry-pick, and when not to","md":"Good uses:\n\n- **Backporting** a fix to a maintained release branch.\n- **Rescuing** a commit made on the wrong branch (cherry-pick it to the right one, then remove it from the wrong one).\n- Pulling **one useful commit** out of an abandoned branch.\n\nOveruse is a smell: if you constantly cherry-pick between two long-lived branches, the same change exists twice under different ids, which makes later merges noisy. Usually a merge, or branching from the right place in the first place, is better."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Cherry-pick traps","md":"**Picking a commit that depends on earlier ones.** The change may not apply cleanly (conflict) or may break the build. Pick the dependencies too, in order.\n\n**Picking a merge commit** needs `-m 1`, and is rarely what you want.\n\n**Duplicated commits.** The same change under two ids can confuse readers. `-x` helps."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Backport and rescue","md":"A fix landed on main that a release branch also needs, and a commit was made on the wrong branch. Use cherry-pick for both jobs."}},"rewriting/golden-rule":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The rebase that confused everyone","md":"Tariq rebased the shared `develop` branch to \"clean it up\" and force-pushed. The next morning, five teammates pulled and got strange results: duplicated commits, conflicts in code they had never touched, and one colleague's work that seemed to vanish. It took the team half a day to untangle.\n\nNothing in git broke. Tariq had rewritten commits that other people had already built on. Their repositories still referenced the old commits; his now referenced new copies with different ids. Git saw two different histories and did its best to combine them."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Private vs shared history","md":"```diagram\nrebase-vs-merge\n```\n\n**The golden rule of rebasing: never rewrite commits that exist outside your own repository and that other people may have built on.**\n\n| History is… | Safe tools | Avoid |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Private** (only on your machine, or on your own branch nobody else uses) | amend, reset, rebase, interactive rebase, squash | nothing: polish freely |\n| **Shared** (on main, develop, or any branch teammates pull) | merge, revert, new commits | amend, reset, rebase, force push |\n\n\"Pushed\" is a decent proxy for \"shared\", but the real question is: *could someone else have these commits and build on them?* Your personal feature branch on GitHub that nobody else touches can still be rebased (followed by `git push --force-with-lease`, covered in the Remotes realm)."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Editing a document after it is shared","md":"Editing your own draft before sending it is normal. But once you have emailed the document to ten people and they have started adding comments to page 4, you do not silently renumber the pages and send a \"new\" document with the same name. You send an addendum (a new commit) instead."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Merge or rebase? A practical policy","md":"Teams argue about this endlessly. A sensible default that most teams converge on:\n\n1. **Rebase your own feature branch** onto main to stay current (`git pull --rebase`, `git rebase main`). Clean, linear, no noise.\n2. **Merge (or squash-merge) the feature into main** through a pull request. Shared history only ever moves forward.\n3. **Never rebase main** or any branch others commit to.\n\n| | Merge | Rebase |\n|---|---|---|\n| History | True record, with merge commits | Linear, tidy |\n| Commit ids | Unchanged | Rewritten |\n| Conflicts | Resolved once | Possibly once per replayed commit |\n| Safe on shared branches | Yes | No |\n| Best for | Integrating finished work into shared branches | Updating and polishing private work |"},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Recovering from a rewrite","md":"If you rebased or reset something you should not have, and **nobody has pulled yet**:\n\n```term\n$ git reflog                      # find the pre-rebase position\n$ git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD      # ORIG_HEAD: where the branch was before the last rebase/reset\n```\n\nIf teammates **already have** the rewritten history, talk to them before doing anything else. Each person may need to rebase their own work onto the new history (`git rebase --onto`), which is advanced territory: prevention is far cheaper."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Golden-rule traps","md":"**\"I'll just force-push, it's fine.\"** It may be fine on your own branch. It is never fine on main.\n\n**Rebasing a branch you shared for review** while a reviewer has checked it out. Tell them, or prefer adding commits until the review ends.\n\n**Thinking merge commits are \"bad\".** They are an honest record. Linear history is a preference, not a rule."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Choose the right tool","md":"Two branches, two situations. One branch is private and messy; the other is shared. Tidy the private one with rebase, and integrate into the shared one with merge, leaving the shared history untouched."}},"rewriting/interactive-rebase":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Twelve commits called \"fix\"","md":"Before opening his pull request, Ben looked at his branch:\n\n```term\n$ git log --oneline main..feature/search\nf1e2d3c fix typo\na9b8c7d fix tests again\n4d5e6f7 fix tests\n1a2b3c4 WIP\n9z8y7x6 Add search endpoint\n```\n\nReviewers would have to wade through his trial-and-error. He opened an interactive rebase:\n\n```term\n$ git rebase -i main\n```\n\nGit opened an editor listing the five commits. He changed a few words, saved, and the branch became two clean commits: \"Add search endpoint\" and \"Add search tests\". Same final code, much clearer story."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"The todo list","md":"`git rebase -i <base>` opens a **todo list** of every commit after `<base>`, oldest first:\n\n```text\npick 9z8y7x6 Add search endpoint\npick 1a2b3c4 WIP\npick 4d5e6f7 fix tests\npick a9b8c7d fix tests again\npick f1e2d3c fix typo\n```\n\nChange the word at the start of each line, or reorder lines, then save. Git replays the list top to bottom.\n\n| Command | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| `pick` | keep the commit as is |\n| `reword` | keep the changes, edit the message |\n| `edit` | stop after applying it so you can amend (split it, change files) |\n| `squash` | meld into the previous commit and combine the messages |\n| `fixup` | meld into the previous commit and discard this message |\n| `drop` | remove the commit entirely (or delete the line) |\n\nReordering lines reorders commits. `squash` and `fixup` always join the commit **above** them.\n\n```replay\ntitle: Squash the fix-ups into one clean commit\nshow: graph\nsetup: init; commit \"Create app\"; commit \"Add search endpoint\"; commit \"WIP\"; commit \"fix tests\"\nstep: git reset --soft HEAD~3; git commit -qm \"Add search endpoint with tests\" | The result of squashing three commits into one: a single, well-described commit. (Interactive rebase does this through the todo list; a soft reset is the shortcut when the commits are the newest ones.)\n```"},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Interactive recipes","md":"```term\n$ git rebase -i HEAD~4              # edit the last four commits\n$ git rebase -i main                # edit everything on this branch since main\n$ git commit --fixup <commit>       # make a commit marked \"fixup! <message of commit>\"\n$ git rebase -i --autosquash main   # automatically move fixup! commits under their targets\n$ git rebase --continue             # after an edit stop or conflict\n$ git rebase --abort                # cancel everything\n```\n\nThe `--fixup` + `--autosquash` pair is a professional favourite: while working, record small fixes as `git commit --fixup <id>`; before review, one autosquash rebase tidies everything."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Editing a draft before publishing","md":"A writer drafts a chapter in many sittings, leaving notes like \"fix later\" and \"typo\". Before sending it to the editor, she rearranges paragraphs, merges duplicate sections, and rewrites the headings. The content is the same; the presentation is clear. Interactive rebase is that editing pass for your commits, and like a draft, you only edit what has not been published."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Clean history, reviewed faster","md":"- Reviewers read **commit by commit**. Clean, logical commits make reviews faster and more accurate.\n- Many teams \"squash and merge\" pull requests on GitHub instead, which turns the whole PR into one commit. Interactive rebase gives you finer control.\n- Keep each commit **buildable**: a reviewer (or `git bisect`) should be able to check out any commit and run the tests."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Interactive traps","md":"**Squash on the first line.** `squash` needs a previous commit to join; the first line must be `pick` (or `reword`/`edit`).\n\n**Deleting a line by accident** drops that commit. If it happens, `git rebase --abort` during the rebase or the reflog afterwards.\n\n**Rewriting pushed commits.** Same rule as always: only private history.\n\n**Getting lost in an `edit` stop.** `git status` tells you where you are; `git rebase --continue` moves on."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Tidy a messy branch","md":"A feature branch has five messy commits. Use interactive rebase to turn them into a clean, reviewable story."}},"rewriting/rebase":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Railroad tracks","md":"Lucía's team merged `main` into their feature branches every morning to stay up to date. After a month, `git log --graph` looked like a railway junction: dozens of \"Merge branch 'main' into feature/…\" commits crossing each other. Nobody could follow it.\n\nA senior engineer showed her another way to bring a branch up to date:\n\n```term\n$ git switch feature/export\n$ git rebase main\nSuccessfully rebased and updated refs/heads/feature/export.\n```\n\nInstead of adding a merge commit, rebase **replayed** her feature's commits on top of the latest `main`, as if she had started the feature today. History became a straight line again."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Replaying commits on a new base","md":"```replay\ntitle: Rebasing a feature onto the latest main\nshow: graph\nsetup: init; commit \"Create app\"; git switch -c feature/export; commit \"Add CSV export\"; commit \"Add export button\"; git switch main; commit \"Fix login bug\"; commit \"Update README\"; git switch feature/export\nstep: git rebase main | Git takes the feature's two commits, sets them aside, moves the branch to main's tip, and reapplies them one by one. They get NEW ids (look at the hashes).\nstep: git switch main | Now main can be fast-forwarded to the feature.\nstep: git merge feature/export | A fast-forward: a perfectly straight history.\n```\n\nWhat rebase does, step by step:\n\n1. Find the commits on your branch that are not on the target (`main..feature`).\n2. Temporarily set them aside.\n3. Move your branch to the target's tip.\n4. Re-apply each commit, in order, creating **new commits** with the same changes and messages but **new ids** (their parents changed).\n\n```diagram\nrebase-vs-merge\n```"},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Rebase basics","md":"```term\n$ git switch feature/export\n$ git rebase main              # replay feature's commits on top of main\n$ git rebase --continue        # after resolving a conflict in the current commit\n$ git rebase --skip            # drop the commit that conflicted\n$ git rebase --abort           # give up: everything back to how it was\n$ git pull --rebase            # fetch, then rebase your local commits on the remote ones\n```\n\n**Conflicts during rebase** happen one commit at a time. Git stops at the commit that conflicts, you resolve that file, `git add` it, and run `git rebase --continue`. Git then carries on with the next commit.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> During a rebase, `--ours` means **the branch you are rebasing onto** (e.g. main) and `--theirs` means **your commit being replayed**. It is the opposite of a merge, because rebase is building on top of the target."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Re-recording on a new backing track","md":"Imagine you recorded a guitar part over a song's first draft. The band then improves the backing track. Instead of awkwardly splicing two recordings (merge), you **re-record your part on top of the new backing track** (rebase). The result sounds as if you had played along with the final version from the start. But it is a new recording: anyone holding the old one now has something different."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"When teams rebase","md":"- **Updating your own feature branch** before opening or updating a pull request: `git rebase main` (or `git pull --rebase`).\n- **Keeping `main` linear**: some teams require \"rebase and merge\" on GitHub.\n- **Never** on commits other people are building on (the golden rule, two lessons ahead). If you rebase a branch you already pushed, you will need `git push --force-with-lease`, and you should be sure nobody else uses that branch."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Rebase traps","md":"**Rebasing shared branches.** Teammates' copies still have the old commits; things get duplicated and confusing.\n\n**Confusing `--ours`/`--theirs`** during a rebase conflict (see the warning above).\n\n**Panicking mid-rebase.** `git rebase --abort` is always there. And the reflog remembers your branch's pre-rebase position (`ORIG_HEAD` too).\n\n**Rebasing with uncommitted changes.** Git refuses. Commit or stash first."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Straighten the history","md":"Your feature branch has fallen behind main. Rebase it, handle one conflict along the way, and land the feature with a fast-forward."}},"rewriting/rewriting-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"brief","kind":"brief","title":"Ready for review","md":"**The scenario.** You have been working for three days on `feature/invoices` at *Ledgerly*, an accounting app. Your branch is private: nobody else has pulled it. Tomorrow you open a pull request and your tech lead is known for strict reviews.\n\nYour branch is a mess:\n\n- it is several commits behind `main`, and rebasing onto `main` will conflict once,\n- it contains a \"WIP\" commit, two \"fix\" commits that belong with earlier commits, and a debugging commit that must not ship,\n- one commit has a misleading message.\n\nSeparately, a security fix landed on `main` this morning and must also go to the maintained `release/1.x` branch.\n\nYour goal for `feature/invoices`: up to date with `main`, and **exactly three clean commits** with clear messages:\n\n1. `Add invoice model`\n2. `Add invoice PDF export`\n3. `Add invoice email sending`\n\n| Need | Tools |\n|---|---|\n| Catch up with main | `git rebase main`, `--continue`, `--abort` |\n| Polish commits | `git rebase -i main`: pick, reword, squash, fixup, drop, reorder |\n| Backport | `git cherry-pick -x <commit>` |\n| Safety net | `git reflog`, `ORIG_HEAD` |"},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"What Rewriting History gave you","md":"- `git rebase <base>` replays your commits on a new base: linear history, new ids.\n- `git rebase -i` edits a series of commits: reword, squash, fixup, drop, reorder, edit.\n- `git cherry-pick` copies one commit's change onto the current branch.\n- **The golden rule**: rewrite only private history. Merge and revert for shared history.\n- Conflicts during rebase come one commit at a time; `--ours`/`--theirs` are swapped compared with merge.\n\nNext realm: Remotes. Your repository meets the outside world."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Polish feature/invoices and backport the fix","md":""}},"time-travel/amend":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The forgotten file","md":"Leo committed his new search feature, looked at the result and winced:\n\n```term\n$ git commit -m \"Add serach to the product page\"\n[main 3e1f0a2] Add serach to the product page\n 1 file changed, 40 insertions(+)\n$ git status\nUntracked files:\n\tsearch.css\n```\n\nA typo in the message, and he had forgotten to add the stylesheet. He had not pushed yet, so he fixed both in one move:\n\n```term\n$ git add search.css\n$ git commit --amend -m \"Add search to the product page\"\n[main 9b77c40] Add search to the product page\n 2 files changed, 58 insertions(+)\n```\n\nThe history now shows one clean commit, as if the mistake never happened. Notice the id changed from `3e1f0a2` to `9b77c40`: amend does not edit a commit, it **replaces** it."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Amend replaces the last commit","md":"```replay\ntitle: --amend swaps the tip commit for a new one\nshow: graph\nsetup: init; commit \"Add product page\"; commit \"Add serach\"\nstep: git commit --amend -m \"Add search\" | A new commit replaces the old tip. main now points to it. The old commit is no longer on any branch (it fades out), though the reflog still remembers it.\n```\n\n`git commit --amend` takes the **current staging area** and the parent of the last commit, and creates a new commit from them. So it can:\n\n- change the message (`-m \"new\"` or edit it in the editor),\n- add forgotten changes (stage them first),\n- remove a file from the last commit (`git rm --cached file`, then amend),\n- keep the message unchanged (`--no-edit`).\n\nThe old commit is not destroyed immediately. It just stops being part of the branch."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Amend recipes","md":"```term\n$ git commit --amend -m \"Better message\"   # fix only the message\n$ git add forgotten.css\n$ git commit --amend --no-edit             # add a file, keep the message\n$ git commit --amend --author=\"Leo Park <leo@example.com>\"   # fix the author\n$ git commit --amend                       # open the editor with the old message\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Reprinting the last page","md":"Amend is like reprinting the last page of a document before you have handed it in. The new page replaces the old one in the binder. But if you already gave a copy to a colleague, they hold the old page, and now the two binders disagree."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"The one rule","md":"**Amend only commits you have not pushed.** Once a commit is on a shared branch, teammates may have built on it. Replacing it means your history and theirs no longer match: your next push is rejected, and if you force it, you overwrite their view of history.\n\nOn your **own** feature branch, before anyone else uses it, amending is common and fine (you will then need `git push --force-with-lease`, covered in the Remotes realm)."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Amend traps","md":"**Amending a pushed commit on `main`.** Don't. Make a new commit instead.\n\n**Accidentally amending.** You meant a new commit but typed `--amend`: the previous commit's content is merged into the new one. The reflog (later in this realm) can get the old commit back.\n\n**Expecting amend to touch older commits.** It only replaces the very last commit. For older ones you need interactive rebase (Rewriting History realm)."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Fix it before anyone sees","md":"You just made a commit with a typo in the message and a forgotten file, and then another small slip. Repair the last commit with amend, without creating extra commits."}},"time-travel/detached-head":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"Did it work in March?\"","md":"A customer reported that the PDF export of invoices had been broken \"for a while.\" Hannah wanted to know whether it had ever worked. Instead of guessing, she went back in time:\n\n```term\n$ git log --oneline --until=\"2026-03-31\" -1\n7d3e9b2 Add invoice PDF export\n$ git switch --detach 7d3e9b2\nHEAD is now at 7d3e9b2 Add invoice PDF export\n```\n\nHer working directory now looked exactly like the project did that day. She ran the export: it worked. So the bug arrived later, which narrowed the search enormously. Then she returned to the present:\n\n```term\n$ git switch main\n```\n\nMoving to an old commit like this puts you in **detached HEAD** state. It sounds alarming. It is simply git's name for \"looking at a commit that is not the tip of a branch.\""},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Where HEAD points","md":"```diagram\ndetached-head\n```\n\nNormally HEAD points at a **branch**, and the branch points at a commit. When you commit, the branch moves forward and HEAD comes along.\n\nWhen you check out a commit directly, HEAD points straight at **that commit**: it is *detached* from any branch. Everything still works: you can look, build, run tests, even commit. But new commits made here belong to no branch. Once you switch away, nothing points at them, and they will eventually be garbage-collected.\n\n```replay\ntitle: Detaching HEAD and coming back\nshow: graph\nsetup: init; commit \"Add invoices\"; commit \"Add PDF export\"; commit \"Redesign header\"; commit \"Add dark mode\"\nstep: git switch --detach HEAD~2 | HEAD now points directly at \"Add PDF export\". main has not moved.\nstep: commit \"Experiment: bigger font\" | A commit made here hangs off the old commit. No branch points at it.\nstep: git switch -c font-experiment | Creating a branch here gives the experiment a name, so it is safe.\nstep: git switch main | Back to the present. The experiment branch keeps its commit.\n```"},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Visiting and returning","md":"```term\n$ git switch --detach <commit>      # visit a commit (modern)\n$ git checkout <commit>             # visit a commit (classic; prints the detached HEAD notice)\n$ git checkout v1.2.0               # visit a release tag\n$ git switch -                      # go back to where you were\n$ git switch main                   # return to a branch\n$ git switch -c rescue-branch       # keep commits made while detached\n```\n\nYou cannot switch while you have uncommitted changes that would be overwritten; git stops you with \"Your local changes would be overwritten by checkout.\" Commit or stash first."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A museum visit","md":"Detached HEAD is a museum visit to a past version of your project. You can walk around and look at everything. If you start building something in the museum, though, it is not part of any exhibition: when you leave, the cleaning staff will eventually clear it away. If you want to keep what you built, put a label on it (create a branch) before you leave."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"When developers travel back","md":"- **\"Did this ever work?\"**: check out an old release tag and test.\n- **Reproducing a customer's bug** on the exact version they run: `git checkout v2.3.1`.\n- **Comparing behaviour** before and after a change.\n- **Bisecting** (Power Tools realm) automates this: git detaches HEAD on commit after commit for you."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Detached HEAD traps","md":"**Committing while detached and then leaving.** Your commits are orphaned. If it happens, don't panic: `git reflog` (later in this realm) shows their ids, and `git branch rescue <id>` saves them.\n\n**Confusing `git checkout <file>` and `git checkout <commit>`.** The old `checkout` command does both jobs. `git switch` (branches/commits) and `git restore` (files) split them up, which is why modern docs prefer them.\n\n**Forgetting where you are.** Your prompt or `git status` says `HEAD detached at 7d3e9b2`. Check before you start real work."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Visit the past","md":"An invoicing app's history has a release tag and several later commits. Travel back, look around, make an experimental commit, save it with a branch, and return."}},"time-travel/history-detective":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Who changed the shipping fee?","md":"On Monday the support inbox of an online bookshop filled with complaints: shipping to Canada had jumped from $8 to $80. Somebody had added a zero. Nobody admitted it, nobody remembered it, and the change could have been made any time in the last two weeks among 140 commits.\n\nMei, the newest developer, found it in under two minutes:\n\n```term\n$ git log -S \"80\" --oneline -- shipping/rates.json\n4c1d0a7 Update rates for 2026\n$ git show 4c1d0a7 --stat\n shipping/rates.json | 2 +-\n$ git log -1 --format=\"%an, %ar\" 4c1d0a7\nJoel Kim, 6 days ago\n```\n\n`git log -S` finds commits that **added or removed** a piece of text. Combined with a path filter and `git show`, it pointed straight at the commit, its author and its date. Joel fixed the typo in a new commit five minutes later, and Mei earned a reputation as the person who can find anything.\n\nHistory is only valuable if you can search it. This lesson turns `git log` from a wall of text into a search engine."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Naming commits","md":"Before you can travel through history you need ways to **name** points in it:\n\n| Name | Means |\n|---|---|\n| `a1b2c3d` | A commit by (short) id. 7 characters are usually unique. |\n| `HEAD` | The commit you are on now |\n| `main`, `feature/x` | The commit a branch points at |\n| `HEAD~1`, `HEAD~3` | 1 or 3 generations back, following first parents |\n| `HEAD^` | The first parent (same as `HEAD~1`) |\n| `HEAD^2` | The **second** parent (only merge commits have one) |\n| `v1.0` | A tag |\n| `main..feature` | Commits on feature that are not on main |\n\n```replay\ntitle: HEAD~n walks back through parents\nshow: graph\nsetup: init; commit \"One\"; commit \"Two\"; commit \"Three\"; commit \"Four\"\nstep: git log --oneline -1 HEAD | HEAD is the newest commit, \"Four\".\nstep: git log --oneline -1 HEAD~1 | HEAD~1 is its parent, \"Three\".\nstep: git log --oneline -1 HEAD~3 | HEAD~3 goes three generations back, to \"One\".\n```"},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Searching history","md":"```term\n$ git log --oneline -n 10                 # last ten commits\n$ git log --since=\"2 weeks ago\" --until=\"yesterday\"\n$ git log --author=\"Joel\"                 # by author (a regex: partial names work)\n$ git log --grep=\"shipping\"               # by words in the message\n$ git log -S \"freeShipping\"               # commits that added/removed this text (\"pickaxe\")\n$ git log -- shipping/rates.json          # only commits touching this path\n$ git log --stat                          # files changed per commit\n$ git log -p -2                           # full diffs of the last two commits\n$ git log main..feature                   # what feature has that main does not\n$ git log --format=\"%h %an %ar %s\"        # your own format\n```\n\nFormat placeholders worth knowing: `%h` short id, `%H` full id, `%an` author name, `%ar` relative date, `%ad` date, `%s` subject (first line).\n\nFilters combine. `git log --author=Joel --since=\"1 month ago\" -- shipping/` reads like a sentence."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A detective's toolkit","md":"A detective rarely reads every file in the archive. They filter: *who* was there, *when*, *what* was touched, *which words* appear in the statement. `git log` filters work the same way:\n\n- `--author` is \"who was there\"\n- `--since` / `--until` is \"when\"\n- `-- path` is \"what was touched\"\n- `--grep` searches the statements (messages)\n- `-S` searches the evidence itself (the code)"},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"History on a real team","md":"- **Incident reviews** start with \"what changed recently?\": `git log --since=\"2 days ago\" --oneline`.\n- **Onboarding**: reading `git log --oneline -- src/payments` shows how a module evolved and who knows it best.\n- **Release notes**: `git log v1.3.0..v1.4.0 --oneline` lists everything shipped between two releases.\n- **Code review**: `git log main..my-branch` shows exactly what your pull request contains.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Create an alias for the view you use most: `git config --global alias.lg \"log --oneline --graph --all\"`."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Log pitfalls","md":"**Forgetting `--` before a path that looks like a branch name.** `git log -- main.py` is unambiguous; use `--` when in doubt.\n\n**`--grep` searches messages, not code.** To search code changes, use `-S`.\n\n**`--author` matches name or email**, partial and case-sensitive by default. `--author=joel` might not match \"Joel\".\n\n**`HEAD~2` vs `HEAD^2`.** The first means \"grandparent\"; the second means \"second parent of a merge\". Mixing them up gives confusing results on merge commits."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Find the shipping-fee culprit","md":"The bookshop repository has a few weeks of history from several people. Use log filters to answer the questions. The **Commit graph** panel helps you check your answers."}},"time-travel/reflog":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The branch that vanished","md":"Diego deleted what he thought was an old branch:\n\n```term\n$ git branch -D payment-retries\nDeleted branch payment-retries (was 5e8a1c3).\n```\n\nIt was not old. It held two days of unpushed work. His stomach dropped. A senior colleague leaned over:\n\n```term\n$ git reflog\na10d2e4 HEAD@{0}: checkout: moving from payment-retries to main\n5e8a1c3 HEAD@{1}: commit: Retry failed payments with backoff\n...\n$ git branch payment-retries 5e8a1c3\n```\n\nThe branch was back, commits and all. Git had never deleted the commits, only the label pointing at them. And git keeps a private diary of every position HEAD has been at: the **reflog**."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Commits are hard to lose","md":"Git almost never deletes commits immediately. When you reset, amend, rebase or delete a branch, the commits are simply no longer *reachable* from a branch. They stay in the object database for at least 30 days (by default) before garbage collection may remove them.\n\nThe reflog records every move of HEAD (and of each branch) **on your machine**:\n\n```term\n$ git reflog\n9fba052 (HEAD -> main) HEAD@{0}: reset: moving to HEAD~2\ne90e551 HEAD@{1}: commit: Add invoice totals\n3c4a1b0 HEAD@{2}: commit: Add invoice table\n98969a4 HEAD@{3}: checkout: moving from feature to main\n```\n\n`HEAD@{1}` means \"where HEAD was one move ago\". You can use it anywhere you would use a commit id: `git reset --hard HEAD@{1}` undoes the reset you just did.\n\n```replay\ntitle: Recovering from reset --hard\nshow: graph\nsetup: init; commit \"Add invoices\"; commit \"Add invoice table\"; commit \"Add invoice totals\"\nstep: git reset --hard HEAD~2 | Oops. main jumped back two commits. The lost commits are drawn faded: unreachable, but still stored.\nstep: git reflog | The reflog lists every position HEAD has had, including before the reset.\nstep: git reset --hard HEAD@{1} | Jump back to where HEAD was one move ago. Everything is restored.\n```"},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Recovery recipes","md":"```term\n$ git reflog                          # every move of HEAD, newest first\n$ git reflog show main                # moves of one branch\n$ git reset --hard HEAD@{1}           # undo the last reset / amend / rebase\n$ git branch rescued <id>             # give a lost commit a branch again\n$ git switch -c rescued <id>          # same, and switch to it\n$ git cherry-pick <id>                # copy one lost commit onto the current branch\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"The building's CCTV","md":"Branches are like name tags on desks. Remove a name tag and the desk (the commit) is still there; you just cannot find it by name. The reflog is the building's CCTV footage: it shows every desk you visited, in order, with timestamps. Find the moment in the footage, walk back to that desk, and put a name tag on it again."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"What the reflog can and cannot do","md":"- It is **local only**. It is not pushed and does not exist on GitHub or on a teammate's machine.\n- It records **commits**, so it cannot recover uncommitted edits destroyed by `reset --hard` or `restore`. Commit early, even as \"WIP\", and squash later.\n- Entries expire (90 days for reachable, 30 for unreachable by default).\n- It is the reason senior developers seem fearless with git: they know that anything **committed** is almost always recoverable."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Reflog misunderstandings","md":"**\"I'll recover it from GitHub's reflog.\"** There isn't one you can use. Recover on the machine where the work happened.\n\n**Searching the wrong reflog.** `git reflog` is HEAD's log. If you are looking for a branch's old position, `git reflog show <branch>` can be clearer.\n\n**Using `HEAD@{n}` after more moves.** Each new move shifts the numbers. Use the commit id from the reflog line to be safe."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Undo the undo","md":"A careless afternoon: a hard reset too far and a deleted branch. Use the reflog to recover both, without losing anything."}},"time-travel/reset":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Three commits too many","md":"Aisha spent the morning on a new checkout flow and made three commits. At lunch her team lead said the product team had cancelled the feature. The commits were never pushed.\n\nOption one: delete the code by hand and make a fourth commit. But the history would then contain three commits for a feature that never existed, plus an undo.\n\nOption two:\n\n```term\n$ git reset --hard HEAD~3\nHEAD is now at 4ab9e10 Fix footer links\n```\n\nThe branch jumped back three commits and her files matched that commit again. Her unpushed history was as if the morning never happened.\n\n`git reset` moves the current branch to another commit. Its three modes decide what happens to your staging area and your files along the way. Understanding them is the difference between a precise undo and losing a day of work."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What each mode resets","md":"```diagram\nreset-modes\n```\n\n`git reset <commit>` always **moves the current branch** (and HEAD) to `<commit>`. Then:\n\n| Mode | Staging area | Working directory | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `--soft` | untouched | untouched | Undo commits but keep everything **staged**: re-commit differently, squash several commits into one |\n| `--mixed` (default) | reset to match `<commit>` | untouched | Undo commits and **unstage**: keep the edits as normal changes |\n| `--hard` | reset | **reset**: files overwritten | Throw commits **and** uncommitted edits away |\n\n```replay\ntitle: Soft, mixed and hard on the same history\nshow: graph,areas\nsetup: init; commit \"Add cart\"; commit \"Add checkout step 1\"; commit \"Add checkout step 2\"\nstep: git reset --soft HEAD~1 | --soft: main moves back one commit. The undone change is still staged.\nstep: git reset HEAD~1 | --mixed (the default): main moves back another commit and the staging area is reset. Both changes are now unstaged edits in the working directory.\nstep: git reset --hard HEAD | --hard: the staging area and working directory are reset to HEAD. The edits are gone.\n```\n\nWithout a commit, `git reset` defaults to `HEAD`, so `git reset` alone just unstages everything, and `git reset file.txt` unstages one file (like `git restore --staged`)."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Reset recipes","md":"```term\n$ git reset --soft HEAD~1      # undo the last commit, keep its changes staged\n$ git reset HEAD~1             # undo the last commit, keep its changes unstaged\n$ git reset --hard HEAD~1      # undo the last commit and delete its changes\n$ git reset --hard             # discard ALL uncommitted changes (back to HEAD)\n$ git reset --soft HEAD~3      # squash: then commit once to replace three commits\n$ git reset --hard origin/main # make your branch identical to the remote one\n$ git reset app.js             # unstage one file\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Rewinding a tape","md":"Imagine recording music on a multi-track tape:\n\n- `--soft` rewinds the tape counter but leaves the recorded sound sitting in the mixing desk, ready to be re-recorded.\n- `--mixed` rewinds and clears the mixing desk, but the musicians still have their parts written on their sheet music.\n- `--hard` rewinds, clears the desk **and** tears up the sheet music."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Reset on a team","md":"Reset rewrites **your branch's history**. The rules match amend:\n\n- Reset freely on **local, unpushed** commits.\n- On a branch others have pulled, use `git revert` (next lesson) instead.\n- `git reset --hard origin/main` is a common \"make my local main match the server exactly\" command, e.g. after a messed-up experiment on main.\n\n> [!CAUTION]\n> `git reset --hard` deletes uncommitted work with no confirmation and no recycle bin. Committed work can usually be recovered with the reflog; uncommitted edits cannot. Run `git status` first."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Reset traps","md":"**`--hard` with uncommitted work you wanted.** Gone. Stash first if unsure.\n\n**Resetting a pushed branch.** Your next push is rejected (non-fast-forward). Forcing it rewrites history for everyone.\n\n**Confusing reset and revert.** Reset moves the branch backwards (history disappears). Revert moves forwards by adding an undo commit (history kept).\n\n**Forgetting the reflog exists.** \"I reset too far!\" is fixable: `git reflog`, find the old id, `git reset --hard <id>`."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Undo at three strengths","md":"A shopping-cart project has some commits you want to undo in different ways. Watch the **Three areas** and **Commit graph** panels after each reset: the graph keeps showing undone commits faded, because the reflog still remembers them."}},"time-travel/revert":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Friday's bug, Monday's fix","md":"A bad commit reached `main` on Friday and was deployed. Since then, four teammates have pulled `main` and two more commits have been merged on top. Rewriting history with reset now would pull the floor out from under everyone.\n\nInstead, the team lead ran:\n\n```term\n$ git revert 9fba052\n[main 6546696] Revert \"Change checkout button handler\"\n 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)\n```\n\n`git revert` creates a **new** commit that applies the exact opposite of the bad one. Nothing is erased: history now honestly says \"this was added, and later undone.\" Everyone simply pulls the new commit."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Undo by adding","md":"```replay\ntitle: Revert adds an inverse commit\nshow: graph\nsetup: init; commit \"Add login\"; commit \"Change button colour\"; commit \"Add reviews\"\nstep: git revert --no-edit HEAD~1 | A new commit undoes \"Change button colour\". The commits after it are untouched and history only moves forward.\n```\n\n| | `git reset` | `git revert` |\n|---|---|---|\n| Direction | Moves the branch **back** | Adds a commit **forward** |\n| History | Rewritten (commits drop off) | Preserved, plus an undo commit |\n| Safe on shared branches? | No | Yes |\n| Can undo an old commit in the middle? | Not without also dropping everything after it | Yes, just that one |\n\nIf later commits changed the same lines, revert can produce a **conflict**, just like a merge. You resolve it, then run `git revert --continue`. Conflicts are covered in the Merging realm."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Revert recipes","md":"```term\n$ git revert <commit>              # undo one commit (opens the editor for the message)\n$ git revert --no-edit <commit>    # use the default \"Revert …\" message\n$ git revert HEAD                  # undo the latest commit\n$ git revert A..B                  # undo a range of commits (one revert commit each)\n$ git revert --no-commit <commit>  # apply the inverse but let me commit it myself\n$ git revert --abort | --continue  # control a revert that hit a conflict\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Correcting a published newspaper","md":"A newspaper never un-prints yesterday's edition. If an article had an error, today's edition carries a correction: \"Yesterday we reported X; in fact Y.\" Readers of both editions end up with the truth, and the record stays honest.\n\nReset is pulping yesterday's print run. Fine if nobody has bought a copy yet; a scandal if they have."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Revert in practice","md":"- **Production incident**: the fastest safe fix is often \"revert the commit that caused it, deploy, then investigate calmly.\"\n- On GitHub, merged pull requests have a **Revert** button that opens a new PR containing the revert commit.\n- Reverting a **merge commit** needs `-m 1` to tell git which parent is the mainline. You will see it again in the Merging realm."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Revert traps","md":"**Reverting the wrong commit.** Check `git show <id>` first.\n\n**Reverting a revert.** Undoing a revert brings the original change back. It works, but leave a clear message.\n\n**Expecting revert to remove sensitive data.** The bad commit is still in history. For leaked secrets, rotate the secret."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Undo a bad commit in shared history","md":"A team's `main` branch contains a bad commit somewhere in the middle, with good work after it. Undo just that commit without rewriting history."}},"time-travel/time-travel-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"brief","kind":"brief","title":"Bad Friday","md":"**The scenario.** It is Friday afternoon at *Pinecone*, a small company that sells a recipe app. You inherited a messy local repository from a colleague who left in a hurry. Before the weekend, you must leave it in a clean, correct state.\n\nHere is what you know:\n\n- A commit on `main` from last week introduced a bug (it set the servings calculation to divide by zero). Other people's work came after it, and `main` has already been pushed and pulled by the whole team.\n- Your colleague's last local commit (not pushed) has a typo in its message and is missing a file that is sitting untracked in the folder.\n- Your colleague deleted a branch called `ingredient-search` that held unpushed work the product manager now wants.\n- There is also a throwaway \"debug\" commit on top that must disappear, but it was never pushed.\n\nPlan before you act: which undo is right for **shared** history, and which for **local** history?\n\n| Situation | Tool |\n|---|---|\n| Undo a pushed commit | `git revert` |\n| Fix the last unpushed commit | `git commit --amend` |\n| Drop unpushed commits | `git reset` |\n| Recover deleted or \"lost\" work | `git reflog`, `git branch <name> <id>` |\n| Find the bad commit | `git log -S`, `git log --grep`, `git show` |"},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"What Time Travel gave you","md":"- Name any commit: ids, `HEAD~n`, `HEAD^2`, branches, tags, `HEAD@{n}`.\n- Search history with `git log` filters: `--author`, `--since`, `--grep`, `-S`, `-- path`.\n- Visit the past with `git switch --detach` / `git checkout <commit>`; save experiments with `git switch -c`.\n- `git commit --amend` replaces the last commit.\n- `git reset --soft | --mixed | --hard` moves the branch back with increasing force.\n- `git revert` undoes safely by adding an inverse commit.\n- `git reflog` remembers every position: committed work is almost never truly lost.\n\n**The rule that ties it together:** rewrite only history that is still private to you. Once it is shared, add new commits instead."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Leave the repo clean before the weekend","md":""}},"workflows/commit-conventions":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Release notes that write themselves","md":"Every two weeks, someone on Yuki's team spent half a day writing release notes by reading through 120 commit messages. Then the team adopted **Conventional Commits**:\n\n```term\n$ git log --oneline v1.4.0..HEAD\ne3a1b2c feat(search): filter results by author\n9d8c7b6 fix(cart): keep quantities when the page reloads\n4f5e6d7 docs: explain the new search filters\n1a2b3c4 feat!: drop support for API v1\n```\n\nA tool now reads the prefixes and generates the release notes and the next version number automatically: one `feat!` (breaking change) means a new major version. What used to take half a day takes one command."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Conventional Commits","md":"The format:\n\n```text\n<type>[optional scope][!]: <description>\n\n[optional body]\n\n[optional footer(s), e.g. \"BREAKING CHANGE: …\" or \"Refs #123\"]\n```\n\n| Type | Use for |\n|---|---|\n| `feat` | a new feature (→ minor version) |\n| `fix` | a bug fix (→ patch version) |\n| `docs` | documentation only |\n| `refactor` | code change that neither fixes a bug nor adds a feature |\n| `test` | adding or fixing tests |\n| `chore` | tooling, dependencies, housekeeping |\n| `perf` | performance improvement |\n| `ci`, `build`, `style` | pipelines, build system, formatting |\n\nA `!` after the type/scope, or a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer, marks a breaking change (→ major version)."},{"id":"model-2","kind":"model","title":"Atomic commits","md":"Independent of the message format, good commits are **atomic**:\n\n- **one logical change** per commit,\n- the project **builds and tests pass** at every commit,\n- **reviewable on its own**,\n- **revertable on its own** without breaking unrelated features.\n\nAtomic commits make `git revert`, `git bisect` and code review work the way they are meant to."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Writing them","md":"```term\n$ git commit -m \"feat(search): filter results by author\"\n$ git commit -m \"fix(cart): keep quantities when the page reloads\" -m \"Quantities were stored in memory only. Fixes #88\"\n$ git commit -m \"feat!: drop support for API v1\" -m \"BREAKING CHANGE: clients must call /v2 endpoints.\"\n$ git log --oneline --grep=\"^feat\"        # list features\n```\n\nTeams often enforce the format with a `commit-msg` hook (Power Tools realm) or a CI check on PR titles."},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Filing with labelled folders","md":"An office where every document goes into a folder labelled *Invoices*, *Contracts* or *Letters* can answer \"show me all contracts from March\" in seconds. Conventional Commits label every change so machines and people can find, group and count them."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Making conventions stick","md":"- Agree on the types in `CONTRIBUTING.md`.\n- With **squash merging**, only the PR title becomes the commit on main, so enforce the convention on **PR titles**.\n- Tools such as release-please or semantic-release generate changelogs and version bumps from these messages.\n- Do not let the format replace a good description: `fix: bug` is still a bad message."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Convention traps","md":"**Type soup.** \"feat: fix typo and refactor cart and add tests\" is several commits.\n\n**Forgetting breaking changes.** Missing a `!` means users get a breaking change in a minor version.\n\n**Over-scoping.** Scopes are optional; use them when they help."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Write a conventional history","md":"Make a series of atomic commits on a small project, each with a correct Conventional Commit message, including one breaking change."}},"workflows/git-flow-trunk":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Three teams, three workflows","md":"At one conference, three engineers compared notes:\n\n- **Sofia** builds a mobile banking app. Releases go through app-store review and customers run several versions at once. Her team uses **git flow**: a `develop` branch, release branches, hotfix branches, and tags for every version.\n- **Arjun** works on a large web platform with hundreds of engineers. They use **trunk-based development**: everyone merges tiny changes into `main` at least daily, with unfinished work hidden behind feature flags.\n- **Mia** is in a ten-person web team using **GitHub flow**.\n\nEach workflow was right for its context. The skill is knowing the trade-offs."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Git flow","md":"```diagram\ngit-flow\n```\n\n| Branch | Lives | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| `main` | forever | released code only; every merge is tagged (v1.2.0) |\n| `develop` | forever | integration of finished features |\n| `feature/*` | days–weeks | branch from develop, merge back to develop |\n| `release/*` | days | from develop; only fixes; merges into main **and** develop |\n| `hotfix/*` | hours | from main; urgent fix; merges into main **and** develop |\n\nStrength: clear, versioned releases and support for maintenance. Cost: many branches, many merges, slower integration."},{"id":"model-2","kind":"model","title":"Trunk-based development","md":"```diagram\ntrunk-based\n```\n\n- One trunk (`main`). Branches, if any, live for hours.\n- Everyone integrates at least daily, so merges stay tiny.\n- **Feature flags** hide incomplete work in production.\n- Requires excellent automated tests and fast CI.\n- Releases are cut from the trunk (sometimes with short release branches)."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Git flow in practice","md":"```term\n$ git switch -c feature/statements develop\n$ ...commits...\n$ git switch develop && git merge --no-ff feature/statements\n$ git switch -c release/2.1 develop        # stabilise\n$ git switch main && git merge --no-ff release/2.1 && git tag -a v2.1.0 -m \"Release 2.1.0\"\n$ git switch develop && git merge --no-ff release/2.1\n$ git switch -c hotfix/2.1.1 main          # urgent fix on production\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Choosing a road network","md":"Git flow is a city with ring roads, bypasses and service lanes: excellent for complex traffic, but more junctions. Trunk-based development is a single, very well-maintained motorway that everyone merges onto constantly, which only works if everyone drives carefully (tests) and there are emergency lanes (flags). GitHub flow is a small town's main street with short side roads."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Choosing","md":"| Situation | Good fit |\n|---|---|\n| Web app, deploys continuously, small/medium team | GitHub flow |\n| Very large team, strong CI culture, continuous deploy | Trunk-based |\n| Versioned products, several versions supported, scheduled releases | Git flow (or a lighter release-branch model) |\n\nWhatever you choose: write it down in `CONTRIBUTING.md`, protect the important branches, and automate the checks."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Workflow traps","md":"**Adopting git flow by default** for a web app that deploys daily: lots of ceremony, little benefit.\n\n**\"Trunk-based\" without tests or flags**: broken production.\n\n**Forgetting to merge release/hotfix branches back into develop** in git flow: the fix disappears in the next release."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Ship a release and a hotfix, git-flow style","md":"A product team uses git flow. Finish a feature into develop, cut a release, tag it, and then handle an urgent hotfix so that both main and develop get it."}},"workflows/github-flow":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"Deploying twenty times a day","md":"A small payments startup shipped once a month. Every release was a stressful weekend: dozens of features, merged together at the last minute, conflicts everywhere, and nobody sure which change broke what.\n\nThey switched to a simple rule set called **GitHub flow**: `main` is always deployable; every change is a short-lived branch; every branch becomes a pull request; merged PRs deploy immediately. Releases went from monthly to twenty times a day, and each one was so small that problems were obvious and easy to revert."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"One rule and a loop","md":"```diagram\ngithub-flow\n```\n\n**The rule:** anything in `main` is deployable.\n\n**The loop**, for every change, however small:\n\n1. Create a descriptively named branch from `main`.\n2. Commit and push early; open a (draft) pull request to discuss.\n3. Review, automated checks (CI), maybe deploy the branch to a preview environment.\n4. Merge into `main`.\n5. Deploy `main` (often automatically).\n\nNo `develop` branch, no release branches, no long-lived anything. The workflow you practised in the GitHub realm *is* GitHub flow."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"The loop in commands","md":"```term\n$ git switch main && git pull                  # start from the latest main\n$ git switch -c feature/saved-cards\n$ git commit -am \"Store cards with the payment provider token\"\n$ git push -u origin feature/saved-cards\n$ gh pr create --fill --draft                  # early feedback\n$ gh pr ready                                  # mark ready for review\n$ gh pr merge --squash --delete-branch         # after approval and green checks\n$ git switch main && git pull                  # sync, start the next task\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"A kitchen that plates as it goes","md":"A restaurant kitchen does not cook every order for the night and serve them all at 10 pm. Each dish is prepared on its own station and goes out as soon as it is ready and checked by the head chef. The pass (main) only ever holds finished dishes. That is GitHub flow."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"When it fits, and what it needs","md":"GitHub flow fits teams that **deploy continuously**: web apps, APIs, internal tools. It works because of supporting practices:\n\n- **Automated tests** on every PR (required checks).\n- **Small PRs** that review quickly.\n- **Feature flags** to merge unfinished features safely (code is deployed but switched off).\n- **Fast rollback**: revert a PR or redeploy the previous commit.\n\nIt fits less well when you must support several released versions at once (mobile apps in app stores, installed software). Those teams add release branches, as in the next lesson."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"GitHub-flow traps","md":"**Long-lived feature branches.** They quietly turn GitHub flow back into merge hell. Aim for days, not weeks.\n\n**Merging without CI.** \"Main is always deployable\" is only true if something checks it.\n\n**Big-bang PRs.** Split work behind feature flags."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Run the loop twice","md":"Ship two small changes to a team web app through the GitHub flow loop, starting from an up-to-date main each time."}},"workflows/protected-branches":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"The Friday push to main","md":"At a fast-growing startup, an engineer pushed a \"quick fix\" straight to `main` on a Friday evening, skipping review and tests. It took the checkout down for two hours.\n\nOn Monday the team turned on **branch protection** for `main`: no direct pushes, every change through a pull request, at least one approving review, and passing CI checks before merge. The same mistake became impossible."},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"What protection rules can require","md":"On GitHub (Settings → Branches / Rulesets), a protected branch can require:\n\n| Rule | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| Require a pull request before merging | direct pushes are rejected |\n| Required approvals (e.g. 1 or 2) | merge blocked until enough reviewers approve |\n| Dismiss stale approvals | a new push resets approvals |\n| Require review from Code Owners | the owners of the touched files must approve |\n| Require status checks to pass | CI must be green |\n| Require branches to be up to date | PR must include the latest main |\n| Require linear history | no merge commits (squash/rebase only) |\n| Block force pushes and deletions | history on main cannot be rewritten |\n\n**CODEOWNERS** is a file (`.github/CODEOWNERS`) mapping paths to responsible people or teams:\n\n```text\n# path                 owners\n*                      @acme/core\n/payments/             @acme/payments-team\n/docs/                 @maria\n*.css                  @acme/design\n```\n\nWhen a PR touches `/payments/`, the payments team is automatically requested for review, and (with the rule enabled) must approve."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Living with protection","md":"```term\n$ git push origin main\nremote: error: GH006: Protected branch update failed for refs/heads/main.\nremote: error: Changes must be made through a pull request.\n ! [remote rejected] main -> main (protected branch hook declined)\n\n$ git switch -c fix/tax-rounding        # the right way\n$ git push -u origin fix/tax-rounding\n$ gh pr create --fill\n$ gh pr view                            # shows what is still blocking the merge\n$ gh pr checks                          # CI status\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Two signatures on a cheque","md":"Companies require two signatures on large cheques. Not because the first signer is dishonest, but because a second look catches mistakes and makes fraud harder. Branch protection is the two-signature rule for your production code."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Sensible defaults","md":"- Protect `main` (and release branches): PR required, 1 approval, required CI checks, no force pushes.\n- Add `CODEOWNERS` for sensitive areas (payments, auth, infrastructure).\n- Keep admins subject to the rules too, except in documented emergencies.\n- Review your rules when the team grows."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Protection traps","md":"**Self-approval workarounds.** Rules count approvals from *other* people with write access.\n\n**Required checks that never run** (renamed workflow): every PR gets stuck. Keep the rule's check names in sync with your workflows.\n\n**Protecting everything.** Feature branches should stay flexible; protect the shared ones."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Work within the rules","md":"The team's `main` is protected and has CODEOWNERS. Try the forbidden path once, then add a CODEOWNERS entry through a proper pull request that gets approved."}},"workflows/tags-releases":{"lenses":[{"id":"story","kind":"story","title":"\"Which version is the customer on?\"","md":"A customer reported a bug in \"the version from last month\". Developer Leila asked: which commit was that? Nobody knew. The deploy log said \"deployed main\" twelve times last month.\n\nThe fix was **tags**: a permanent, human-readable name on the exact commit that was released.\n\n```term\n$ git tag -a v2.3.0 -m \"Release 2.3.0\"\n$ git push origin v2.3.0\n$ git checkout v2.3.0      # months later: exactly what the customer runs\n```"},{"id":"model","kind":"model","title":"Tags and semantic versioning","md":"A **tag** is a ref that, unlike a branch, **never moves**.\n\n| | Lightweight tag | Annotated tag |\n|---|---|---|\n| Create | `git tag v1.0.0` | `git tag -a v1.0.0 -m \"Release 1.0.0\"` |\n| Stores | just a pointer | a tag object: tagger, date, message (can be signed) |\n| Use for | private bookmarks | releases |\n\n```diagram\nsemver\n```\n\n**Semantic versioning** (`MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`):\n\n- `PATCH` (2.4.**1**): bug fixes, nothing else changes.\n- `MINOR` (2.**5**.0): new features, backwards compatible.\n- `MAJOR` (**3**.0.0): breaking changes.\n- Pre-releases: `3.0.0-beta.1`.\n\nA **GitHub release** is a tag plus release notes (and optional downloadable files), shown on the repository's Releases page."},{"id":"commands","kind":"commands","title":"Tagging and releasing","md":"```term\n$ git tag                              # list\n$ git tag -l \"v2.*\"                    # filter\n$ git tag -a v2.4.0 -m \"Release 2.4.0\" # annotated, at HEAD\n$ git tag -a v2.3.1 -m \"Hotfix\" a1b2c3d   # tag an older commit\n$ git show v2.4.0                      # tag details + commit\n$ git push origin v2.4.0               # tags are NOT pushed by plain git push\n$ git push --tags                      # push all tags\n$ git describe --tags                  # \"v2.4.0-3-g9e8f7b6\": 3 commits after v2.4.0\n$ git tag -d v2.4.0-oops               # delete locally\n$ git push origin --delete v2.4.0-oops # delete on the remote\n$ gh release create v2.4.0 --generate-notes   # GitHub release with notes from merged PRs\n```"},{"id":"analogy","kind":"analogy","title":"Edition numbers on a book","md":"A book's text changes between printings, but \"Second edition, 2024\" always refers to exactly the same pages. A tag is the edition number for your code. Semantic versioning tells readers what kind of change the new edition contains before they buy it."},{"id":"team","kind":"team","title":"Release habits","md":"- **Tag every release**, annotated, from the commit that was actually deployed.\n- **Never move a published tag.** If v2.4.0 was wrong, release v2.4.1.\n- **Automate**: a release workflow can build, test and publish whenever a `v*` tag is pushed (GitHub Automation realm).\n- **Changelog**: keep `CHANGELOG.md` or use generated release notes."},{"id":"traps","kind":"traps","title":"Tag traps","md":"**Forgetting to push tags.** `git push` alone does not send them.\n\n**Lightweight tags for releases.** They lack tagger and date; use `-a`.\n\n**Re-tagging.** Deleting and recreating a pushed tag confuses everyone who already fetched it."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Cut a release","md":"Tag the current release properly, push it, publish a GitHub release, then ship a patch release after a bug fix."}},"workflows/workflows-boss":{"lenses":[{"id":"brief","kind":"brief","title":"Release day at Harbour","md":"**The scenario.** *Harbour* is a shipping-logistics app. The team uses a light release model: `main` is protected (PR + 1 approval), features merge through PRs, releases are tagged with semantic versions, and commits follow Conventional Commits.\n\nToday you are the release captain:\n\n1. Two feature PRs are waiting. Review state is fine; your job is to ship your own small feature too: add a `feat:` commit on a branch, open a PR, get it approved, and squash-merge it with a conventional PR title.\n2. Cut release **v1.3.0**: annotated tag on the merged `main`, pushed, and published as a GitHub release.\n3. A bug is reported in v1.3.0 right after release. Fix it with a `fix:` commit through a PR and release **v1.3.1**.\n4. Leave your local `main` up to date with GitHub.\n\n| Need | Tools |\n|---|---|\n| Ship through PRs | `git switch -c`, `git push -u`, `gh pr create --title \"feat: …\"`, `gh pr merge --squash` |\n| Tag and release | `git tag -a vX.Y.Z -m`, `git push origin vX.Y.Z`, `gh release create` |\n| Versioning | feat → minor, fix → patch, `!` → major |"},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"What Team Workflows gave you","md":"- **GitHub flow**: main always deployable, short branches, PRs, deploy on merge.\n- **Git flow**: develop + release + hotfix branches for versioned products. **Trunk-based**: tiny, frequent merges to one trunk with feature flags.\n- **Conventional Commits** (`feat:`, `fix:`, `feat!:`) and **atomic commits** make history searchable and automate releases.\n- **Tags** mark releases (`git tag -a`, `git push origin <tag>`), numbered with **semantic versioning**; GitHub releases add notes.\n- **Branch protection** and **CODEOWNERS** enforce review and checks on the branches that matter."}],"lab":{"id":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Ship v1.3.0 and v1.3.1","md":""}}};
